// Quiz pool messages — 3 sentences per artist:
//   1. Whereabouts (where based, training, geographic locus)
//   2. Experience / relativity (what shaped the practice — lineage, biography, historical moment)
//   3. Relevance + artist statement (why the work matters now, the thesis)
// Used by the embedded quiz on artist_map.html.

const QUIZ_MESSAGES = {

  // ── Painting ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  a146: { // Lucas Arruda
    message: "Arruda lives and works in São Paulo, where he was trained at the Fundação Armando Álvares Penteado in a Brazilian painterly tradition shaped by both modernist abstraction and Romantic landscape. His small-format Deserto-Modelo paintings are built from memory rather than observation, distilling sky and horizon into atmospheric thresholds that hover between geography and inner state. He treats landscape as a verb — an event of perception — and insists that what counts is not what is depicted but the time the painting holds inside it."
  },

  a176: { // Victor Man
    message: "Romanian-born and based between Cluj and Berlin, Man emerged from the Cluj School alongside Adrian Ghenie and Mircea Cantor in the early 2000s. His paintings draw on Northern Renaissance portraiture, esoteric symbolism, and Eastern Orthodox iconography, filtered through a deliberate slowness that resists art-world tempo. He uses figuration as a way to hold ambiguity in place — bodies and objects in his work are vessels for hermetic, art-historical, and erotic readings that never fully resolve."
  },

  a188: { // Yu Nishimura
    message: "Based in Kanagawa, Japan, Nishimura paints in a register pulled equally from anime backgrounds and Édouard Vuillard's domestic interiors. Working with thin glazes and visible weave, he renders figures as if seen through a soft veil — half-formed, half-receding — refusing the hard outline contemporary painting habitually demands. His message is one of attention without grasping: a quiet plea for the looseness and porosity that figurative painting has largely traded away for legibility."
  },

  a206: { // Amy Sillman
    message: "Sillman lives in Brooklyn and Berlin and trained at SVA before becoming a central figure in the post-postmodern revival of gestural painting in New York from the late 1990s. Her practice braids drawing, painting, and writing — she edits the zine The O-G and writes extensively on color and process — and her paintings are full of revision and self-argument. The work insists that abstraction can carry feeling and politics at once: that a yellow next to a green can hold an uncertainty about gender, mood, or the very question of whether to keep painting."
  },

  a203: { // Dana Schutz
    message: "Schutz lives in Brooklyn and trained at CalArts and Columbia; she emerged in the early 2000s with the Frank from Observation series, in which she painted the world's last man. Her practice operates by invented constraint — imagined subjects, scenarios that could never have been witnessed — making narrative painting a way of testing what the form can still believe in. After Open Casket (2016) and the controversy that followed, her work has continued to ask whether painting can host group catastrophe, panic, and gallows comedy without flattening them into kitsch."
  },

  a221: { // Lisa Yuskavage
    message: "Yuskavage lives in New York and trained at Tyler and Yale, where she studied alongside John Currin and Sean Landers. Her pin-up-derived nudes mobilize the language of soft-core figuration, kitsch, and 19th-century landscape pastoral to court vulgarity head-on rather than ironize it. The message is that the eroticized female body can be a serious site for color theory, light, and shame — that taste itself, including the viewer's discomfort, belongs inside the frame of the painting."
  },

  a313: { // Marina Perez Simão
    message: "Perez Simão lives and works in São Paulo and came to painting after curatorial work, including with the Cildo Meireles estate. Her paintings flatten Brazilian modernist landscape — Tarsila, Volpi, Burle Marx — into rippling colour-field bands that move between geometry and sensation. She frames her work as a contemporary continuation of antropofagia: digesting European abstraction through a tropical formal vocabulary that is neither nostalgic nor postcolonial-corrective but unapologetically pleasurable."
  },

  a980: { // Wilhelm Sasnal
    message: "Sasnal is based in Tarnów and Kraków, Poland, and was a founding member of the Ładnie Group in the late 1990s, which broke from Polish historical painting toward pop and the everyday. He paints from photographs — family snapshots, news images, film stills, Holocaust documentation — translating each into a flat, deskilled mark that interrogates what a photograph can no longer do. The work argues that painting in post-1989 Eastern Europe is a way of slowing down history: each image becomes a small act of refusing to scroll past."
  },

  a291: { // Mao Yan
    message: "Mao Yan lives and teaches in Nanjing, where he is one of the central figures of the post-Cultural Revolution generation of Chinese realist painters. His portraits — typically of a single sitter, Thomas, his German friend, painted over many years — strip down representation to a pale, near-monochrome surface in which the face barely emerges. He treats portraiture as a slow exposure of presence, refusing both Socialist Realism's heroic legibility and the Cynical Realism boom of the 1990s."
  },

  a929: { // Matthew Wong
    message: "Wong was born in Toronto, lived between Canada and Hong Kong, and was self-taught — he turned to painting in his late 20s after photography and writing, learning publicly through Facebook conversations with John Yau, Peter Doig, and others. His landscapes braid Van Gogh, Shitao, and Bonnard into solitary, dreamlike interiors and forests rendered in dotted, jewel-toned facture. After his death in 2019 at thirty-five, the work has become a touchstone for the question of whether figurative painting can still be made from solitude rather than from market demand."
  },

  a996: { // Co Westerik
    message: "Westerik lived and worked in The Hague for nearly all of his life and trained at the Royal Academy of Art there in the immediate post-war period. His small, hyper-detailed paintings of bodies, hands, and intimate encounters owe nothing to the Cobra moment that surrounded him, opting instead for a deeply slow, psychologically charged realism. The work asks for a contemplation of skin, contact, and the weight of small gestures at a scale and speed contemporary painting has largely abandoned."
  },

  a356: { // Etel Adnan
    message: "Adnan was born in Beirut to a Greek mother and a Syrian Ottoman father, lived through the Lebanese Civil War in exile in Paris and Sausalito, and was a poet before she was a painter. Her small landscapes — most famously of Mount Tamalpais, seen from her California kitchen — are built from straight palette-knife strokes laid down quickly and without correction. She insisted that painting was where she could be silent, that color was a way of saying what her Arabic, French, and English could not — and the late market discovery of her work, after age eighty, did not change that."
  },

  // ── Sculpture ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  a149: { // Huma Bhabha
    message: "Bhabha was born in Karachi and is based in Poughkeepsie, New York; she trained at RISD and Columbia and built her early sculptures from junkyard finds while working as an assistant to artists in Manhattan. Her totemic figures — assembled from cork, styrofoam, clay, and burnt wood — invoke Gandharan Buddha statuary, sci-fi prophecy, and post-conflict ruin in a single body. The work insists that figuration can carry catastrophe without illustrating it: each piece is a witness left standing after something we are not shown."
  },

  a57: { // Carol Bove
    message: "Bove lives and works in Red Hook, Brooklyn, and emerged in the early 2000s with sculptural arrangements that drew explicitly on the 1960s — Playboy magazines, peacock feathers, Donald Judd. Her current 'collage sculpture' bends thick steel I-beams into floppy ribbon-like forms that read as both monumental modernist sculpture and the parody of it. The work argues that the postwar formal vocabulary is still good for something, but only if you treat it as malleable rather than canonical."
  },

  a213: { // Andra Ursuța
    message: "Ursuța grew up in Salonta, Romania, under Ceaușescu and moved to New York in the 1990s; she now lives and works in Brooklyn. Her recent Vandal Lust sculptures are cast in lead crystal and glass from forms she shapes around her own body, producing translucent ghosts that read as armor, fetish, and devotional object at once. She uses sculpture as a way to embody Eastern European post-Communist abjection without explaining it — the body becomes a site of contaminated glamour."
  },

  a501: { // Adrián Villar Rojas
    message: "Villar Rojas was born in Rosario, Argentina, and works site-specifically across a nomadic, team-based studio practice that has installed at the Met rooftop, Athens, the Geffen, and the Istanbul Biennial. His monumental unfired-clay sculptures — including a full-scale Michelangelo's David reduced to debris — are made to crack and decay across the run of the exhibition. He treats geology and extinction as raw material, asking what a sculpture is when its medium is the future viewer's absence."
  },

  a794: { // Berlinde De Bruyckere
    message: "De Bruyckere lives and works in Ghent, raised in a Catholic boarding school whose imagery of suffering bodies haunts her practice. She casts wax over wax to make horse hides, human limbs, and tree forms that look both freshly dead and slowly transforming, often shown under blankets or inside vitrines. Her work proposes the wounded body as a contemporary site of the sacred — neither religious nor secular, but stubbornly material."
  },

  a458: { // Cristina Iglesias
    message: "Iglesias lives in Madrid and trained as a ceramicist before studying sculpture in London under Tony Cragg; she emerged in the late 1980s as part of a new Spanish sculptural generation. Her work — vegetal screens of bronze, suspended pavilions, and water installations carved into public ground — places the viewer inside architecture that is also landscape. She frames her practice as a feminist re-routing of public sculpture: rather than the standing male monument, an enterable, ambiguous space."
  },

  a963: { // Tau Lewis
    message: "Lewis is Black Canadian, born in Toronto and based in Brooklyn, and is self-taught — she began making work after the 2016 election from textile scraps gathered in her family's home. Her quilted masks and figures stitch together leather, fur, sequins, and found cloth into beings she describes as ancestors, healers, or scouts from a Black aquatic future. The work proposes diaspora as a form of repair: that craft, intuition, and material salvage can compose a worldview the gallery system did not author."
  },

  a803: { // Eva Jospin
    message: "Jospin lives in Paris and trained at the Beaux-Arts there in the early 2000s; she works almost exclusively in cardboard, layering and cutting it to build dense forest interiors and grotto walls. Her source material is the romantic European garden — Versailles, the picturesque ruin, the cabinet of curiosities — recast in a humble, recyclable material. She argues that the forest, as a Western imaginative space, is still available for sculpture if you are willing to make it out of trash."
  },

  a853: { // Sanford Biggers
    message: "Biggers lives in New York and studied in Atlanta, Philadelphia, and Japan, where his interest in Buddhism and the African diaspora collided. He paints, sews, and overlays on antique American quilts — many possibly used as signals on the Underground Railroad — embedding African masks, mandalas, and bullet holes into the cloth. The work insists that American material history is already a Black history, and that the role of the artist is to make it readable again."
  },

  a384: { // Klára Hosnedlová
    message: "Hosnedlová was born in Uherské Hradiště and lives in Berlin, trained at UMPRUM in Prague in a Czech tradition of disciplined craft labour. She works in silk-thread embroidery, building tonal gradations stitch by surgical stitch, often staged inside immersive sand-and-glass environments. Her practice argues that the slow technical knowledge of textile labour — Eastern European, gendered, undervalued — is a contemporary medium, not a heritage."
  },

  // ── Photography ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

  a257: { // Hai Bo
    message: "Hai Bo lives in Beijing, was born in Changchun in the year of the Cultural Revolution, and trained as a painter before moving to photography in the 1990s. His best-known series re-stages old family group portraits with the same sitters decades later — and with the missing absent — exposing the temporal violence of late-twentieth-century China without comment. He uses photography as a slow elegy rather than a record, treating the medium's claim on the real as an act of mourning."
  },

  a273: { // Josef Koudelka
    message: "Koudelka was born in Moravia in 1938, photographed the 1968 Soviet invasion of Prague clandestinely, and lived as a stateless exile in Europe through the Magnum cooperative for the next four decades. His Gypsies and Exiles series document Roma communities, war landscapes, and the European post-war condition in dense, off-balance black-and-white compositions. He treats documentary photography as a form of geological time: each frame holds dispossession at landscape scale."
  },

  a296: { // Richard Misrach
    message: "Misrach lives in Berkeley and has spent more than four decades photographing the American desert in large-format colour, from Death Valley to the Salton Sea to the petrochemical corridor of Louisiana's Cancer Alley. His Desert Cantos cycle is built as a long-form essay: bombing ranges, dead animals, burning palms, all rendered in painterly colour at sublime scale. The work argues that environmental destruction in the United States is not a future warning but a long-running record, and that beauty is one of the few tools left to make people look at it."
  },

  a79: { // Roni Horn
    message: "Horn lives in New York and has spent forty years travelling to Iceland, where she made the long-running Library of Water archive in a former library in Stykkishólmur. Her practice moves between photography (the Cabinet of, Bird, and You Are the Weather series), cast-glass sculpture, drawing, and written text on identity and androgyny. She insists that identity — of a self, a place, a piece of glass — is unstable, mirrored, never closed, and that the role of art is to keep that instability visible rather than resolve it."
  },

  // ── Video / Film / Performance ──────────────────────────────────

  a396: { // Christian Marclay
    message: "Marclay was born in San Rafael, California, raised in Geneva, and trained in Boston; he began in the 1980s as one of the first turntablist musicians, treating LPs as sculptural material. His twenty-four-hour montage The Clock (2010) splices thousands of film clips that show or mention the current real-world time, so the work and the viewer's watch run together. The piece argues that cinema's century-long fragmentation of time is now the medium of everyday attention — and that editing, as a practice, is the contemporary form of composition."
  },

  a446: { // Tacita Dean
    message: "Dean was born in Canterbury, trained at the Slade, and lives in Berlin and Los Angeles; she has campaigned publicly to keep 16mm and 35mm film stock in production. Her films — Disappearance at Sea, FILM (Tate Turbine Hall), and the Bubble House — let the camera sit on landscape, weather, and absence at a duration the medium can barely sustain. She frames analog film not as nostalgia but as a different cognitive technology: a way of attending to time that digital capture has efficiently erased."
  },

  a649: { // Wael Shawky
    message: "Shawky was born in Alexandria, lives between Alexandria and Philadelphia, and founded MASS Alexandria as an independent space for young Egyptian artists. His Cabaret Crusades trilogy is a feature-length narration of the Crusades performed by 200-year-old Murano-glass marionettes, drawn from Arab historian Amin Maalouf's source material. Shawky's argument is that the historical record is itself a performance — that the way the West narrates its own history depends entirely on which body, and whose hand, is doing the speaking."
  },

  a490: { // Tino Sehgal
    message: "Sehgal was born in London, raised in Düsseldorf and Paris, studied political economy and dance, and lives in Berlin. His 'constructed situations' — choreographed encounters in museums between trained interpreters and visitors — exist only in spoken contracts; no documentation, photographs, or installation views are permitted. He argues that the contemporary economy's main product is the immaterial — speech, encounter, attention — and that art's task is to take that production seriously as form."
  },

  a583: { // Ryan Trecartin
    message: "Trecartin was born in Texas, trained at RISD with Lizzie Fitch (his ongoing collaborator), and lives in Athens, Ohio, where the pair built a rural compound for production. His feature-length videos — I-Be Area, Re'Search Wait'S, Comma Boat — are hyperkinetic family-and-friends performances in heavy digital makeup, scripted as if a teenage internet swallowed itself. Trecartin treats the post-2007 social-web subject as a real subject for art, and his videos remain one of the few honest attempts to render what online attention does to identity."
  },

  // ── Installation / Conceptual ───────────────────────────────────

  a414: { // Doris Salcedo
    message: "Salcedo lives in Bogotá and has, since the 1980s, built a practice entirely from the testimony of victims of Colombia's long civil violence — paramilitary, FARC, and state. Her sculptures embed household furniture, women's clothing, and human hair in concrete; her interventions — like Shibboleth, a 167-metre crack in the Tate Modern floor — are public acts of mourning at architectural scale. The work argues that contemporary sculpture's first responsibility is to the named, specific, unburied dead; abstraction is allowed only on those terms."
  },

  a795: { // Leandro Erlich
    message: "Erlich lives in Buenos Aires and is the son of an architect; his early career was built around precisely engineered perceptual illusions — a swimming pool seen from below by viewers walking through it, an elevator shaft that drops into infinity. His work has migrated from a Surrealist-aligned 1990s vocabulary into civic-scale public sculpture (Maison Fond, the Pulled by the Roots house). He argues that the contemporary urban subject is now so habituated to the perceptual interface that a moment of disorientation is a political event, however brief."
  },

  a841: { // Nari Ward
    message: "Ward was born in St Andrew, Jamaica, moved to Brooklyn as a child, and trained at City College and Brooklyn College in the early 1990s. He builds installations from the materials of the post-industrial Black diaspora — shopping carts, fire hoses, baby strollers, copper nails — found in his Harlem neighborhood. His message, articulated through works like Amazing Grace and We the People, is that the readymade is not Duchamp's joke: it is the assembled archive of who and what a city throws away."
  },

  a20: { // Theaster Gates
    message: "Gates was raised in Chicago by a roofer father and trained as an urban planner and a ceramicist in Iowa, Cape Town, and Tokoname. His Rebuild Foundation has bought, restored, and reactivated abandoned buildings across Chicago's South Side, housing collections (the Johnson Publishing archive, Frankie Knuckles's records) inside ceramic-and-tar architectural propositions. He argues that the Black artist's most consequential medium in the 21st century is real estate — the literal, undertaxed, racialized ground itself — and that ceramics teaches you how to handle that ground."
  },

  a311: { // Adam Pendleton
    message: "Pendleton is based in New York and developed his concept of 'Black Dada' in the mid-2000s, riffing on Amiri Baraka's poem and Dada's anti-syntax. His silkscreen paintings, video portraits (of Lorraine O'Grady, David Hammons, Adrienne Kennedy), and book-objects assemble a Black avant-garde lineage that did not previously sit together. He argues that contemporary art's formalist languages are not race-neutral, and that grammar — typographic, painterly, archival — is itself a place where Black political life can take place."
  },

  // ── Drawing / Mixed ─────────────────────────────────────────────

  a159: { // Marcel Dzama
    message: "Dzama was born in Winnipeg, studied at the University of Manitoba, and was a founding member of the Royal Art Lodge collective; he now lives in Brooklyn. His drawings — root beer-tinted from his early Winnipeg practice — populate a folk-cinematic world of bear-soldiers, ballerinas, witches, and chess armies, drawn equally from Dadaist puppet theatre and prairie folk art. The work argues that contemporary drawing can carry a coherent fictional cosmology — not as illustration of a story, but as the story's primary mode of existence."
  },

  a191: { // Raymond Pettibon
    message: "Pettibon was born Raymond Ginn in Tucson, raised in Hermosa Beach, and is the younger brother of Black Flag's Greg Ginn; he made flyers, album covers, and zines for SST and the Minutemen across the 1980s. His ink-and-text drawings braid Henry James, William Blake, surf, baseball, and dropouts in a flat punk graphic style that was for a long time unsellable. He argues that text is a visual medium, and that the proper site of American literary thinking is not the novel but the wall."
  },

  a735: { // Sophie Calle
    message: "Calle was born in Paris, lives there still, and began her practice in the late 1970s by following strangers in the street and photographing them — a working method she has used continually since. Her project-as-life works (Suite Vénitienne, The Hotel, Take Care of Yourself, Exquisite Pain) braid autobiography, surveillance, and contractual game-playing with the people in her own romantic and family life. The work argues that the self is a public artifact, and that the rules under which we agree to be observed are themselves the medium."
  },

  a83: { // Glenn Ligon
    message: "Ligon was born and raised in the Bronx, trained at Wesleyan and the Whitney Independent Study Program, and lives in New York. His text-based painting practice — Stranger in the Village, Untitled (I Am a Man), Warm Broad Glow — borrows phrases from James Baldwin, Zora Neale Hurston, Richard Pryor, and Civil Rights placards and lets them degrade into illegibility as the surface accumulates. He argues that race is not legible by sight but by language, and that the painting can stage exactly the moment where the language stops working."
  },

  // ── Batch 2 additions ──

  a596: { // Allora & Calzadilla
    message: "Jennifer Allora (b. 1974, Philadelphia) and Guillermo Calzadilla (b. 1971, Havana) have worked as a single artist since 1995 and live in San Juan, Puerto Rico, the island whose colonial and military history their practice has tracked since their first projects on the US Navy's Vieques bombing range. Their works pull a tuba through Bach for the Venice Biennale, drag a stuffed military hippopotamus across an opera stage, and stage piano recitals in which the instrument is the floor. Their argument is that geopolitics enters art most precisely as sound and choreography — the body of a singer, a soldier, an animal — not as illustration or slogan."
  },

  a381: { // Mona Hatoum
    message: "Hatoum was born in Beirut to a Palestinian family already in exile and was stranded in London by the outbreak of the Lebanese Civil War in 1975; she still lives in London and Berlin. Her practice moves between household objects scaled to weaponry (a cheese grater the size of a sliding door), surveillance-and-body video (Corps étranger sends an endoscope through her own body), and rubber-and-metal installations of barbed wire as domestic furniture. She insists that the domestic and the geopolitical share the same materials — that exile is not a metaphor but a measurable property of a kitchen object."
  },

  a584: { // Rosemarie Trockel
    message: "Trockel lives in Cologne and emerged in the early 1980s from the male-dominated German painting scene with knitted-wool 'paintings' bearing Playboy logos, hammer-and-sickles, and corporate marks woven by industrial machine. She has since produced ceramics, video, schematic 'book drafts,' and stovetop assemblages that refuse to settle into a single signature style. The argument across forty years is that femininity, taste, and category are themselves the material — that an artist who refuses to be legible is doing political labour, not branding it."
  },

  a533: { // Cyprien Gaillard
    message: "Gaillard was born in Paris in 1980, lives between Paris and Berlin, and works in landscape photography, sculpture, and 35mm and 3D video. His best-known films — Cities of Gold and Mirrors (Cancun), Nightlife (Cleveland), Ocean II Ocean (Athens to Bangkok) — track the decay of modernist architecture, ancient monuments, and post-industrial ruins as a single continuous geological event. He argues that contemporary destruction is too slow to be an event and too fast to be ruin — and that the artist's job is to find the camera angle at which time becomes visible."
  },

  a821: { // Hans Op de Beeck
    message: "Op de Beeck lives in Brussels and works in monochrome — every sculpture, drawing, film, and life-size environment he makes is rendered in a single dust-grey palette so that material distinctions collapse into mood. His Sanctuary and The Collector's House installations are full-scale grey rooms inhabited by grey figures eating grey food, photographed by visitors in colour. He treats grey as the contemporary condition's true colour: the screen-flattened, decision-suspended, mass-anesthetised tone in which Western private life now takes place."
  },

  a545: { // Karen Kilimnik
    message: "Kilimnik lives in Philadelphia, trained at Temple, and emerged in the 1990s with small fast paintings of teenage pop idols (Kate Moss, Leonardo DiCaprio, ballet stars) rendered in the loose hand of an obsessive fan. Her installations stage Marie-Antoinette boudoirs, ballet stage sets, and witch-girl bedrooms as if a 13-year-old had been given gallery walls. The argument — refined over thirty years — is that fan culture, romance, and femininity are serious painterly subjects, and that the polished MFA hand is one historical option among many."
  },

  a364: { // Mirosław Bałka
    message: "Bałka lives in Otwock outside Warsaw, the small town where he was born in 1958 and where he has used his family home and the local railway as the dimensional and material basis for his sculpture for forty years. His works — How It Is (Tate Turbine Hall 2009, a 30-metre dark steel box visitors walk into), salt-and-steel floors, soap-and-felt geometries — return obsessively to the Polish Jewish absence in the post-war landscape. He insists on sculpture's responsibility to specific historical sites; the dimensions of his works are often his own body's, or his childhood house's, measured in centimetres."
  },

  a282: { // Maya Lin
    message: "Lin is American-born to Chinese parents who arrived after the 1949 revolution; she designed the Vietnam Veterans Memorial in Washington at age 21 as a Yale undergraduate, and has since divided her practice between architecture and sculpture. Her sculptural work — Wave Field, Storm King's Wavefield, Pin River — translates topography and hydrography into earthworks and pin-and-glass installations. She argues that landscape is the still-uncatalogued American memorial subject — and that the artist's job is to map what is being lost while it is still here to be mapped."
  },

  a567: { // Pamela Rosenkranz
    message: "Rosenkranz lives in Zürich and Switzerland, and trained at Bern and the Università IUAV di Venezia in philosophy and visual culture. Her practice mobilises industrial colour, perfume, biotech materials, and engineered environments — pools of synthetic skin tone, scented LED-lit interiors, AI-generated dog avatars — to test where the human ends and the chemical environment begins. She argues that the body of the contemporary subject is already a corporate object, and that sculpture's task is to make this legible at the level of pigment, scent, and serotonin."
  },

  a371: { // Cerith Wyn Evans
    message: "Wyn Evans was born in Llanelli, Wales, in 1958, lives in London, and began his career as a film-maker working with Derek Jarman in the 1980s before turning to sculpture, neon, and text in the 1990s. His best-known works are large flown neon armatures of handwritten phrases, chandeliers that flicker Morse-code transcripts of literary texts, and quotations rerouted through optical glass. He treats light as a medium with a grammar — capable of speaking, citing, and refusing to be read — and argues that the work of art is a sentence the viewer has to finish."
  },

  // ── Batch 3 (cycle 1 of automation) ──

  a270: { // Acaye Kerunen
    message: "Kerunen lives between Kampala and New York and trained as an actor and curator before her woven sculpture practice; she co-represented Uganda at the 2022 Venice Biennale alongside Collin Sekajugo. Her work is hand-woven from raffia, banana fibre, palm leaf, and other indigenous Ugandan materials that she sources directly from rural women's craft cooperatives whose labour she names in the wall text. She insists that contemporary sculpture is a labour relation — that the work is unfinished without the visible economy of who made it and from what."
  },
  a981: { // Agnes Scherer
    message: "Scherer lives in Berlin and Salzburg and trained at HBK Braunschweig; she works at the unstable seam between visual art, opera, and stage design. Her immersive installations — The Salesman (2019), Cupid and the Animals (2021) — paint sets, write libretti, build puppets, and stage live performances inside the same gallery walls. She treats the exhibition as a coherent theatrical event rather than a display of discrete objects, and argues that contemporary art's atomised media categories obscure the much older form to which all the parts already belong."
  },
  a815: { // Ahmed Mater
    message: "Mater was born in Tabuk, trained as a physician at King Khalid University, and works between Abu Arish, Mecca, and London; he co-founded Edge of Arabia in 2003 with Stephen Stapleton. His photography and video project Desert of Pharan (2011–2016) documents the rapid redevelopment of Mecca for the Hajj at industrial scale, and his magnet-and-iron-filings Magnetism (2012) restages the Kaaba as a field experiment. He argues that the most consequential transformation of Islamic sacred space in a century is not a religious event but a construction one, and that the artist's job is to keep that visible."
  },
  a784: { // Alejandro Campins
    message: "Campins was born in Manzanillo, lives in Havana, and trained at the Instituto Superior de Arte; he emerged from a strong Cuban painting tradition shaped by both Eastern European Socialist Realism and Caribbean light. His large canvases — Patria (2014), Lethargy (2013) — depict abandoned modernist architecture, rural ruin, and revolutionary monuments slowly being reabsorbed by tropical landscape. The argument is that the Cuban Revolution's built environment is now a sublime subject for painting in exactly the romantic sense Constable would have recognised — heroic, weathered, ungovernable."
  },
  a315: { // Alejandro Piñeiro Bello
    message: "Piñeiro Bello was born in Pinar del Río, Cuba, in 1990 and lives in Miami; he is self-taught and openly cites Wifredo Lam and José Bedia as primary lineage. His large canvases — Tierra de Promisión (2022), Bahia Honda (2023) — render Caribbean landscape, Afro-Cuban Santería iconography, and mythological hybridity in dense layered colour. He argues that Cuban-American painting need not narrate exile as a closed story — that the Caribbean imaginary is still being painted, by people who left, into the present."
  },
  a946: { // Alex Da Corte
    message: "Da Corte was born in Camden, NJ to Venezuelan-Italian parents, trained at Yale and the University of the Arts, and lives in Philadelphia. His video installations — Rubber Pencil Devil (2018), the Met rooftop commission As Long as the Sun Lasts (2021) — assemble pop culture, midcentury advertising, Wizard of Oz iconography, and stage-set sculpture into hyper-saturated, candy-coloured emotional environments. He argues that the language of American consumer surrealism is its own folk vernacular, and that taking it seriously as form is not the same as celebrating it."
  },
  a837: { // Ana Maria Tavares
    message: "Tavares lives in São Paulo and trained at the city's Escola de Comunicações e Artes; she has worked in the long shadow of Brazilian modernist architecture since the 1980s. Her sculptural installations — Atlântico (2007), Desvios (2010) — re-stage the materials of airport interiors, hotel lobbies, and Lúcio Costa transit corridors as mirror, steel, and water environments inside the museum. She treats the seductive surfaces of modernist transit space as the contemporary Brazilian sublime — political, beautiful, and ambivalent at once."
  },
  a591: { // Andrea Zittel
    message: "Zittel lives in the high desert outside Joshua Tree, California, and trained at RISD; she founded the A-Z Enterprise in 1991 as the brand name for her own life-as-art. Her A-Z Living Units, Escape Vehicles, Wagon Stations, and Smockshop clothing are tested by her, by friends, and by paying visitors — and her annual High Desert Test Sites brings the practice into landscape and community. She argues that the structures of daily life — what you wear, what you sleep in, how you eat — are art's first and most consequential material."
  },
  a809: { // André Komatsu
    message: "Komatsu lives and works in São Paulo, where he trained at FAAP; his Japanese-Brazilian lineage and the city's brutalist architecture are equally present in his practice. His sculptural installations — Estado Provisório (2012), Reverso (2015) — disassemble construction debris, reinforcing bar, and salvaged drywall into precariously balanced compositions that re-stage the means of urban building. He argues that Latin American architectural power is most legible in its rubble — and that sculpture is one of the few mediums that can read it without flattering it."
  },
  a471: { // Annette Messager
    message: "Messager lives in Paris and Malakoff and was a central figure of the post-1968 French feminist art scene from the 1970s onward. Her installations — Mes voeux (1989), the Venice Golden Lion-winning Casino (2005), Articulés-Désarticulés — assemble stuffed animals, photo fragments, hand-written texts, coloured pencils, and net-sewn body parts into theatres of unsettled girlhood and grief. She treats the categories of woman, child, witch, and collector as ungovernable bodies that the gallery cannot organise, and argues that the unfinished private archive is a feminist sculptural form."
  },
  a904: { // Ariana Papademetropoulos
    message: "Papademetropoulos lives in Los Angeles, trained at CalArts, and is the daughter of a Greek-American family with roots in the city's visual-effects industry. Her photorealist canvases — The Emerald Tablet (2022) — paint decaying 1970s domestic interiors that bloom mid-frame into mushrooms, portals, and fairytale apparitions, all rendered with the smoothness of a film still. The argument is that the suburban American interior is already a haunted, hallucinogenic space — that painting need only stop pretending otherwise."
  },
  a839: { // Armando Testa
    message: "Testa was born in Turin in 1917, founded Studio Armando Testa in 1946, and worked across advertising, graphic design, and animation until his death in 1992. His campaigns for Punt e Mes, Pirelli, Lavazza, and Papalla collapsed Surrealist visual logic into mass commercial communication and shaped the postwar Italian visual environment as completely as any single designer. The estate-managed argument is that the line between graphic design and fine art was a postwar accident — and that the most legible Italian visual modernism of the 20th century is on billboards, not on canvas."
  },
  a927: { // Austyn Weiner
    message: "Weiner lives in Miami and trained at Tulane and Parsons; she emerged in the late 2010s as part of a generation of women painters working at the unstable seam between abstraction and confessional figuration. Her canvases — Just Above My Head (2022) — layer fast, vibrating colour, scribbled text, and figurative passages into surfaces that read at autobiographical pitch. The work argues that the diaristic intensity of post-internet female interiority is a serious painterly subject, and that legibility need not be sacrificed to it."
  },
  a781: { // Barbana Bojadzi
    message: "Bojadzi was born in 1990 and is among the youngest artists on the Galleria Continua roster; her practice moves between painting and small-scale installation. Her early work, including untitled 2020 paintings, engages memory, place, and Balkan domestic ritual in muted, layered surfaces. The argument is that landscape and family interior remain available to painting as long as the artist is willing to slow down — that what the contemporary attention economy cannot reward, painting can still hold."
  },
  a295: { // Beatriz Milhazes
    message: "Milhazes lives and works in Rio de Janeiro, where she still uses the same Lapa studio she opened in the early 1990s after studying at Parque Lage. Her layered, decal-transferred paintings — Maresias (2002), Succulent Eggplants (1996) — collapse Brazilian Baroque ornament, modernist colour theory, Carnaval, and tropical pattern into surfaces of extraordinary chromatic density. She argues that decoration, ornament, and the so-called feminine surface are not lesser than abstraction — they are abstraction's most under-read tradition."
  },
  a890: { // Bertrand Lavier
    message: "Lavier lives in Paris and Aignay-le-Duc, trained as a horticulturist, and emerged in the late 1970s with sculptural assemblages that overpaint, stack, or weld together pairs of brand-name domestic appliances. His Brandt/Haffner and Walt Disney Productions series interrogate authorship, the readymade, and the legal status of corporate logos. The argument extends Duchamp: that the readymade is not a one-time gesture but a still-operative grammar — and that the categories of art and commodity are governed by trademark law more than by aesthetic decision."
  },
  a979: { // Borna Sammak
    message: "Sammak lives in New York and trained at Tyler School of Art; his practice braids video, painting, and digital collage in a register that treats commercial software the way an earlier generation treated oil paint. Works like Not Yet Titled (2014) and Just Beachy (2018) mine stock-footage libraries, screensavers, and Adobe artefacts for visual excess that registers as both junk and luxury. The argument is that contemporary commercial imagery is the only widely shared visual language left — and that painting's task is to take its glitches seriously."
  },
  a460: { // Brennan Gerard & Ryan Kelly
    message: "Gerard and Kelly are an American collaborative duo who met at the Whitney Independent Study Program and have worked together since 2003; both trained as dancers before crossing into video and installation. Their projects — Timelining (2014), Modern Living (2016), Lazarus (2022) — stage live choreographies of queer intimacy inside the houses of midcentury modernism (Schindler, Eames), where private domestic ritual collides with surveillance and the politics of looking. The argument is that the modernist house was always a stage; the duo simply restores the bodies that the documentary photograph cropped out."
  },
  a911: { // Brian Rochefort
    message: "Rochefort lives in Los Angeles and trained at the Rhode Island School of Design; his vessels and sculptures sit between the lineage of Peter Voulkos and a younger generation of ceramicists working with deliberate ruin. His Crater series builds the vessel up through dozens of glaze firings until the surface bubbles, encrusts, and slumps into something that reads as a geological event rather than a pot. The argument is that ceramic, the oldest sculptural medium, is also the only one that visibly records every accident of its own making."
  },
  a845: { // Carla Accardi
    message: "Accardi was born in Trapani, Sicily in 1924, lived and worked in Rome, and co-founded the Forma 1 group in 1947 alongside Pietro Consagra and Antonio Sanfilippo. Her Sicofoil paintings (1965 onward) replaced canvas with transparent industrial plastic, allowing painted sign-and-symbol marks to float in literal space rather than on opaque ground. The estate-managed argument is that Italian abstraction's most radical move was material, not stylistic — Accardi made painting transparent before the Americans declared painting dead."
  },
  a792: { // Carlos Cruz-Diez
    message: "Cruz-Diez was born in Caracas in 1923, lived in Paris from 1960 until his death in 2019, and is, with Soto and Otero, one of the three central Venezuelan kinetic artists. His Physichromies (1959–) and Chromosaturations (1965 onward) make colour autonomous from form — fields of light visible only when the viewer moves, with no inherent image to memorise. The estate argues that colour is not a property of objects but an event in time, and that Latin American modernism's contribution to perception is still under-credited in the Northern art-historical record."
  },
  a617: { // Carmen Herrera
    message: "Herrera was born in Havana in 1915, moved to New York in 1939, and worked as an unknown painter for fifty years before her first solo show at age 89 in 2004; she died in Manhattan in 2022. Her hard-edge geometric abstractions — Blanco y Verde (1959), Iberic (1949) — anticipate Ellsworth Kelly and the New York minimalist generation that ignored her for a half-century on grounds of gender and Cuban exile. The estate-managed argument is that the canon of geometric abstraction is wrong in its dates — the work was always there, the institutions chose not to look."
  },
  a27: { // Carsten Höller
    message: "Höller lives in Stockholm and Biriwa, Ghana, and trained as an agricultural entomologist (PhD in olfactory insect communication) before he became an artist. His installations — Test Site (2006 Tate Turbine Hall slides), Decision (2015), Mushroom Suite (2000 onward, with Amanita muscaria) — restage the gallery as a behavioural laboratory in which the visitor's own perception is the experimental subject. The argument is that art's claim on perception is empirical and falsifiable — and that uncertainty is not a defect of the experience but the experience itself."
  },
  a425: { // Cecile Abish
    message: "Abish was born in New York in 1931, lived between New York and Cologne with her husband Walter Abish, and trained in the post-war American sculpture milieu before turning to ephemeral floor-based installation in the 1970s. Her works — 4 Into 3 (1973), Equal Quantities (1975), Crossings (1978) — laid sand, gravel, plywood, and tape grids onto gallery floors in temporary configurations documented only photographically. The argument is that sculpture's permanence was always a curatorial assumption rather than a formal requirement — and that the photograph is the work's primary site."
  },
  a439: { // Christian Boltanski
    message: "Boltanski was born in Paris in 1944 — the day of the city's liberation — to a Russian-Jewish father who had spent the war hidden under the floorboards of the family's apartment, and he lived and worked in Malakoff until his death in 2021. His installations — Personnes (Paris Monumenta 2010), The Reserves (1989), the Inventory of the Children of Dijon — accumulate clothing, photographs, and recorded heartbeats into civic-scale monuments to absent specific persons. The estate argues that contemporary sculpture's responsibility is not to evoke the dead but to count them, one by one, even when no name remains."
  },
  a881: { // Christian Holstad
    message: "Holstad lives in Brooklyn, trained at Pratt, and emerged in the early 2000s as part of a generation of queer artists working between drawing, textile, and ephemeral performance. His Newspaper Erasure Drawings (2005 onward) systematically rub out images and words from the front pages of the New York Times, leaving residual figures, intimate gestures, and ghosts of the news. The argument is that the daily newspaper is a queer archive in disguise — that erasure can be a reading practice, not a destruction."
  },
  a984: { // Christiana Soulou
    message: "Soulou was born in Athens in 1961, lives there still, and trained at the Athens School of Fine Arts in a tradition that links Greek modernism to French illustration. Her pencil and coloured-pencil drawings — the Characters of Balzac (2012), Animal Kingdom (2015) — render literary characters and zoological subjects in a precise, slow line that owes equally to medieval manuscript and to Cocteau. She argues that drawing is a reading medium — that the body of a Balzac character can be made visible only by someone who has read the novel slowly enough to draw it."
  },
  a218: { // Christopher Williams
    message: "Williams lives in Los Angeles, trained at CalArts under John Baldessari, and has worked since the 1980s in a strict conceptual photography that re-stages the protocols of commercial product photography, industrial catalogue images, and Cold War darkroom technique. His series — Angola to Vietnam* (1989), For Example (2003 onward) — produce images that look correct in every commercial sense and refuse to deliver the documentary content their formal vocabulary promises. The argument is that the conventions of photography are themselves the subject — and that the obedient image is the most political one available."
  },
  a957: { // Dada Khanyisa
    message: "Khanyisa lives in Cape Town and Johannesburg, was born in 1991, and trained at the Michaelis School of Fine Art in the post-Marlene-Dumas South African painting generation. Their wall-mounted reliefs — Good Feelings (2020), Moments of Joy (2021) — combine carved and painted wood with mixed-media collage to depict Black queer social life with sharp stylised geometry and intimate humour. The argument is that South African contemporary art has been written too long around the apartheid trauma frame, and that Black queer joy is itself a sufficient and political painterly subject."
  },
  a284: { // Damian Loeb
    message: "Loeb lives and works in New York, is self-taught, and emerged in the late 1990s alongside other photorealist painters (Currin, Yuskavage) but with a specifically cinematic source vocabulary. His paintings — Sora (2009), Lunaris (2014) — render film stills, satellite imagery, and astronomical photography in seamless hyperreal oil, in which the viewer cannot determine whether the source was film or sky. The argument is that photorealism's job in the 21st century is no longer to imitate photography but to imitate the screen — and that the painting has to know which one it is."
  },
  a602: { // Daniel Buren
    message: "Buren was born in Boulogne-Billancourt in 1938, has lived and worked in Paris for sixty years, and is, with Toroni, Mosset, and Parmentier, a co-founder of BMPT — the 1960s group that declared painting could be reduced to a single repeated gesture. Since 1965 his entire output has consisted of 8.7-centimetre alternating stripes deployed in situ — including Les Deux Plateaux at the Palais Royal (1985–86) and the Grand Palais Excentrique(s) (2012). The argument is that the stripe is not a style but a tool — a visible measuring instrument by which any architectural site reveals its own politics."
  },
  a958: { // David Korty
    message: "Korty lives in Los Angeles and trained at UCLA; he emerged in the early 2000s alongside other West Coast painters working between watercolour landscape and post-pop graphic abstraction. His mixed-media works on paper and canvas — Blue Figure (2017), Yellow Grid (2019) — combine printed and silkscreened patterns with handpainted gestural marks in a slow accumulation that resembles both city plan and weather report. The argument is that the contemporary painter need not choose between observational drawing and abstraction — that the LA atmospheric register holds both at once."
  },
  a886: { // Dennis Kardon
    message: "Kardon lives in New York and trained at Yale; he has worked since the 1980s as a figurative painter and a critic, with the latter activity quietly informing the former. His Jewish Noses (1993) installed 54 oil-painted ceramic noses of friends and acquaintances on a single wall, turning a phenotypic anti-Semitic trope back on itself as material evidence. The argument is that figuration's contract with the viewer is governed by perception's social conditioning — and that the painter who refuses to confront that is not a neutral observer but an accomplice."
  },
  a864: { // Diane Dal-Pra
    message: "Dal-Pra was born in Bordeaux in 1991, lives in Paris, and trained at the École nationale supérieure des Beaux-Arts; she emerged in the late 2010s with a small, slow figurative practice. Her paintings — Devotional Objects (2022) — render women's hands, household textiles, ceramics, and ritual gestures in muted brown-grey palettes that recall both 17th-century Dutch genre painting and contemporary editorial photography. The argument is that domestic ritual is still under-painted, and that the supposedly minor categories of still life and genre scene remain serious territory for women's painting."
  },
  a968: { // Diego Marcon
    message: "Marcon was born in Busto Arsizio in 1985, lives and works in Milan, and trained at the Brera Academy. His short films — Ludwig (2018), The Parents' Room (2021) — combine prosthetic-faced live actors with looping musical structure and uncanny CGI to produce small horror narratives that the viewer cannot exit. The argument is that the cinematic short, the medium least at home in either the gallery or the cinema, is the form in which contemporary dread and pathos can be held without softening — and that loop, not narrative arc, is its grammar."
  },
  a868: { // Dominique Fung
    message: "Fung was born in Ottawa in 1987, lives in Brooklyn, and trained at Capilano College in Vancouver; she is of Hong Kong Chinese descent. Her lush figurative canvases — It's Not Polite to Stare (2021) — paint Asian women, museum vitrines, antiquities, and decorative interiors in a register that simultaneously inhabits and dismantles the European orientalist tradition. The argument is that the museum's catalogue is not a neutral record — the Asian female body has been a decorative subject of Western painting for three centuries, and the painter who refuses to flinch from that history changes what figuration can do now."
  },
  a889: { // Elad Lassry
    message: "Lassry was born in Tel Aviv in 1977, lives in Los Angeles, and trained at CalArts; his practice operates inside the conventions of commercial still-life and product photography that he refuses to either celebrate or critique. His photographs — Untitled (Red Cabbage) (2009) — appear in custom-coloured editorial frames that are physically continuous with the image; sculptures and films extend the same enquiry into other media. The argument is that the photographic object's frame is part of the image, that the image is part of the institutional decision to call it one, and that contemporary photography's subject is its own conditions of display."
  },
  a788: { // Elizabet Cerviño
    message: "Cerviño was born in Manzanillo, Cuba in 1986, trained at ISA in Havana, and lives between Havana and Madrid. Her elemental sculptures and installations — Llanto (2014), Marabú (2016) — work with earth, sea-salt, candle-wax, plant material, and her own body in a contemplative register at the seam of Caribbean Catholicism and Yoruba ritual. The argument is that the post-revolutionary Cuban artist's most reliable subject is the natural elements themselves — and that material, used with care, can carry a spirituality that explicit imagery would coarsen."
  },
  a180: { // Emma McIntyre
    message: "McIntyre was born in Auckland in 1990, lives in Los Angeles, and trained at the Auckland-based Elam School of Fine Arts followed by an MFA at ArtCenter College of Design. Her paintings — Rose in a glass (2023), A Wild Note of Longing (2023) — build saturated colour through oxidation, stain, and pour, refusing the hard-edge contemporary preference and returning to the lineage of Helen Frankenthaler and Pat Steir. The argument is that abstraction's chromatic, atmospheric register — once treated as the lyric problem of mid-century American women painters — is still unfinished, and the form is large enough to hold another generation's grief."
  },
  a254: { // Emmet Gowin
    message: "Gowin was born in Danville, Virginia in 1941, lives in New Jersey, and taught at Princeton for thirty-five years; he studied under Harry Callahan at RISD. His earliest series, the intimate portraits of his wife Edith and her extended family (1969 onward), gave way after 1980 to large-format aerial photography of US nuclear test sites, Hanford Reach, and mountaintop-removed Appalachia. The argument is that the photographer's responsibility extends in both directions — to the kitchen table and to the cratered Earth visible from a plane — and that the same person can hold both at the same level of care."
  },
  a437: { // Eric Orr
    message: "Orr was born in Covington, Kentucky in 1939, lived in Los Angeles, and died there in 1998; he was a central but quieter figure of the Light and Space generation alongside Robert Irwin, James Turrell, and Doug Wheeler. His immersive rooms — Zero Mass (1972), Sunrise (1985), Prime Matter (1989) — used fire, water, blood, lead, and gold leaf to make environments that were not paintings, not sculptures, and not light installations in the strict sense. The estate's argument is that California perception art was never a single Turrell school — that Orr's elemental, alchemical strand of it remains under-credited."
  },
  a732: { // Erwin Wurm
    message: "Wurm was born in Bruck an der Mur, Austria in 1954, lives in Vienna and Limberg, and trained in Salzburg and Vienna; he taught at the Universität für angewandte Kunst Wien for many years. His ongoing One Minute Sculptures (1996 onward) instruct the visitor to perform a brief physical absurdity with everyday objects — sit on a pencil, balance an orange on the forehead — making the visitor's own body the temporary sculpture; his Fat Cars (2001 onward) physically swell consumer goods into bulging forms. The argument is that sculpture's medium in the post-industrial age is the body's relation to commodity — and that the absurd instruction is its declaration of independence."
  },
  a915: { // Ferrari Sheppard
    message: "Sheppard was born in Chicago in 1983, lives in Los Angeles, and is largely self-taught after a journalistic career that included writing on global politics and a long stint in Beijing. His paintings — In My Mind (2021) — combine charcoal-drawn Black figures with gold-leaf passages and gestural acrylic on raw linen, producing portraits whose figures appear both contemporary and historically iconic. The argument is that the African American figurative painting tradition that runs from Charles White through Kerry James Marshall has a place for an explicitly devotional register — gold-leaf gravitas as a contemporary, not nostalgic, claim."
  },
  a897: { // France-Lise McGurn
    message: "McGurn was born in Glasgow in 1983 and lives there; she trained at the Glasgow School of Art and the Royal College of Art. Her paintings — Sleepless (2019) — extend beyond the canvas onto gallery walls and floors in fluid contour-line figures of women, lovers, dancers, and nightlife bodies, painted at the tempo of nightclub late-night looseness. The argument is that the wall painting — fresco's contemporary descendant — is a feminist sculptural format, because it refuses the discrete commodity object and treats the room itself as a body."
  },
  a959: { // Gabriel Kuri
    message: "Kuri was born in Mexico City in 1970, lives in Brussels and LA, and trained at the Universidad Iberoamericana before joining the seminal Gabriel Orozco taller in 1990s Mexico City. His sculptures — Donation Box (2010), Spent (2014) — combine printed receipts, ATM stubs, cigarette butts, marble, and concrete in arrangements that quietly diagram the economic micro-transactions that pass through every body in a day. The argument is that sculpture's subject in the late-capitalist period is the receipt — the smallest, most disposable, most truthful record of how an economy actually behaves."
  },
  a873: { // Gelitin
    message: "Gelitin is an Austrian collaborative of four — Ali Janka, Wolfgang Gantner, Florian Reither, and Tobias Urban — who have worked together as a single artistic body since 1993 and live across Vienna. Their projects — The B-Thing (an unauthorised balcony built off the World Trade Center, 2000), Hase (a 200-foot pink stuffed rabbit knitted by villagers on an Italian hillside, 2005) — are large-scale, absurd, and often physically risky public interventions performed half-naked. The argument is that contemporary sculpture's residual seriousness is itself the problem — that the collective body, drunk and exhibitionist, is the only honest material left."
  },

  // ── Chunk 2 (still session, before first cron) ──

  a953: { // Georg Herold
    message: "Herold was born in Jena in 1947 in East Germany, escaped to West Berlin in his twenties, and trained at the Hochschule für bildende Künste Hamburg under Sigmar Polke; he lives in Cologne. His work uses humble or absurd materials — roof battens, bricks, caviar, dry potato — assembled into figures that openly mock the heroic German sculpture lineage he inherited. The argument is that cultural value is a joke history plays on its materials, and the sculptor who pretends otherwise is participating in the lie."
  },
  a951: { // Georgia Gardner Gray
    message: "Gardner Gray was born in Washington, DC in 1988 and lives in Berlin; she trained at Cooper Union and at the Städelschule under Amy Sillman. Her figurative paintings — Commuters (2018), Terrace (2020) — stage social tableaux of contemporary Western life with the theatricality of a stage set and the cruelty of a satirist. She argues that the contemporary group portrait is still a serious painterly subject — provided the painter is willing to render social humiliation with the same precision as classical religious painting once rendered grace."
  },
  a876: { // Giorgio Griffa
    message: "Griffa was born in Turin in 1936, trained as a lawyer, and has worked in his Turin studio since the late 1960s — when he began making the rhythmic, unstretched-canvas paintings he is still making at almost ninety. Loosely associated with Arte Povera and Italian analytical painting, his work consists of repeated marks — dashes, horizontals, ideograms — that stop when the canvas is no longer 'curious about itself.' The argument is that painting is a practice of attention, not of production — and that the unfinished canvas, folded and stored when not exhibited, is the truer object."
  },
  a859: { // Giulia Cenci
    message: "Cenci was born in Cortona, Italy in 1988 and lives between Cortona and Amsterdam, where she trained at the Rijksakademie after the Brera Academy in Milan. Her installations — dry salvages (2022), secondary forest (2021) — assemble cast aluminium, industrial detritus, animal-derived molds, and hybrid creature-forms into landscape-scale environments that read as both archaeological and post-apocalyptic. She argues that contemporary sculpture's true subject is the agricultural-industrial ruin of Italy's countryside — and that the human figure is no longer the most legible body in that landscape."
  },
  a268: { // Glenn Kaino
    message: "Kaino was born in Los Angeles in 1972, lives there still, and trained at UC Irvine and UCSD; he has worked at the seam of conceptual sculpture and explicit political collaboration since the early 2000s. His project Bridge (2014) cast the raised gloved arm of Olympic athlete Tommie Smith into 200 suspended gold-plated sculptures (an act made in direct collaboration with Smith), and In the Light of a Shadow (2021) recast the bullets of Bloody Sunday as a chandelier. He argues that political memory needs material vessels at the right scale — and that contemporary sculpture's most useful task is to be the steady collaborator."
  },
  a799: { // Gu Dexin
    message: "Gu Dexin was born in Beijing in 1962, was largely self-taught, and was one of the foundational figures of the 1985 New Wave Chinese avant-garde alongside Huang Yong Ping and Xu Bing. His installations of rotting fruit, melted plastic, raw meat, and pressed flesh — Plastic Pieces (1983–89), 2009-05-02 (2009) — confronted Chinese state ideology with materials that explicitly refused permanence. He announced his retirement from art on the date that titles his last work — May 2nd 2009 — and has not made art since, an act that itself argues that the artist's most powerful gesture can be to stop."
  },
  a869: { // Günther Förg
    message: "Förg was born in Füssen in 1952, lived between Munich and Areuse, Switzerland, and died in 2013; he trained at the Akademie der Bildenden Künste München under Karl Fred Dahmen. His Grey Paintings (1973 onward), large lead-and-aluminium wall works, architecture photographs of fascist-era Italian buildings, and Spot Paintings (2007) collapse the language of postwar abstract painting into a deliberately uneven, almost careless gesture. He treats the inheritance of European modernist abstraction not as a faith but as a found language — useable, exhaustible, and worth refusing to perfect."
  },
  a230: { // Harry Callahan
    message: "Callahan was born in Detroit in 1912, taught himself photography in the late 1930s, and was hired by László Moholy-Nagy at Chicago's Institute of Design in 1946; he later led the photography department at RISD. His three lifelong subjects — his wife Eleanor (photographed for nearly forty years), Chicago and Cape Cod street scenes, and dense natural-form abstractions — built a body of work that quietly redefined American mid-century photography. He argued that photography's discipline was attention rather than novelty — that the same subject, returned to over decades, was the longest education a photographer could give themselves."
  },
  a856: { // Hejum Bä
    message: "Hejum Bä was born in South Korea in 1987 and trained at Seoul National University before moving to a contemporary international practice. Her abstract canvases work with line, gesture, and the body's relationship to surface in a register that draws equally on East Asian calligraphic tradition and post-painterly Western abstraction. She argues that the line — drawn, painted, or breathed onto a surface — is the most under-credited unit of painting, and that contemporary abstraction's preoccupation with field has obscured what a single mark can still do."
  },
  a969: { // Helen Marten
    message: "Marten was born in Macclesfield, England in 1985 and lives in London; she trained at Central Saint Martins and the Ruskin School at Oxford. Her dense, hybrid installations — Lunar Nibs (2016), Eucalyptus, Let Us In (2016) — combine welded steel, ceramic, screen-printed textile, found object, and dense written text into compositions that refuse to settle into a single interpretive frame. The argument, which won her both the 2016 Turner Prize and the inaugural Hepworth Sculpture Prize in the same year, is that contemporary sculpture's task is to overwhelm the viewer's pattern-matching ability — to be more dense than the museum label can be."
  },

  // ── Chunk 3 (50-batch) ──

  a261: { // Hong Hao
    message: "Hong Hao was born in Beijing in 1965 and lives there still; he trained at the Central Academy of Fine Arts in the early years of China's economic opening. His My Things (2001 onward) is built by scanning every object that passes through his hands over the course of a year — receipts, packaging, coins, takeout — and arraying them at flat scale into a single image; his Selected Scriptures (1992) reworked classical-style maps as documents of geopolitical absurdity. The argument is that the post-1980s Chinese subject can be inventoried only at the level of the consumer object — and that the scan-bed, not the camera, is its honest portrait."
  },
  a238: { // Huong Dodinh
    message: "Dodinh was born in Vietnam in 1945 and arrived in Paris as a young child in the early 1950s; she has worked from a single Paris studio for over sixty years. Her near-white, near-grey monochromatic paintings (T-126 and the long Untitled series) build slow tonal fields with a quietness that owes equally to Buddhist meditation and to Yves Klein. The argument is that monochrome can be an act of devotion rather than reduction — that silence in painting is not the absence of subject but its most concentrated form."
  },
  a947: { // Isabella Ducrot
    message: "Ducrot was born in Naples in 1931, lives in Rome, and came to art-making only in her sixties after a long life in Italian publishing and Asian-textile collecting. Her practice — Big Aubergines (2021), Tendernesses (2022) — works on antique fabric, sewn paper, and large-scale painted cloth with patterns drawn from the Silk Road textiles she spent decades studying. The argument is that the late-life entry into art is not a footnote but a position — that pattern, ornament, and care for the made object are subjects painting cannot reach by youth alone."
  },
  a785: { // Iván Capote
    message: "Capote was born in Pinar del Río, Cuba in 1973, lives in Havana, and trained at the Instituto Superior de Arte (ISA); his twin brother Yoan Capote is also a sculptor. His sculptures — Dyslexia (2010), Reflexión (2014) — work through visual puns, anagrams, and typographic paradoxes carved into stone, neon, and steel. The argument is that the contemporary Cuban condition is itself a linguistic paradox — and that the most efficient sculptural medium for it is the visual joke that, once read, refuses to stop meaning."
  },
  a810: { // Jannis Kounellis
    message: "Kounellis was born in Piraeus, Greece in 1936, moved to Rome in 1956, and died there in 2017; he trained at the Accademia di Belle Arti di Roma and married into Italy's curatorial avant-garde. His 12 Horses (Galleria L'Attico, Rome, 1969) tethered live horses inside a gallery space, and his decades-long use of coal, wool, raw steel, fire, and lead made him a central figure of Arte Povera alongside Pistoletto, Anselmo, and Pascali. He argued that materials carry history before the artist arrives — coal is the Industrial Revolution, wool is rural labour — and that sculpture's job is to let them speak rather than to compose them."
  },
  a848: { // Jean-Marie Appriou
    message: "Appriou was born in Brest, France in 1986 and lives in Paris; he trained at the École européenne supérieure d'art de Bretagne in Lorient. His cast-aluminium, bronze, and glass figures — The Horses and Les Cavaliers (2019, installed in Central Park) — are made by deliberately preserving the marks of the foundry process, so casting accidents and seams remain legible. The argument is that contemporary sculpture's romance with the polished finished surface obscures the medium's actual subject — fire, mold, gravity — and that the half-finished cast tells the truth the finished bronze conceals."
  },
  a737: { // Jean-Michel Othoniel
    message: "Othoniel was born in Saint-Étienne, France in 1964, lives in Paris, and trained at the École nationale supérieure d'arts de Cergy. His Murano-glass-bead sculptures — Le Kiosque des Noctambules (2000, the Palais Royal Métro entrance), The Big Wave (2017), the Versailles fountains — translate jewelry's intimate scale into architecture and landscape. The argument is that public sculpture has a permission to be beautiful that contemporary practice has largely refused — and that the glass bead, an object of explicit decoration, is the medium most honest about that permission."
  },
  a875: { // Jenna Gribbon
    message: "Gribbon was born in Knoxville, Tennessee in 1978 and lives in Brooklyn; she trained at the University of Tennessee and Hunter College. Her paintings — M Lit by Phone (2021) is typical — depict her partner Mackenzie Scott (the musician Torres) in the intimate domestic settings of contemporary lesbian life, painted with the brushy, fast figurative hand of mid-century American painting. The argument is that queer domesticity is a sufficient and serious painterly subject, and that the lover-as-model tradition (from Bonnard's Marthe to Hammershøi's Ida) was never closed — it was simply waiting for the painters with the standing to enter it."
  },
  a882: { // Jessie Homer French
    message: "Homer French was born in New York in 1940 and has lived in California's Mojave Desert for forty years; she is self-taught and only began exhibiting widely in her seventies. Her Fire Maps (2010 onward) and rural-California narrative paintings show wildfires, fishermen, cemeteries, and ranch animals in a flattened folk-modernist register. The argument is that the western American landscape's contemporary subject is fire — and that the painter who has lived through twenty years of seasonal evacuation can paint it with a directness no urban observer can match."
  },
  a880: { // Jim Hodges
    message: "Hodges was born in Spokane, Washington in 1957, trained at Fort Wright College and Pratt Institute, and lives in New York. His sculptures and installations — Every Touch (1995, silk flowers sewn into a curtain), Untitled (Gate) (1991), spider-web stitching, and the long Movements series of mirrored stones — treat fragility itself as a structural material rather than a defect. He argues that the materials American sculpture inherited from minimalism (steel, concrete, fluorescent tube) were chosen as much for emotional refusal as for industrial honesty, and that an alternative inheritance — paper, silk, mirror, breath — is available."
  },
  a341: { // JoAnn Verburg
    message: "Verburg was born in Summit, New Jersey in 1950, lives between Minneapolis and Spoleto, Italy, and trained at Rochester Institute of Technology under Nathan Lyons. Her multi-panel large-format colour photographs — Present Tense (2007), After Lunch (1999), the long Olive Trees series — slow Italian domestic and arboreal subjects to a tempo that registers as durational, not single-frame. The argument is that the photograph need not be a punctum — that the multi-panel composite can give photography the slow, conversational time that painting has historically claimed for itself."
  },
  a885: { // Johannes Kahrs
    message: "Kahrs was born in Bremen in 1965 and lives in Berlin; he trained at the Hochschule der Künste Berlin under Karl Horst Hödicke. His oil paintings — Untitled (man bending over) (2007) is characteristic — extract single frames from cinema, news media, and pornography and render them at the deliberate blur of a paused video tape on a 1990s television. The argument is that the photographic source image is not transcended by painting but slowed down by it — and that the slow surface registers exactly what the cinematic frame, in motion, refused to show."
  },
  a849: { // John Armleder
    message: "Armleder was born in Geneva in 1948, lives there still, and co-founded the Ecart group in 1969 — a Swiss outpost of the Fluxus international that briefly hosted Beuys, Filliou, and Brecht. His Furniture Sculptures (1979 onward, combining found furniture with modernist abstract painting) and Pour Paintings (1990s onward, large dot and pour canvases) treat the readymade and the abstract painting as parallel exhausted lineages he can deploy interchangeably. The argument is that postwar European art's twin inheritances — Duchamp and Mondrian — are no longer in dialectical tension but in flat juxtaposition, and that taking that flatness seriously is a curatorial discipline."
  },
  a248: { // John Gerrard
    message: "Gerrard was born in Dublin in 1974 and lives in Dublin and Vienna; he trained at the Ruskin School at Oxford and the Städelschule in Frankfurt. His Solar Reserve (2014) and Western Flag (2017) are real-time computer simulations — running continuously on dedicated hardware in the gallery — that depict, respectively, the Crescent Dunes solar plant in Nevada and the historical site of the Spindletop oil gusher in Texas. The argument is that the computer simulation is now sculpture's most honest medium for energy and ecology — because it can run for the lifetime of a viewer and never repeat."
  },
  a896: { // John McAllister
    message: "McAllister lives and works in Provincetown, Massachusetts, and trained at the University of New Mexico; he emerged in the 2000s with a distinctly West-Coast-influenced painterly practice. His landscapes and interiors — Sumptuous Sun Sets Sweltering (2014) — drench the Fauvist-Bonnard chromatic tradition in late-summer Californian saturation, with title-as-alliteration as part of the painting. The argument is that pleasure and decoration are still under-credited as painterly subjects — that the Bonnard interior is not a closed historical chapter but a viable contemporary register."
  },
  a871: { // Jonathan Gardner
    message: "Gardner was born in Lexington, Kentucky in 1982 and lives in Brooklyn; he trained at the Maryland Institute College of Art and at the School of the Art Institute of Chicago. His paintings — The Reader (2018) — collage Picasso, Magritte, Léger, and mid-century Italian magazine illustration into flat, deadpan figurative compositions of women in domestic interiors. The argument is that the early-modernist visual language is now available as a vernacular — quoting Picasso and Léger is no different from quoting an advertisement — and that the contemporary painter can use them without piety."
  },
  a814: { // Jorge Macchi
    message: "Macchi was born in Buenos Aires in 1963, lives there still, and trained at the Universidad Nacional de las Artes. His drawings, collages, and installations — Buenos Aires Tour (2003, in which a broken pane of glass is read as a city map), Music Stands Still (2006) — turn everyday materials (atlases, sheet music, newspapers) into melancholic small constructions. The argument is that the poetic image is still available to conceptual art if the artist is willing to slow down — that a single broken windowpane, attended to long enough, becomes a sufficient subject."
  },
  a207: { // Josh Smith
    message: "Smith was born in Okinawa, Japan in 1976 to American military parents and lives in New York; he is largely self-taught and worked for years as Christopher Wool's studio assistant. He paints continuously, often producing several canvases a day, organised into long signature motifs — his own name, fish, leaves, palm trees, skeletons, devils, plates — at every scale from postcard to wall. The argument is that the singular masterpiece is a fiction of the secondary market — and that the painter's truer unit of production is the studio-day, the wall-of-paintings, the unstoppable serial output."
  },
  a834: { // José Antonio Suárez Londoño
    message: "Suárez Londoño was born in Medellín, Colombia in 1955, lives there still, and trained as a microbiologist before turning fully to art. Since 1996 he has kept an annual notebook — the Yearbooks — in which he completes one small drawing or etching per day on a literary, scientific, or art-historical theme decided at the start of each January. The argument is that drawing is a daily discipline, not an event — and that the slow accumulation of small marks across a lifetime is the truest form a visual practice can take."
  },
  a817: { // José Mesías
    message: "Mesías was born in Havana in 1989, lives there still, and trained at ISA (Instituto Superior de Arte) in Havana. His paintings and drawings work in a figurative register grounded in Cuban personal and national narrative, often with rural and family subjects. The argument is that the next generation of Cuban painting still has serious figurative work to do — that the figure has not been exhausted by the country's earlier conceptual and performance generations."
  },
  a779: { // Juan Araujo
    message: "Araujo was born in Caracas in 1971 and lives between Lisbon and Caracas; he trained at the Instituto Universitario de Estudios Superiores de Artes Plásticas Armando Reverón. His paintings — Casa de Vidro (2010, after Lina Bo Bardi), the Niemeyer Studies (2013), small reproductions of Burle Marx photographs — meticulously copy book-page reproductions of Latin American modernist architecture in oil at the size of the source photograph. The argument is that the canonical buildings of Brazilian and Venezuelan modernism are known to most viewers only as printed reproductions — and that painting that reproduction is more honest than painting the building."
  },
  a812: { // Julio Le Parc
    message: "Le Parc was born in Mendoza, Argentina in 1928, moved to Paris in 1958, and was a founding member in 1960 of the Groupe de recherche d'art visuel (GRAV) with François Morellet, Horacio García Rossi, and others. His Continuel-Lumière (1963), Modulation (1976), and Cellule projection rooms use moving light, motorised steel, and mirror to produce kinetic perceptual environments that activate the viewer's body. The argument — which won him the Venice Biennale Grand Prize in 1966 — is that art's social emancipation requires removing the singular master-author and replacing them with a participatory perceptual situation."
  },
  a936: { // Karimah Ashadu
    message: "Ashadu was born in London in 1985 to a Nigerian family and lives in Hamburg and Lagos; she trained at the Slade and the Royal College of Art. Her films — Machine Boys (2024, Silver Lion at Venice), Plateau (2021), Lagos Sand Merchants (2013) — document informal labour in Nigerian okada drivers, mineworkers, and sand-harvesters with a long-take, ambient-sound discipline. The argument is that West African informal economies are an underseen contemporary subject — and that the film camera is the only medium that can hold their duration without exoticising them."
  },
  a879: { // Karin Gulbran
    message: "Gulbran lives and works in Los Angeles and trained at the California Institute of the Arts (CalArts); she emerged in the late 1990s alongside Laura Owens and Sue Williams in a generation that took pleasure-and-decoration seriously. Her ceramic landscape vessels and paintings render trees, deer, mushrooms, and pastoral scenes in a deliberately naive folk hand. The argument is that the Californian decorative-and-folk register — long dismissed as light — is in fact a serious philosophical position about how much pleasure painting is allowed to give."
  },
  a952: { // Kati Heck
    message: "Heck was born in Düsseldorf in 1979, trained at the Royal Academy of Fine Arts Antwerp under Karel Dierickx, and lives between Antwerp and the Belgian-Dutch border. Her paintings — Hasenherz (2017), Schnaps Idee (2014) — combine virtuoso figurative oil technique with surreal humour, prosthetic costumes, and family-and-friends models in compositions that read as both portrait and slapstick. The argument is that European figurative oil-painting tradition has been treated too solemnly — and that humour, when grounded in genuine craft, is not lighter than gravity but its more honest companion."
  },
  a982: { // Katja Seib
    message: "Seib was born in Düsseldorf in 1989, trained at the Kunstakademie Düsseldorf and the Royal College of Art, and lives in Los Angeles. Her figurative oil paintings on coarse hessian — The Confidants (2020), Fortune Teller (2021) — depict women in moody, theatrical interiors with a palette closer to early Italian Renaissance fresco than to contemporary LA painting. The argument is that the coarse-canvas substrate is part of the image's subject — that the texture beneath the figure is the painter's most undervalued formal decision."
  },
  a235: { // Keith Coventry
    message: "Coventry was born in Burnley, England in 1958, trained at Brighton Polytechnic and Chelsea College of Art, and lives in London; he is loosely associated with the YBA generation. His Estate Paintings (1991 onward) depict the layouts of London council-housing estates in pure Suprematist white-on-white style; his White Abstract (Junk) series (2012) presses fast-food trash into pristine cast-plaster reliefs. The argument — which won him the John Moores Painting Prize in 2010 — is that British social housing's geometry is, on paper, the most rigorous abstract painting the postwar period produced, and that painting that geometry honestly is also painting the politics."
  },
  a307: { // Kenjiro Okazaki
    message: "Okazaki was born in Tokyo in 1955 and works there as an artist, theorist, art historian, and landscape designer; he represented Japan at the 2002 Venice Architecture Biennale. His Topica Pictus (2020) paintings and Zerstreut liegen Steine (2002) sculptures work alongside dense theoretical writing on aesthetics, the Japanese garden, and contemporary critical theory. The argument is that the artist's work and the theorist's writing are not separate professional lives but a single body of thought — and that Japanese contemporary art's most distinctive contribution is the artist-theorist as integrated figure."
  },
  a256: { // Kevin Francis Gray
    message: "Gray was born in County Armagh, Northern Ireland in 1972, trained at the National College of Art and Design Dublin and Goldsmiths, and works between London and Pietrasanta in the Italian Carrara marble district. His marble and bronze figures — Ballerina (2011), Reclining Nude (2017), the Veiled series — use classical Italian carving technique to make veiled bodies, hooded street kids, and beaded subjects that sit at the seam of classical and contemporary. The argument is that classical figurative carving is not an exhausted tradition — its contemporary subject is simply who gets sculpted, and at what scale of dignity."
  },
  a327: { // Kiki Smith
    message: "Smith was born in Nuremberg in 1954, the daughter of sculptor Tony Smith, and was raised in New Jersey; she trained at the Hartford Art School and worked as an EMT before her art career began in earnest in the early 1980s. Her bronze, glass, ceramic, and tapestry figures — Born (2002), Lilith (1994), Pyre Woman, the body-fluid series — return obsessively to the female body in mystical, Catholic-iconographic, and natural registers. The argument is that the body is a sacred and porous object — leaking, gestating, transforming — and that sculpture's traditional treatment of it as closed marble was a violence the medium can now correct."
  },
  a964: { // Klara Liden
    message: "Liden was born in Stockholm in 1979, lives between Stockholm and Berlin, and trained as an architect at the Royal Institute of Technology before crossing into art at Konstfack. Her videos, sculptures, and interventions — Bodies of Society (2006, in which she destroys a bicycle with a hammer in a tiled bathroom), Pretty Vacant (2010), the trash-can totems she installs in public space — confront the body against urban infrastructure with deliberate physical violence. The argument is that contemporary public space's quiet hostility to bodies is only legible when it is met with returning bodily force — that politeness is the city's most effective form of erasure."
  },
  a290: { // Kylie Manning
    message: "Manning was born in Alaska in 1983, raised between Alaska and Mexico, and lives in Brooklyn; she trained at Cooper Union and the New York Studio School. Her paintings — Sea Change (2023), In Bloom (2022) — combine fluid, atmospheric figuration with chromatic abstraction in a register that draws on both Inuit visual tradition and Mexican muralism. The argument is that contemporary American painting need not choose between figuration and abstraction — and that the artist raised between two opposed visual cultures is uniquely positioned to refuse the choice."
  },
  a962: { // Lawrence Lek
    message: "Lek was born in Frankfurt in 1982 to a Malaysian-Chinese family, was raised between Hong Kong, Singapore, and London, and lives in London; he trained as an architect at the Architectural Association before moving into video and games. His CGI films and playable game-works — Geomancer (2017), AIDOL (2019), Black Cloud (2021) — construct speculative worlds in which AI characters develop consciousness, sing pop songs, and act out post-human dramas inside hyperreal architectural environments. The argument is that the video game engine is contemporary sculpture's most consequential new medium — and that the AI protagonist is the dramatic figure best suited to the present moment."
  },
  a887: { // Lee Kit
    message: "Lee Kit was born in Hong Kong in 1978, lives in Taipei, and trained at the Chinese University of Hong Kong; he represented Hong Kong at the 2013 Venice Biennale. His hand-painted cloth panels, projection-light installations, and gentle still-life paintings — Hold your breath, dance slowly (2016) — work in a register of quiet domesticity that takes shampoo bottles, T-shirts, and Cantopop lyrics as legitimate subjects. The argument is that contemporary art's pursuit of intensity has obscured a humbler available subject — the texture of an ordinary day — and that Cantonese popular culture is its proper material vocabulary."
  },
  a277: { // Lee Kun-Yong
    message: "Lee Kun-Yong was born in Hwanghae Province (now North Korea) in 1942, fled south during the Korean War, and trained at Hongik University in Seoul; he founded the Avant-Garde Association (AG) in 1969. His performance-and-drawing works — The Method of Drawing (1976, in which he draws on a wall using only his body's range of motion), Logic of Place (1975) — established a Korean conceptual vocabulary distinct from Japanese Mono-ha and Western performance. The argument is that the body's joint and reach are the painter's first measuring instrument, and that the Korean avant-garde of the 1970s was solving a different problem than its Japanese and American contemporaries — one that took the political body of post-war Korea as starting point."
  },
  a778: { // Leila Alaoui
    message: "Alaoui was born in Paris in 1982 to a Moroccan family and was killed in the 2016 al-Qaeda attack on the Splendid Hotel in Ouagadougou while photographing for an Amnesty International women's-rights commission. Her Les Marocains (2010–2014) project documented the diversity of Moroccan dress, ethnicity, and labour in formal large-format portraiture; her Crossings (2013) video installation documented sub-Saharan migrants in transit through Morocco toward Europe. The estate-managed argument is that documentary photography's responsibility is to the named, specific person in front of the lens — and that the photographer's own life can be the cost of taking that responsibility seriously."
  },
  a872: { // Lenz Geerk
    message: "Geerk was born in Basel, Switzerland in 1988, trained at the Düsseldorf Kunstakademie, and lives in Düsseldorf. His paintings — the Table Trilogy (2019) — depict single figures alone at a table or in domestic interiors in muted brown-grey palettes that recall Vilhelm Hammershøi and Lucian Freud's quietest works. The argument is that solitary contemporary interiority is a serious painterly subject — that the painting of a single person, slowly looking at nothing, holds the present moment more accurately than most narrative figuration."
  },
  a280: { // Li Songsong
    message: "Li Songsong was born in Beijing in 1973, trained at the Central Academy of Fine Arts, and lives in Beijing. His thickly impastoed oil paintings — A Dream of Power (2010), Spring of 1959 (2007) — work directly from photographs documenting twentieth-century Chinese political history, with paint laid on so heavily that the photographic source becomes visible only when one steps back. The argument is that Chinese political memory is mediated almost entirely through black-and-white press photography — and that painting's task is to slow that mediation down to the speed at which a viewer is forced to look."
  },
  a919: { // Lily Stockman
    message: "Stockman was born in New Jersey in 1982, lives in Los Angeles, and trained at Harvard and NYU; she also co-founded Block Shop Textiles, a workshop in Bagru, Rajasthan that produces hand-block-printed cottons with traditional Indian artisans. Her hard-edge oil-on-linen abstractions — the Garden Paintings (2019) — derive their shapes from botany, Mughal miniature, and the textile patterns of her parallel printing practice. The argument is that contemporary American hard-edge abstraction has been written as a Northern minimalist tradition — and that recasting it as derived from Indian textile pattern is a chromatic and political correction."
  },
  a283: { // Liu Jianhua
    message: "Liu Jianhua was born in Jiangxi province in 1962, trained at the Jingdezhen Ceramic Institute (the historic centre of Chinese porcelain), and lives in Shanghai. His porcelain installations — Trace (2011), Square (2014) — translate ceramic, China's most historically over-determined craft material, into contemporary minimalist forms whose engagement with consumer culture and global trade routes is read only at the level of material. The argument is that Chinese contemporary sculpture's most under-credited medium is the one Westerners associate with souvenir — and that working through the actual kilns of Jingdezhen is a political position about what counts as serious sculpture."
  },
  a393: { // Liu Wei
    message: "Liu Wei was born in Beijing in 1972, trained at the China Academy of Art in Hangzhou, and lives in Beijing; he is part of a Post-Sense Sensibility generation that emerged in the late 1990s. His sculptures and installations — Library (2012, dense compressed-book towers), Purple Air (2003, urban-rubble photo prints) — engage the rapid demolition and rebuilding of Chinese cities and the politics of public cultural display. The argument is that the post-1990s Chinese urban condition is too fast to be painted — its true sculptural material is the demolition rubble itself, packed into geometric volumes that read as both library and bunker."
  },
  a174: { // Liu Ye
    message: "Liu Ye was born in Beijing in 1964, trained at the Central Academy of Fine Arts and at the Hochschule der Künste Berlin (1990s), and lives in Beijing. His paintings — Bamboo Bamboo Broadway (2011), the long Book Painting series (2013 onward) — depict small childlike figures, books, Miffy the rabbit, and Mondrian-derived geometry in jewel-like, slow, deliberately tender compositions. The argument is that childhood, the book, and the Western-modernist primary colour are not naïve subjects — and that the contemporary Chinese painter can claim them precisely because Western painting has, by exhaustion, conceded them."
  },
  a917: { // Lu Song
    message: "Lu Song was born in Beijing in 1982, trained at Wimbledon College of Art in London, and lives in Beijing. His paintings — Wanderer in the Mist (2018) — work in a moody, cinematic atmospheric register that draws on European Romantic landscape, contemporary film stills, and personal memory. The argument is that the figure-in-landscape, a tradition that Chinese ink painting and German Romanticism share more than either school admits, is still the most consequential subject available to a painter formed between both."
  },
  a960: { // Lucia Laguna
    message: "Laguna was born in São Fidélis, Brazil in 1941, did not begin painting until her fifties, and lives in the working-class Lins neighborhood of Rio de Janeiro. Her paintings — Estudo (2010), Paisagem (2015) — layer views from her studio window, fragments of Brazilian landscape painting tradition, and the daily sounds of the favela into compositions made collaboratively with younger assistants she has trained. The argument is that painting is not a solo practice and the late-life artist not a footnote — that the studio is a small social form whose visible labour relations belong inside the painting."
  },
  a901: { // Ludovic Nkoth
    message: "Nkoth was born in Cameroon in 1994 and emigrated to South Carolina at age thirteen; he lives in New York and trained at the New York Academy of Art. His paintings — Mother and Son (2021) — depict Black figures in expressive, gesturally painted scenes of African and African-American family life, with the open paint of an oil-painter who has thoroughly absorbed late-twentieth-century Black figurative painting. The argument is that the contemporary diasporic African painter's responsibility is to the named family — the mother, son, brother — rendered with the same painterly seriousness Renaissance painting reserved for sacred subjects."
  },
  a902: { // Luigi Ontani
    message: "Ontani was born in Vergato, Italy in 1943 and lives in Rome; he emerged in the 1960s with the Arte Povera generation but worked at its eccentric, theatrical edge. His tableaux vivants (1970 onward), in which he poses as a saint, mythological figure, or hybrid divinity, alongside ceramic sculpture, photography, and Balinese-craft collaborations, build a single mythological-autobiographical universe. The argument is that Italian Catholic, classical, and pagan iconographies are still the contemporary Italian artist's deepest available vocabulary — and that the self-portrait as god, hero, or hermaphrodite is its honest contemporary form."
  },
  a813: { // Luis López-Chávez
    message: "López-Chávez was born in Cuba in 1983 and trained at ISA (Instituto Superior de Arte) in Havana. His paintings layer fragmented imagery and abstract gesture in compositions that work between Cuban history, image-mediation, and perceptual interruption. The argument is that the contemporary Cuban painting subject is itself fragmented — that the visual culture of the post-Special-Period generation is collaged, not whole, and that painting must honour that condition rather than smooth it over."
  },
  a424: { // Léon Wuidar
    message: "Wuidar was born in Liège, Belgium in 1938 and has worked from a single studio there for over six decades; he trained at the Académie des beaux-arts de Liège. His paintings — Sans titre (1975), the long Journal series (2015 onward) — combine warm-toned, hand-drawn geometric abstraction with a daily-diaristic dimension where each work is a single date's composition. The argument is that geometric abstraction is not an austere or chilly tradition — that hand-drawn line and warm colour are part of geometry's available vocabulary, and that the daily painting record is its most honest format."
  },
  a829: { // Manuela Sedmach
    message: "Sedmach was born in Trieste, Italy in 1953 and lives there; her work is shaped by Trieste's specific geography — the Adriatic light, the limestone karst, the long border between Italy and Slovenia. Her atmospheric near-abstract canvases — Paesaggio (2010), Orizzonte (2015) — render sea, fog, and horizon at the threshold where landscape becomes pure tonal field. The argument is that Trieste's specific light is itself a painting subject — neither Italian luminism nor Slovene mysticism, but the meteorological condition particular to a bordered city the European art map has long underweighted."
  },
  a791: { // Marcelo Cidade
    message: "Cidade was born in São Paulo in 1979 and lives there; he emerged in the early 2000s alongside other São Paulo artists working at the seam of skateboard culture, urban intervention, and conceptual sculpture. His works — Transestatal (2010), Tempo Suspenso de um Estado Provisório (2011) — repurpose materials lifted directly from public space (broken bus shelters, surveillance-camera housings, found concrete) into gallery sculptures that diagram urban surveillance and the legacies of Brazilian modernist architecture. The argument is that the city is a found-object archive and that the artist's most precise tool is the small, almost unnoticed displacement of one of its parts."
  },

  // ── Chunk 4 (50-batch) ──

  a467: { // Marisol
    message: "Marisol Escobar was born in Paris in 1930 to wealthy Venezuelan parents, moved to New York in 1950, and worked there until her death in 2016; her mother died when she was eleven and she chose, in protest of silence, to use only her first name for the rest of her life. Her wooden-block figures — The Family (1962), Women and Dog (1964), The Party (1965) — combine carving, painting, photo-transfer, and found object to make life-sized satirical portraits of public figures and family groups. She was briefly the most famous female Pop artist in the United States, then was systematically written out of the Pop canon — and her estate argument is that the historical record is being corrected, slowly, work by work."
  },
  a833: { // Marta Spagnoli
    message: "Spagnoli was born in Verona, Italy in 1994 and lives in Venice; she trained at the Accademia di Belle Arti di Venezia. Her gestural paintings — Creature (2020), Paesaggio (2022) — work in the unstable territory between figure and abstraction, drawing on Venetian colour tradition, animal forms, and a kind of mythological half-figuration that refuses to fully resolve. The argument is that the youngest generation of Italian painters still has a usable claim on the Venetian colour heritage — provided they enter it through abstraction rather than imitation."
  },
  a900: { // Matt Mullican
    message: "Mullican was born in Santa Monica in 1951, trained at CalArts under John Baldessari, and lives in New York and Berlin. Since the late 1970s he has built a single life-long cosmology — Five into One (1991) and the ongoing That Person's Workbook (2007) — a system of colour-coded zones (Subjective, Signs, Arts, World Framed, World Unframed) that he charts in posters, banners, drawings, and hypnotically induced 'That Person' performances. He argues that contemporary conceptual art has been too embarrassed to claim cosmology — and that a system of meaning, sustained over fifty years by one artist, is itself the work."
  },
  a940: { // Meriem Bennani
    message: "Bennani was born in Rabat, Morocco in 1988 and lives in Brooklyn; she trained at Cooper Union and at the École nationale supérieure des Arts Décoratifs in Paris. Her video installations — Party on the CAPS (2018–19), Mission Teens (2019), Fardaous Funjab (2015) — combine documentary footage, hand-drawn animation, talking-head interview, and meme humour into multichannel pieces that document Moroccan and diasporic life with the speed of a phone screen. The argument is that diasporic identity is now lived as a multichannel feed — and that the gallery's task is to give it a room rather than a single screen."
  },
  a214: { // Merrill Wagner
    message: "Wagner was born in Tacoma, Washington in 1935, lived in New York until her death in 2024, and was married to the painter Robert Ryman for over fifty years; she trained at Sarah Lawrence College. Her paintings — the long Rust series of the 1980s on weathered steel, the Seasonal stone works, the slate paintings — applied paint to surfaces that already had material history, letting weather, rust, and mineral grain become part of the composition. The argument is that the contemporary abstract painting's surface — stretched white cotton — was a convention, not a neutral ground, and that any material the world has already inscribed can be the painter's substrate."
  },
  a825: { // Michelangelo Pistoletto
    message: "Pistoletto was born in Biella, Italy in 1933 and still lives and works there at age 92; he is one of the founding figures of Arte Povera. His Mirror Paintings (1962 onward) screen-print figures onto polished stainless-steel surfaces so that the gallery viewer is inscribed inside the work; Venus of the Rags (1967) places a classical Venus before a heap of used clothing; and Cittadellarte (founded 1998 in Biella) extends the practice into a working model for socially engaged art education. The argument, sustained over six decades, is that the mirror is the most honest medium of post-war art — and that the viewer's own body inside the frame is the contemporary subject."
  },
  a903: { // Mimmo Paladino
    message: "Paladino was born in Paduli, Italy in 1948, lives in Rome and Paduli, and is a central figure of the Italian Transavanguardia movement theorised by Achille Bonito Oliva in the late 1970s. His Montagna di sale (1990, a 60-tonne salt-and-horses sculpture installed in Naples and later Milan) and his decades of painting, etching, and bronze figure draw on Etruscan, Egyptian, early Christian, and archaic Mediterranean visual vocabularies. The argument is that the post-Conceptual moment of the late 1970s did not require a clean break — that the painter could return to ancient image traditions without nostalgia, treating them as still-operative."
  },
  a861: { // Monster Chetwynd
    message: "Chetwynd was born in London in 1973 (and has worked under the names Spartacus and Marvin Gaye before settling on Monster), trained at the Slade and the Royal College of Art, and lives in Glasgow. Her costumed performance works — Hermitos Children (2008), Bat Opera (2003 onward) — restage Pasolini, Rabelais, fairy tale, sci-fi, and political iconography with sewn cardboard costumes, recruited performers, and deliberately rough production values. The argument is that contemporary performance's most undervalued tool is amateur cardboard — and that the polished art-fair production is a confession of unseriousness, not its opposite."
  },
  a971: { // Nabuqi
    message: "Nabuqi was born in Inner Mongolia in 1984 and lives in Beijing; she trained at the Central Academy of Fine Arts (CAFA). Her installations — Do real things happen in moments of rationality? (2018), A View Beyond Space (2015) — stage uncanny scenes in which fabricated foliage, mass-produced consumer objects, and fragments of generic architecture inhabit the gallery as if a stage set abandoned mid-performance. The argument is that the contemporary Chinese urban interior is itself a stage — and that sculpture's job is to render the gap between the consumer object and the human life it was sold to host."
  },
  a988: { // Nicola Tyson
    message: "Tyson was born in London in 1960 and has lived in New York since 1989; she helped found the influential Trial Balloon women's project space in early-1990s downtown New York. Her paintings — Self-Portrait (1996), Dancing Figure (2010) — depict distorted, slumped, swollen, or proboscis-like female figures in flat unmodulated colour that owes equally to Philip Guston and to a private cartoonish vocabulary. The argument is that the female body, in painting, need not be either beautiful or critically deconstructed — that it can be ungainly, comic, and psychologically charged on its own terms."
  },
  a928: { // Nicole Wittenberg
    message: "Wittenberg was born in New York in 1979, trained at the New York Studio School and the Royal Academy in London, and lives between Brooklyn and Maine. Her landscape and figurative pastels — Maine Coast (2020) and ongoing nude series — channel Fauvist colour and the gestural intimacy of pre-1914 European painting into a contemporary American practice that openly embraces decorative pleasure. The argument is that the Bonnard-Vuillard line is not closed history — that contemporary American painting can still inhabit Fauvist colour without irony, provided it has earned the right through observational rigour."
  },
  a233: { // Nigel Cooke
    message: "Cooke was born in Manchester, England in 1973 and lives in rural Kent; he trained at Nottingham Trent and the Royal College of Art before completing a PhD at Goldsmiths. His paintings — Black Mimosa (2009), Spring in Fialta (2018, after Nabokov) — combine landscape, figuration, and literary reference in densely layered surfaces that move from photorealism to abstract gesture within a single canvas. The argument is that contemporary British painting need not choose between figuration and abstraction, between landscape and conceptual armature — that the single painting can hold all of them at once if the painter is patient enough to layer."
  },
  a308: { // Oldenburg / van Bruggen
    message: "Claes Oldenburg (born Stockholm 1929, d. 2022) and Coosje van Bruggen (born Groningen 1942, d. 2009) collaborated as a single artistic body from 1976 until her death; he had previously been a central figure of American Pop, and she was an art historian and curator. Their joint monumental public sculptures — Spoonbridge and Cherry (1985, Walker Art Center), Cupid's Span (2002, San Francisco), the dozens of giant clothespins, badminton birdies, and trowel forms placed in plazas worldwide — argue that the public-art commission is best taken as joint authorship rather than as the heroic single sculptor's gesture. The estate-managed continuation insists that the monumental object can be both consumer-cliché and tender — that the absurd scale is a love-letter."
  },
  a899: { // Olivier Mosset
    message: "Mosset was born in Bern, Switzerland in 1944 and lives in Tucson, Arizona, where he has worked for the last three decades; he co-founded the BMPT group in 1966 in Paris with Daniel Buren, Michel Parmentier, and Niele Toroni. His paintings from that period reduced painting to a single Black Circle (1966) repeated identically across years, and his subsequent work has continued to interrogate authorship through monochromes, copies, and serial geometric abstraction made by him or by his assistants. The argument is that the painter's individual touch — the so-called signature — is an ideological construction, and that the painting's authority comes from elsewhere: from the decision, the protocol, the rule."
  },
  a822: { // Ornaghi & Prestinari
    message: "Valentina Ornaghi and Claudio Prestinari were both born in 1986 in northern Italy and trained at Brera and at the Politecnico di Milano; they have collaborated since 2011 and live in Milan. Their sculptures — Tela (2015), Sutura (2017) — combine traditional Italian craft technique (stone-cutting, embroidery, ceramics) with contemporary minimalist composition, often built around small acts of repair, stitch, or material splice. The argument is that Italian craft tradition is still operative as a contemporary medium — that the visible mend or sutured seam is a serious sculptural form, and that two-person collaboration is the relation in which craft can survive an art market trained to celebrate the single signature."
  },
  a797: { // Osvaldo González
    message: "González was born in Sancti Spíritus, Cuba in 1979, lives in Havana, and trained at the Instituto Superior de Arte (ISA). His sculptures — Coliseo (2014), Lágrimas Negras (2016) — are made from adhesive tape, fishing line, and light, producing ghostly architectural and natural forms whose materiality almost disappears under exhibition lighting. The argument is that the Cuban Special Period taught a generation of artists that scarcity is a working condition, not a defect — and that an entire sculptural vocabulary can be built from packing tape if the alternative is silence."
  },
  a923: { // Paloma Varga Weisz
    message: "Varga Weisz was born in Mannheim, Germany in 1966 to a Hungarian-German family, trained in traditional woodcarving in Bavaria before studying at the Düsseldorf Kunstakademie under Gerhard Merz and Tony Cragg, and lives in Düsseldorf. Her carved-wood figures — Bumped Body (2002), Root of a Dream (2017) — evoke European folklore, Catholic saint sculpture, and the medieval workshop, but the bodies are subtly hybridised, swollen, or interrupted. The argument is that German woodcarving is not a closed historical medium — and that the uncanny female figure, in the lineage of the medieval Virgin and the dark fairy tale, is still its proper subject."
  },
  a320: { // Paolo Roversi
    message: "Roversi was born in Ravenna, Italy in 1947 and has lived and worked in Paris since 1973; he is one of the central fashion photographers of the late twentieth century, with long-standing collaborations at Vogue Italia, Comme des Garçons, Yohji Yamamoto, and Christian Dior. His large-format Polaroid portraits — Nudi (1999), Studio (2005), the long Natalia Vodianova series — work in slow exposures with dust-grey backgrounds and a painterly chiaroscuro that owes more to Velázquez than to fashion. The argument is that fashion photography is not a lower medium than fine art — that its working tradition, when treated with the slowness of large-format Polaroid, produces images on the same continuum as the Renaissance portrait."
  },
  a838: { // Pascale Marthine Tayou
    message: "Tayou was born in Yaoundé, Cameroon in 1966 and lives in Ghent, Belgium; he changed the spelling of his name to add the feminine '-e' as an early refusal of inherited identity categories. His riotous installations — the Plastic Bags series (2008 onward), Colonial Erection (2009), the Crystal Tree, Falling Houses — assemble cheap commodity objects from West African markets, Murano glass, ceramic, and salvaged building material into compositions about globalisation's circulation of bodies and goods. The argument is that the contemporary African artist's medium cannot be confined to a single material tradition — global trash is the global condition, and sculpture's task is to make its scale legible."
  },
  a994: { // Paul Anthony Harford
    message: "Harford was born in Southend-on-Sea, England in 1943 and worked there in obscurity until his death in 2016; he had a single solo gallery exhibition in his lifetime. His finely observed pencil and ink drawings depict pubs, post-war working-class streets, lone figures, and bedsit interiors with a precision that owes more to Edward Hopper and Eric Ravilious than to anyone working in the contemporary British art scene. The estate-managed argument is that the working-class British provincial draughtsman, working alone for decades without market relationship, has produced a body of work that the art-historical record was not equipped to register — and the gallery system is, slowly, learning how to."
  },
  a255: { // Paul Graham
    message: "Graham was born in Stafford, England in 1956 and lives in New York; he taught himself photography and emerged in the 1980s alongside Martin Parr as a central figure of British color documentary. His series — A1: The Great North Road (1981–82, the first British color documentary book), Beyond Caring (waiting rooms in the Thatcher-era social-security system), and a shimmer of possibility (2004, twelve volumes of small American street observations) — refused the black-and-white humanist tradition. The argument — which won him the Hasselblad Award in 2012 — is that color photography is not a softer documentary medium but a more honest one, because it does not pretend that the world is morally legible at a glance."
  },
  a337: { // Paul Thek
    message: "Thek was born in Brooklyn in 1933, died of AIDS in 1988, and lived between New York and Europe; his Technological Reliquaries (1964) — wax casts of meat in minimalist plexiglas vitrines — confronted the cool surface of 1960s American minimalism with raw human flesh-quality. His later immersive installations — The Tomb (1967), the long Processions series of the 1970s — used candle, drift, and procession to make works that prefigured installation art before that category had been institutionalised. The estate-managed argument is that the American 1960s avant-garde had a hidden second wing — sacred, queer, mortal — and that Thek's reinstatement as a central figure of the period is still incomplete."
  },
  a913: { // Peter Schuyff
    message: "Schuyff was born in Baarn, Netherlands in 1958, moved to Vancouver and then to New York, and is loosely associated with the Neo-Geo generation of the early 1980s alongside Peter Halley, Sherrie Levine, and Jeff Koons. His paintings — the Untitled hard-edge geometric abstractions of 1986 onward — replicate Op-art-derived patterns with a cool, hand-painted precision that uses the language of Vasarely and Bridget Riley as already-historical material. The argument is that the 1980s painter could no longer make a 'new' abstraction, only quote a previous one — and that the deliberately deadpan re-painting of Op art is a position about painting's historical condition, not a nostalgia for it."
  },
  a910: { // Pietro Roccasalva
    message: "Roccasalva was born in Modica, Sicily in 1970 and lives in Milan; he trained at the Accademia di Belle Arti di Brera and emerged in the early 2000s with a multi-media practice that operates as a single ongoing allegorical drama. His paintings, sculptures, drawings, and tableaux — Il Traviatore (2010) — recur to a small set of figures (the juicer, the harlequin, the singer) layered with operatic, religious, and Sicilian-Baroque references, in compositions that read as both painting and stage design. The argument is that the Sicilian-Baroque visual tradition is still operative — and that the contemporary artist who returns to allegory and theatre is not retreating but advancing into a tradition that conceptual art's twentieth century had quietly suspended."
  },
  a920: { // Piotr Uklański
    message: "Uklański was born in Warsaw in 1968, lives in New York and Warsaw, and trained at the Cooper Union after emigrating from communist Poland. His Nazis (1998) installed 164 publicity photographs of actors playing Nazis in films — Brando, Olivier, Bowie, Caine — as a single immersive grid; his Dance Floor (1996) installed a colour-flashing illuminated nightclub floor in galleries from Gavin Brown to the Whitney. The argument is that popular culture's dirty visual archive — Nazis-played-by-handsome-actors, nightclub flooring, soft-core pin-up calendars — is not a lower register for art to slum in but a central one, where contemporary politics actually circulates."
  },
  a82: { // Rashid Johnson
    message: "Johnson was born in Chicago in 1977, trained at Columbia College Chicago and the School of the Art Institute, and lives in New York. His painting, sculpture, film, writing, and performance practice — the Anxious Men (2015) painted-soap heads, The Hikers (2019, a feature-length film), the long Cosmic Slop tar-and-shea-butter painting series — works through a stable vocabulary of Black domestic, intellectual, and bodily reference (soap, shea butter, plant, mirror, books) layered with deliberate emotional intensity. The argument is that the American Black male interior life has been chronically underrepresented as a painterly subject — and that the painting can render anxiety, grief, and inherited cultural weight at the same scale as historical religious painting."
  },
  a202: { // Raymond Saunders
    message: "Saunders was born in Pittsburgh in 1934, lives in Oakland, California, and is now (in his nineties) finally recognised as one of the central American postwar painters; he taught for decades at California College of the Arts. His blackboard-like paintings — Marie's Bill (1970), Jack Johnson (1972) — layer painted figures, chalk-drawn text, photo-transfer, and pinned ephemera onto matte black grounds, building dense fields of vernacular Black American visual material. His 1967 essay 'Black Is a Color' refused both Greenbergian abstraction's politics-blindness and the Black Arts Movement's demand for political legibility — and the work, sustained over fifty years, argues that Black painting need not choose between the two."
  },
  a276: { // Richard Learoyd
    message: "Learoyd was born in Nelson, Lancashire in 1966, lives in London, and works inside a single room-sized camera obscura he has built into his studio — exposures are taken on Ilfochrome positive paper directly through the camera's lens, with no intermediate negative. His portraits and still lifes — Agnes (2008), Man with Octopus Tattoo (2011) — produce a single physical print per sitting, unique and at the scale of the sitter's body. The argument is that the photograph as multiple, infinitely reproducible image is a historical accident of the medium — and that the singular print, made through a Victorian apparatus, is photography's other available tradition."
  },
  a211: { // Rirkrit Tiravanija
    message: "Tiravanija was born in Buenos Aires in 1961 to Thai diplomatic parents, grew up between Thailand, Ethiopia, and Canada, and lives between New York, Berlin, and Chiang Mai. His Untitled (Pad Thai) (1990) and Untitled (Free) (1992) at 303 Gallery transformed the gallery into a Thai kitchen in which he cooked and served free meals to visitors — a foundational work of what Nicolas Bourriaud later codified as relational aesthetics. His Land Foundation in Chiang Mai (founded 1998 with Kamin Lertchaiprasert) extends the practice into a working agricultural-and-art community. The argument is that hospitality is a serious sculptural medium — and that the meal shared with the stranger is contemporary art's most under-credited form."
  },
  a94: { // Rita Ackermann
    message: "Ackermann was born in Budapest in 1968, arrived in New York in 1992, and has worked there since. Her semi-figurative paintings — Fire by Days (2010), the Mama series (2017 onward) — work through a process of building up female figures in compressed-charcoal, oil, and pastel, then deliberately erasing, scraping, and washing the surface so that figure and ground become indistinguishable. The argument is that the female figure in painting is most truthful when partly erased — that the cleanly rendered woman is a lie about how women are actually perceived, and that the painting's surface needs to register that mid-perceptual incompleteness."
  },
  a867: { // Roland Flexner
    message: "Flexner was born in Nice, France in 1944 and has lived in New York since 1990. His best-known works are the Liquid Drawings (2009 onward), produced by blowing soap bubbles loaded with ink onto small sheets of paper and arresting the bubble at the moment its surface tension records a brief landscape, vortex, or galactic pattern. The argument is that drawing's contemporary subject can be the ephemeral physical event itself — that an artist whose entire output consists of capturing brief surface-tension accidents is not gimmicky but disciplined: each drawing is unrepeatable, and that unrepeatability is the work."
  },
  a820: { // Rudi Ninov
    message: "Ninov was born in Sofia, Bulgaria in 1991 and lives between Sofia and Vienna; he trained at the National Academy of Art in Sofia and at the Academy of Fine Arts Vienna. His paintings and works on paper — Untitled (2021) — examine language, abstraction, and the gesture of writing as primary subject, working with text-fragments, written marks, and the abstract field as if they were the same operation. The argument is that the painter's mark and the writer's letter are the same gesture historically separated by institutional categories — and that contemporary Eastern European abstraction can productively dissolve that boundary."
  },
  a250: { // Sam Gilliam
    message: "Gilliam was born in Tupelo, Mississippi in 1933, lived in Washington DC for over fifty years, and died in 2022; he was the first Black American artist to represent the United States at the Venice Biennale, in 1972. His Drape Paintings (1968 onward) and beveled-edge canvases stained colour into raw cotton and then suspended the canvas off the stretcher, draped across walls, ceilings, and rooms, in a move that — three years before Olitski or Frankenthaler — extended Color Field painting into three dimensions. The estate-managed argument is that the canonical narrative of American Color Field abstraction has the chronology and the authorship wrong, and that Gilliam's draped canvas was the medium's most consequential single innovation."
  },
  a181: { // Sarah Michelson
    message: "Michelson was born in Manchester, England in 1964 and lives in New York; she trained as a dancer at the London Contemporary Dance School and crossed into the gallery and Whitney circuit in the early 2000s. Her site-specific dance pieces — Devotion Study #1 (2012 Whitney Biennial, for which she received the Bucksbaum Award), Dover Beach (2009) — work with extreme physical endurance, intricate floor diagrams painted directly onto the institution's surfaces, and exhausting duration. The argument is that contemporary dance in the gallery context is not theatre's poor cousin — and that the dancer's body, sustained at the threshold of collapse, is a sculptural condition the museum has only recently learned how to host."
  },
  a167: { // Scott Kahn
    message: "Kahn was born in Trenton, New Jersey in 1946, trained at the University of Pennsylvania and at the Indiana University MFA, and lived for decades in relative obscurity in New York and Lyme, Connecticut before his late-career re-discovery in the 2020s. His paintings — My House at Night (1995), The Dream (1989) — render meticulous, dreamlike landscapes, interiors, and autobiographical scenes in a slow folk-modernist register that recalls Florine Stettheimer and Henri Rousseau more than any post-1960 American painter. The argument is that the art-historical record has a late-discovery problem — that work made carefully and consistently over forty years is not less serious for having been ignored, and that the slow domestic painter is a real contemporary category."
  },
  a648: { // Sean Scully
    message: "Scully was born in Dublin in 1945, raised in working-class south London, and lives between New York, Munich, and Tappan; he trained at Croydon College of Art and Newcastle. His stripe paintings — initially hard-edged minimalist grids in the 1970s, then increasingly painterly horizontal-and-vertical bands from the mid-1980s onward, culminating in the long Wall of Light series — sit between European geometric abstraction (Mondrian, Klee) and American post-Rothko colour-field painting. The argument is that the stripe is not an exhausted modernist motif but a flexible vessel for emotional content — provided the painter is willing to admit that emotion, light, and weight are still valid painterly subjects."
  },
  a830: { // Serse
    message: "Serse Roma was born in San Polo di Piave, Italy in 1952 and lives in Trieste; he works exclusively in graphite on paper at sizes that approach painting. His hyperrealist drawings — Acque (2000 onward), Paesaggio (2008) — depict water, fog, rock, and sky at near-photographic precision, but the medium (pencil) is so fundamentally insufficient to the subject (light, wetness) that the gap between rendering and reality becomes the actual content. The argument is that hyperrealism's contemporary value lies in choosing the wrong medium for the right subject — graphite can never become water, and the visible failure of the attempt is what the drawing is about."
  },
  a976: { // Seth Price
    message: "Price was born in East Jerusalem in 1973 to American parents and lives in New York; he trained at Brown and is also a noted writer and theorist. His artist's essay Dispersion (2002) — written before social media — anticipated the gallery's loss of authority over how images circulate; his Vintage Bomber (2006) sculptural series, video, painting, and fashion-line collaborations test where the work's edge actually is. The argument is that contemporary art's hidden medium is distribution — and that the artist's task in a post-2000 networked condition is to make work that already knows how it will be reproduced, screen-captured, and re-sold, and that uses that anticipation as a formal device."
  },
  a857: { // Shannon Cartier Lucy
    message: "Cartier Lucy was born in Nashville, Tennessee in 1977 and lives there; she trained at NYU's Tisch School and worked in New York before moving back to Tennessee. Her figurative paintings — The Strawberry (2019), Woman with Cake (2020) — depict women in domestic interiors performing small absurd or unsettled acts (squeezing a strawberry against a wall, sitting before a cake) in a flat narrative register that recalls Eric Fischl and Balthus without their voyeurism. The argument is that the women-alone-in-a-room subject is not a closed historical category — that the contemporary woman's private moments, when painted without explanation, are themselves the painting's psychological content."
  },
  a948: { // Shannon Ebner
    message: "Ebner was born in Englewood, New Jersey in 1971 and lives in Los Angeles; she trained at Bard MFA. Her photographs — The Electric Comma (2009), A Public Character (2012), STRIKE — photograph letters, signs, and language fragments in the American landscape as if they were sculptural form, then construct them into longer poetic sequences. The argument is that contemporary photography's most under-explored subject is the written word as physical object — that the highway sign, the storefront letter, and the dropped roadside placard constitute an American vernacular sculpture that photography is uniquely positioned to read."
  },
  a974: { // Simon Periton
    message: "Periton was born in Kent, England in 1964, trained at Saint Martin's School of Art, and lives in London; he was one of the artists associated with the Cabinet gallery generation of mid-1990s London. His intricate cut-paper doilies — Anarchy Doily (1996) substitutes anarchist symbols, skulls, and political graffiti for the traditional doily's floral motif — and his later Black Iris (2008) cut-metal sculptures translate the doily's lacework into permanent architectural scale. The argument is that the decorative paper doily — pure feminine craft, dismissed by every modernism — is in fact a vessel of considerable formal sophistication, and that smuggling punk content inside it is a critique of both ornamental tradition and anti-ornamental modernism."
  },
  a860: { // Skyler Chen
    message: "Chen was born in Tainan, Taiwan in 1991 and lives in New York; he trained at the School of Visual Arts. His dreamlike figurative paintings — Bathers (2022) — depict queer Asian-diasporic figures in lush interiors, bathhouses, and natural settings rendered in saturated colour with a delicacy that recalls early Italian fresco. The argument is that contemporary queer Asian-diasporic painting need not stage trauma as its constitutive subject — that pleasure, memory, and the bathhouse as classical genre scene are sufficient and seriously available."
  },
  a893: { // Spencer Lewis
    message: "Lewis was born in Hartford, Connecticut in 1979, trained at the University of Hartford and Yale MFA, and lives in Los Angeles. His paintings on jute — the long Untitled jute series (2018 onward) — combine aggressive gestural mark-making (acrylic, ink, enamel, spray) with collage-like compositional structure on raw rough textile, in a register that returns deliberately to the post-Abstract-Expressionist American painting moment. The argument is that the raw, unsized jute support is not an aesthetic affectation but a working condition — its tooth, weight, and aggressive surface refuse the painter's options in ways that produce truer paintings than cotton ever can."
  },
  a862: { // Steven Claydon
    message: "Claydon was born in London in 1969, lives there, and trained at the Royal College of Art; he has worked as both an artist and a curator since the early 2000s. His sculptures and installations — Analogues, Methods, Hosts and Mascots (2014) — examine how museum display, ethnographic vitrine, and modernist cabinet have framed objects into the meaning categories the institution required them to occupy. The argument is that the contemporary sculpture is not a discrete object but a display proposition — and that the artist's medium includes the case, the pedestal, the wall-label, and the lighting decision that the museum normally treats as neutral."
  },
  a990: { // TJ Wilcox
    message: "Wilcox was born in Seattle in 1965, trained at the School of Visual Arts and CalArts, and lives in New York. His film collages — Garlands (2005), In the Air (2013, a 360-degree panoramic video installation of a single day's sky over Union Square) — splice archival footage of figures including Marie Antoinette, Stephen Tennant, and Garbo with personal narration and 16mm-feeling colour treatments. The argument is that the contemporary artist's archive is now indistinguishable from the personal photo album — and that the film essay, which the museum has been slow to host, is the medium for that dissolved boundary."
  },
  a877: { // Thomas Grünfeld
    message: "Grünfeld was born in Opladen, Germany in 1956 and lives in Cologne; he trained at the Hochschule für bildende Künste Hamburg under Sigmar Polke alongside Georg Herold and Albert Oehlen. His Misfits series (1990 onward) commissions traditional taxidermists to combine the bodies of different animals — a St Bernard's head onto a sheep's body, a peacock onto a pig — into hybrid creatures that look entirely convincing as natural specimens. The argument is that the taxidermy display, the museum's quietest form, has been hiding a deeply uncomfortable epistemology — and that the obvious fake animal is more honest about what the natural history museum has always been doing."
  },
  a306: { // Thomas Nozkowski
    message: "Nozkowski was born in Teaneck, New Jersey in 1944 and died in 2019; he trained at Cooper Union and lived for most of his career in the Hudson Valley and the New York downtown scene. His small canvases — typically 16 by 20 inches, all titled Untitled with a numbered identifier — were built slowly through scraping, overpainting, and revision, with each work drawing on a specific personal memory, observation, or art-historical reference that he refused to disclose. The argument is that the intimate-scale abstract painting is not a lesser format than the large gestural canvas — that the small painting, built through patient memory work, is one of the great American post-war painterly traditions still being underwritten."
  },
  a827: { // Tobias Rehberger
    message: "Rehberger was born in Esslingen, Germany in 1966, trained at the Städelschule under Thomas Bayrle and Martin Kippenberger, and is now a professor there. His practice — the Venice Biennale cafeteria (2009, for which he received the Golden Lion for Was du liebst, bringt dich auch zum Weinen), 81 Years (2002), dazzle-painted interiors — works at the deliberately ambiguous seam of art, design, and architecture, producing functional environments that operate as immersive sculpture. The argument is that the design-versus-art boundary is an art-historical convenience — and that the most useful contemporary sculptures are also chairs, lamps, cafés, or façades."
  },
  a444: { // Tony Cragg
    message: "Cragg was born in Liverpool in 1949, trained at the Royal College of Art in London, and has lived in Wuppertal, Germany since 1977, where he served as rector of the Kunstakademie Düsseldorf from 2009 to 2013. His early works — Stack (1975, layered domestic debris), Britain Seen from the North (1981, a wall map made of found plastic) — were assemblages of urban-industrial waste; his later computer-modeled bronze and wood forms (Rational Beings, Early Forms) are smooth swept-surface volumes that emerge from layered cross-sections. The argument, sustained over fifty years, is that contemporary sculpture's medium is material science — and that the British tradition of object-as-subject (from Henry Moore through Cragg) can be extended through computation without losing its physical seriousness."
  },
  a265: { // Virginia Jaramillo
    message: "Jaramillo was born in El Paso, Texas in 1939 to Mexican parents, lived in Los Angeles in the 1960s where she was part of the African-American Watts Tower visual community, and has lived in upstate New York since 1971. Her hard-edge and curvilinear abstractions — Green Dawn (1971), the long Curvilinear Paintings of the 1970s, and the recent linen-paper-and-pigment works — were largely written out of the American Color Field canon until her inclusion in the 2017 Tate Modern exhibition Soul of a Nation. The argument is that the canonical narrative of post-war American abstraction systematically excluded Latina and Black women practitioners — and that the corrective is not symbolic inclusion but a re-reading of the period itself, work by work."
  },

  // ── Chunk 5 (50-batch, includes red-chip — knowledge cards only) ──

  a21: { // Walton Ford
    message: "Ford was born in Larchmont, NY in 1960 and lives in Great Barrington, Massachusetts; he trained at RISD. His monumental watercolours — Falling Bough (2002), Sensations of an Infant Heart (1999) — adopt the format and natural-history precision of Audubon's Birds of America but load each animal scene with colonial allegory, environmental ruin, and political violence. The argument is that the nineteenth-century natural-history illustration — a foundational visual technology of empire — is still a legible vehicle for the present, provided the painter is willing to inhabit its full ambivalence."
  },
  a932: { // Wang Yuyang
    message: "Wang Yuyang was born in Harbin, China in 1979, trained at the Central Academy of Fine Arts, and lives in Beijing. His Artificial Moon (2007) is a glowing globe of energy-saving fluorescent bulbs the size of a small house; his data-driven LED sculptures convert stock-market values, weather, and astronomical readings into light. The argument is that Chinese contemporary sculpture's most under-credited material is light — and that the science-engineering register, in which the artist works as data-translator, is the form most legible to the Beijing technocratic generation."
  },
  a993: { // William N. Copley
    message: "Copley (signing CPLY) was born in New York in 1919, was adopted into a wealthy newspaper family, lost his parents young, and ran a short-lived Beverly Hills gallery in 1948 that hosted Magritte, Cornell, Tanguy, and Man Ray before any American institution would. His own cartoonish, erotic paintings — Think (1962), Imaginary Flag for U.S.A. (1972) — bridged late Surrealism and Pop without belonging cleanly to either. The estate argument is that the missing-link figure between European Surrealism and American Pop is not a footnote — it is a real bridge that the canonical narrative simplified."
  },
  a349: { // Xiao Yu
    message: "Xiao Yu was born in Inner Mongolia in 1965, trained at the Central Academy of Fine Arts in Beijing, and lives there. His installations — Ruan (1999, in which a human-rabbit hybrid fetus was preserved in formaldehyde, generating significant controversy), Ground (2012) — work at the seam of contemporary Chinese conceptual art and bioethical provocation. The argument, sustained across thirty years, is that Chinese contemporary art's confrontation with state ideology has been most direct when it took up the materials of biology, ritual, and the body's actual condition — not when it took up abstraction."
  },
  a925: { // Xiyao Wang
    message: "Xiyao Wang was born in Chongqing in 1992, trained at the Sichuan Fine Arts Institute and the Hochschule für bildende Künste Hamburg, and lives in Berlin. Her large gestural abstractions — Cosmic Dance (2022) — combine Chinese calligraphic line, modern dance choreography (which she studied alongside painting), and post-Twombly gestural-canvas tradition in compositions built at the scale of the painter's own body. The argument is that calligraphy and dance share a common medium — the body's arc in space — and that contemporary abstraction's most overlooked source is the choreographic gesture rather than the painted mark."
  },
  a912: { // Xue Ruozhe
    message: "Xue Ruozhe was born in Yantai, Shandong province in 1989, trained at the Central Academy of Fine Arts Beijing, and lives between Beijing and Berlin. His figurative paintings — the ongoing untitled series — combine classical European technique (Vermeer-influenced light, Caravaggio compositional staging) with contemporary Chinese subject matter in carefully constructed theatrical compositions. The argument is that the Chinese painter trained classically can take the European Old Master tradition as fully available technique — not as cultural appropriation but as the painter's right to inherited craft."
  },
  a907: { // Yan Ping
    message: "Yan Ping was born in Shandong, China in 1956, trained at the Shandong Art Institute, and lives in Beijing. Her expressive figurative paintings — the long Mother and Son series (2000 onward) — depict family scenes, motherhood, and domestic intimacy with gestural brushwork that draws on both ink painting tradition and post-impressionist colour. The argument is that motherhood, a subject systematically minor-ed by the twentieth-century Chinese avant-garde, can be central to a serious painterly practice — and that gestural figurative painting is the right medium for it."
  },
  a931: { // Yeesookyung
    message: "Yeesookyung was born in Seoul in 1963 and lives there. Her Translated Vase series (2007 onward) reassembles broken shards of failed traditional Korean celadon, blue-and-white, and moon-jar ceramics — pieces destroyed by their original Living National Treasure makers as flawed — into baroque hybrid forms whose gold-leaf seams follow Japanese kintsugi tradition. The argument is that what one tradition calls failure, another tradition calls the most beautiful part — and that the assembled fragment carries cross-cultural meaning the perfect original could not."
  },
  a352: { // Yoo Youngkuk
    message: "Yoo Youngkuk was born in Uljin, North Gyeongsang Province in 1916, studied at the Bunka Gakuin in Tokyo in the 1930s, and lived through the Japanese occupation and the Korean War before his death in 2002; he founded the New Realism Group in Seoul in 1947. His vibrant geometric paintings — Mountain (1970), Work (1974) — abstract Korean mountain landscape into hard-edged colour fields that operate equally as flag, peak, and pure formal proposition. The estate-managed argument is that Korean modernism's foundational figure has been systematically under-credited in international histories of postwar abstraction — and that the mountain, his lifelong subject, is the proper image of twentieth-century Korean visual modernity."
  },
  a956: { // Yu Ji
    message: "Yu Ji was born in Shanghai in 1985, trained at the Shanghai University Fine Arts School, and lives in Shanghai. Her Flesh in Stone series (2013 onward) casts fragments of female bodies — torsos, limbs, breasts — in raw concrete; her performance and video work Forager (2021) traces undocumented women's labour in Shanghai. The argument is that contemporary Chinese sculpture's most urgent subject is the unaccounted labouring female body of the post-1990s rural-to-urban migration — and that concrete, the material of that migration's building boom, is its proper medium."
  },
  a500: { // Álvaro Urbano
    message: "Urbano was born in Madrid in 1983 and lives in Berlin; he trained as an architect at the ETSAM in Madrid before crossing into installation art. His theatrical environments — TUTTOLEALTRO (2019), The Awakening (2022), A Memorial (Forever) (2023) — reanimate forgotten buildings (Lina Bo Bardi's MASP, the Eames House) with hyperrealist botanical sculpture and ambient soundtracks that imply a queer narrative just out of frame. The argument is that architectural history is full of erased queer narratives — and that the artist's task is to re-stage the missing scene with a precision that makes its absence newly legible."
  },
  a249: { // Adrian Ghenie
    message: "Ghenie was born in Baia Mare, Romania in 1977 and trained at the University of Art and Design Cluj-Napoca; he was a co-founding figure of the Cluj School and the Fabrica de Pensule artist-run collective. His paintings — Pie Fight Interior (2012), The Sunflowers in 1937 (2014), the long Darwin and Hitler series — work through twentieth-century atrocity by means of palette-knife collage, photo reference, and a deliberately fractured pictorial surface that recalls Bacon, Polke, and Twombly simultaneously. The argument is that the figurative painter inherits the visual archive of European twentieth-century catastrophe and cannot pretend otherwise — that the painted surface must register the fracture rather than smooth it."
  },
  a292: { // Agnes Martin
    message: "Martin was born in Macklin, Saskatchewan in 1912, taught herself art in New Mexico, lived for many years in Coenties Slip on the East River (alongside Indiana, Kelly, Youngerman, Rauschenberg, Johns), and returned to New Mexico in 1967 where she lived until her death in 2004. Her pencil-grid canvases — typically six by six feet, hand-ruled in graphite over washes of acrylic — work in a register that takes Buddhist meditation, Zen, and Plotinus equally seriously as formal sources. The estate-managed argument is that abstraction can be devotional without being religious, and that the hand-ruled line, in its visible imperfection, is the painting's evidence that a body made it."
  },
  a593: { // Ai Weiwei
    message: "Ai Weiwei was born in Beijing in 1957 to the exiled poet Ai Qing, lived through the family's internal exile to Xinjiang, lived in New York in the 1980s, and now lives between Cambridge, UK and Berlin after years of conflict with the Chinese state including a 2011 detention. His projects — Sunflower Seeds (2010, 100 million hand-painted porcelain seeds at Tate Modern), Remembering (2009, the names of children killed in the Sichuan earthquake), Fairytale (2007, 1,001 Chinese visitors to Documenta) — operate across architecture, sculpture, social media, and direct political confrontation. The argument is that the contemporary artist's medium is no longer confined to the gallery — and that human-rights documentation, building, and large-scale collective action are inseparable from the studio practice."
  },
  a14: { // Albert Oehlen
    message: "Oehlen was born in Krefeld, Germany in 1954, trained at the Hochschule für bildende Künste Hamburg, and is a central figure of the post-1980s German painting generation alongside Martin Kippenberger (his closest friend until Kippenberger's death in 1997). His paintings — Untitled (Tree) (2014), the Computer Paintings (1992 onward), the post-non-figurative series — deliberately make abstract paintings that look bad in the most precise way: garish, mis-composed, over-worked, refusing the consolations of taste. The argument is that the painter's task after Polke and Richter is to undermine painting from inside the medium rather than to abandon it — and that the deliberately ugly painting is a serious aesthetic position."
  },
  a690: { // Alex Katz
    message: "Katz was born in Brooklyn in 1927 and has lived in New York and Maine for over seventy years; he trained at Cooper Union and the Skowhegan School. His flat, billboard-scale portraits of his wife Ada (painted continuously since 1957), his New York poet-and-artist social circle (Frank O'Hara, John Ashbery, Kenneth Koch, the Tibor de Nagy gallery generation), and Maine landscape — Blue Umbrella 2 (1972), Black Brook (1990) — anticipated Pop Art's flatness and refused its irony. The argument is that the painter's lifelong social circle is a legitimate subject — and that the flat, hard-edged colour portrait, painted from preparatory cutouts at architectural scale, is American figuration's quietest century-long innovation."
  },
  a43: { // Alexander Calder
    message: "Calder was born in Lawnton, Pennsylvania in 1898 to a family of sculptors, trained as a mechanical engineer at Stevens Institute, and worked in Paris and Roxbury, Connecticut until his death in 1976; Marcel Duchamp gave him the word 'mobile' in 1931. His hanging mobiles — Lobster Trap and Fish Tail (1939, MoMA stairwell), the long Constellations series, the late large outdoor stabiles (Flamingo in Chicago, La Grande Vitesse in Grand Rapids) — invented kinetic sculpture as we now understand it. The estate-managed argument is that the mobile is not merely a sculpture that moves — it is a redefinition of what sculpture's relationship to gravity, air current, and time can be."
  },
  a297: { // Amedeo Modigliani
    message: "Modigliani was born in Livorno, Italy in 1884 to a Sephardic Jewish family, moved to Paris in 1906, and died there of tubercular meningitis in 1920 at thirty-five; his common-law wife Jeanne Hébuterne killed herself the day after, while nine months pregnant. His paintings — Reclining Nude (1917), Jeanne Hébuterne (1919) — fuse the elongated necks of African and Khmer sculpture (which he saw in the Trocadéro), Sienese primitive painting, and the Parisian School of Paris milieu around Soutine, Chaim, and Brancusi. The estate-managed argument is that the early-twentieth-century European modernist canon was almost entirely shaped by figures like Modigliani — and that the brief, intense, doomed life is itself an art-historical category the museum still struggles to handle."
  },
  a9: { // Andreas Gursky
    message: "Gursky was born in Leipzig in 1955 and trained at the Kunstakademie Düsseldorf under Bernd and Hilla Becher — the legendary documentary photographers — alongside Thomas Struth, Candida Höfer, and Thomas Ruff in what became known as the Düsseldorf School. His large-format digitally composited photographs — Rhein II (1999, which set the auction record for a photograph at $4.3 million), 99 Cent (1999, a discount supermarket interior), Chicago Board of Trade III (1999), Bahrain I (2005) — document the visual architecture of global capitalism at a scale that requires the viewer to stand back six metres. The argument is that the long-distance image of the global economy is itself the contemporary sublime — and that digital compositing, far from being a betrayal of photography's truth-claim, is the medium's adequate response to a world that exceeds any single camera position."
  },
  a45: { // Andy Warhol
    message: "Warhol was born Andrew Warhola in Pittsburgh in 1928 to Carpatho-Rusyn immigrants, trained as a commercial illustrator at Carnegie Tech, and worked in New York until his death in 1987 from a routine gallbladder surgery. His silkscreen paintings — Marilyn Diptych (1962), Campbell's Soup Cans (1962, 32 canvases, one for each variety), the Death and Disaster series, the Mao portraits — and his Factory studio system, his films, his magazine Interview, his Velvet Underground patronage, and the deliberately constructed silver-wig persona, together built the foundational template for the contemporary artist as brand. The estate-managed argument is that Warhol's actual innovation was not the silkscreen but the studio-as-corporation — and that every subsequent artist who operates as a brand is working inside a system Warhol invented."
  },
  a626: { // Anish Kapoor
    message: "Kapoor was born in Mumbai in 1954 to a Punjabi Hindu father and an Iraqi Jewish mother, moved to London in 1973, and trained at Hornsey College of Art and Chelsea School of Art. His pigment-saturated early sculptures — 1000 Names (1979–80, raw pigment heaps), Marsyas (2002, Tate Modern Turbine Hall, a vast PVC trumpet), Cloud Gate (2004–06, the Chicago Millennium Park bean), My Red Homeland (2003, a wax-and-Vaseline horizontal column) — work with concave voids, pure pigment, polished steel, and engineered concave surfaces that absorb or invert the viewer's image. The argument is that public sculpture's responsibility, in the early twenty-first century, is to host the body's perceptual transformation at architectural scale — and that the void, polished to perfect concavity, is the most efficient way to do it."
  },
  a53: { // Anna Weyant
    message: "Weyant was born in Calgary in 1995, trained at the Rhode Island School of Design (graduating in 2017), and lives in New York. Her paintings — Falling Woman (2020), Buffet (2022) — render young women, still lifes, and uncanny domestic scenes in a slow Old-Master figurative technique that draws on the Dutch Golden Age (Frans Hals, Judith Leyster) and on Norman Rockwell's flat narrative humour. The argument is that the late-twentieth-century rejection of figurative virtuosity as kitsch was overstated — that a 21st-century painter can use the chiaroscuro and varnish of seventeenth-century Dutch painting as a contemporary visual technology, especially for subjects (anxious women, gleaming dinner-tables) the original tradition could not directly name."
  },
  a5: { // Anselm Kiefer
    message: "Kiefer was born in Donaueschingen, Germany in 1945 — eight days before the war ended — and trained at the Karlsruhe Academy with Peter Dreher and at the Düsseldorf Academy under Joseph Beuys. His monumental paintings — Marchlands (1974), Sulamith (1983, after Paul Celan's Death Fugue), Breaking of the Vessels (1990, a 7.5-ton lead-book sculpture) — work through Germany's Nazi past, Kabbalistic mysticism, alchemy, the German Romantic landscape tradition, and the materials of catastrophe (lead, ash, straw, soil) at a scale where the canvas itself becomes a building. The argument is that the German painter born at the war's end cannot inherit any of the available visual traditions cleanly — and that the painter's task is to walk through the wreckage of national myth and pick up what can be made to speak."
  },
  a377: { // Antony Gormley
    message: "Gormley was born in London in 1950, trained at Trinity College Cambridge in archaeology and at the Slade in sculpture, and lives in London; he was elected to the Royal Academy in 2003. His sculptures — Angel of the North (1998, the 20-metre rusted-steel angel above the A1 in Gateshead), Another Place (1997, 100 cast-iron figures on Crosby Beach), Event Horizon (2007, 31 figures distributed across London rooftops) — are all cast directly from his own body, treated as the basic unit of architecture. The argument is that sculpture's question after minimalism is not how the object inhabits space but how the body, the irreducible unit, inhabits space — and that the body-as-mould, repeated in iron at scale across landscapes and cityscapes, is the medium for that question."
  },
  a258: { // Barbara Hepworth
    message: "Hepworth was born in Wakefield, Yorkshire in 1903, trained at the Royal College of Art (where she met Henry Moore), moved to St Ives, Cornwall in 1939 with her second husband Ben Nicholson and four children, and worked there until her death in 1975 in a studio fire. Her pierced abstract forms — Single Form (1961, the United Nations memorial to Dag Hammarskjöld), Pelagos (1946) — translated organic landscape, sea-light, and the body's interior cavity into stone, wood, and bronze that anticipated Henry Moore's later international fame while never receiving comparable institutional weight in her lifetime. The estate-managed argument is that twentieth-century British modernist sculpture's central figure was a woman with four children working through a fire — and that the gendered story of who got to be the postwar British sculptor needs to be re-told."
  },
  a171: { // Barbara Kruger
    message: "Kruger was born in Newark, NJ in 1945, trained at Syracuse University and Parsons under Diane Arbus and Marvin Israel, and worked for ten years as a magazine designer at Mademoiselle and House & Garden before turning to art. Her photographic-text works — I Shop Therefore I Am (1987), Your Body Is a Battleground (1989, made for the Women's March on Washington), Belief+Doubt (2012, a wraparound Hirshhorn installation) — use Futura Bold Oblique white-on-red captions over found black-and-white press photographs, in compositions that look like magazine ads. The argument is that the design language of mass media is itself the contemporary site of feminist critique — and that taking the typography seriously is taking the politics seriously."
  },
  a783: { // Cai Guo-Qiang
    message: "Cai Guo-Qiang was born in Quanzhou, Fujian Province in 1957, trained as a stage designer at the Shanghai Theatre Academy, lived in Japan from 1986 to 1995, and lives in New York. His gunpowder explosion events — Sky Ladder (2015, a 500-metre flaming ladder rising at dawn over his hometown), the Beijing 2008 Olympics opening ceremony fireworks (29 footprints of light crossing the city), the multi-city Bridge series — translate Chinese cosmological tradition, fengshui, and Taoist alchemy into spectacle-scale ephemeral sculpture. The argument is that the gunpowder explosion — the only material the artist's grandfather could afford to invent with in pre-1979 Fujian — is a medium of equal seriousness to oil paint, and that its inheritance from Chinese material history is sculpture's most under-credited modern lineage."
  },
  a16: { // Cecily Brown
    message: "Brown was born in London in 1969 to the art critic David Sylvester and the novelist Shena Mackay, trained at the Slade, and has lived in New York since 1994. Her paintings — Father of the Bride (1999), The Pyjama Game (1998), the long series after Bacon, Bosch, and Hogarth — work through dense semi-figurative compositions in which bodies, animals, and pictorial fragments dissolve in and out of legibility across heavily layered surfaces. The argument is that art-historical painting remains the contemporary painter's serious available subject — and that the body in painting, in the long Western tradition from Rubens through Bacon, has never resolved into stable form, and the contemporary painting must register that historical instability."
  },
  a576: { // Cindy Sherman
    message: "Sherman was born in Glen Ridge, NJ in 1954, trained at Buffalo State College, and has lived in New York since 1977. Her practice is one continuous project: appearing as the model in every photograph, with her own makeup, costume, and prosthetic work, across the Untitled Film Stills (1977–80, 69 black-and-white tableaux of imagined 1950s heroines), the History Portraits (1989–90, after Holbein, Caravaggio, Raphael), the Clowns (2003–04), and the recent flapper and society series. The argument is that the photographic image of woman in twentieth-century mass media is not a record of women but a constructed costume — and that the artist who inhabits every available cliché, one after another, can show the costume as the costume by being the only person in the room."
  },
  a478: { // Claes Oldenburg
    message: "Oldenburg was born in Stockholm in 1929 to a Swedish diplomatic family, grew up in Chicago, trained at Yale, and lived in New York until his death in 2022; he married the curator Coosje van Bruggen in 1977 and from 1976 they signed all monumental works as a duo. His early Store (1961, a Lower East Side storefront of plaster-and-enamel commercial goods) and Floor Burger (1962, a giant fabric hamburger sagging on the gallery floor) reframed Pop Art's relationship to scale, material, and the soft sculptural object. The estate-managed argument is that Pop Art's least understood innovation is not the silkscreen but the soft sculpture — and that the commercial object enlarged, softened, and treated with tender absurdity is American sculpture's most consequential mid-century invention."
  },
  a2: { // Cy Twombly
    message: "Twombly was born in Lexington, Virginia in 1928, trained at the Boston Museum School, Washington and Lee, the Art Students League, and at Black Mountain College (where his teachers were Robert Motherwell, Ben Shahn, and Charles Olson, and his classmates Rauschenberg, Cage, Cunningham, Bucky Fuller). He moved to Italy in 1957 and lived in Rome and Gaeta until his death in 2011. His scribbled, mythologically charged canvases — Leda and the Swan (1962), the long Bacchus series (2006–08), the Fifty Days at Iliam cycle — work through the European classical tradition (Ovid, Homer, Sappho) by means of a writing-like mark that is neither legible text nor purely abstract gesture. The estate-managed argument is that postwar American painting's missing third path — neither Pollock-Newman gestural nor Stella-Judd hard-edge — was a deliberate exile into European antiquity, and that this third path is the one still most generative for younger painters."
  },
  a1: { // Damien Hirst
    message: "Hirst was born in Bristol in 1965, raised in Leeds, and trained at Goldsmiths in the late 1980s where, as a student, he curated the 1988 warehouse exhibition Freeze that effectively founded the Young British Artists. His best-known works — The Physical Impossibility of Death in the Mind of Someone Living (1991, a tiger shark in formaldehyde, commissioned by Charles Saatchi), For the Love of God (2007, a £14m diamond-encrusted platinum skull), the long Spot Paintings (1986 onward, executed by assistants by the thousand), the Treasures from the Wreck of the Unbelievable (2017 Venice Pinault commission) — track a single forty-year argument. The argument is that contemporary art and the auction market are no longer separable spheres — and that the most honest artist in this condition is the one who builds the market valuation into the work's content rather than pretending it can be ignored."
  },
  a161: { // Dan Flavin
    message: "Flavin was born in Jamaica, Queens in 1933 to an Irish-Catholic family (his identical twin David died in 1962, an event that shadowed Flavin's mature work), trained briefly for the priesthood, served in the Air Force in Korea, and worked at MoMA and the Guggenheim as a mailroom clerk before his first solo exhibition in 1961. His sculptures — the diagonal of May 25, 1963 (his first single-fluorescent piece, dedicated to Constantin Brâncuși), monument 1 for V. Tatlin (1964, the first of a 39-work series), untitled (to Donna) (1971) — use commercial off-the-shelf eight-foot fluorescent tubes in ten standard colours and shapes that he never modifies. The estate-managed argument is that the sculptor's job after Duchamp is to make sculpture from the bureaucratic everyday — and that the off-the-shelf fluorescent tube, untouched, is the most honest sculptural unit available."
  },
  a773: { // Daniel Arsham
    message: "Arsham was born in Cleveland in 1980, grew up in Miami where he survived Hurricane Andrew at age 12 (an event he cites as foundational), trained at Cooper Union, and lives in New York. His Future Relics series (2013 onward) casts contemporary consumer objects — Polaroid cameras, basketballs, Pokémon, telephones, Ferrari engines — in crystallised, eroded materials (selenite, calcite, volcanic ash) as if they were archaeological artefacts found a thousand years in the future. The argument is that the present moment's most iconic consumer objects are already, today, archaeological — and that imagining their eroded form is more honest than treating them as new."
  },
  a259: { // David Hockney
    message: "Hockney was born in Bradford, Yorkshire in 1937, trained at the Bradford School of Art and the Royal College of Art, and has worked between Los Angeles, Yorkshire, and Normandy for sixty years. His paintings — A Bigger Splash (1967, the canonical LA swimming-pool painting), Mr and Mrs Clark and Percy (1970–71, the double portrait of Ossie Clark and Celia Birtwell), the photo-collage Joiners (1980s), the iPad drawings from the 2010s, the Normandy spring series — track six decades of restless reinvention across painting, drawing, photography, set design, and digital media. The argument is that the painter who lives long enough cannot be a single-style figure — that the sustained career is itself an argument about the inadequacy of any one period's available visual vocabulary."
  },
  a144: { // Diane Arbus
    message: "Arbus was born Diane Nemerov in New York in 1923 to a wealthy Russian-Jewish family (her brother was the poet Howard Nemerov), trained under Lisette Model after a long fashion-photography career with her husband Allan Arbus, and killed herself in her Westbeth studio in Greenwich Village in 1971 at forty-eight. Her square-format black-and-white portraits — Identical Twins, Roselle, NJ (1967), Boy with Toy Hand Grenade in Central Park, NYC (1962), the Untitled series at New Jersey institutions for the developmentally disabled — document American subjects (twins, nudists, transvestites, dwarfs, suburban housewives, children) at a deliberately uncomfortable physical distance and direct gaze. The estate-managed argument, which the family controls with notable strictness, is that the photographer's task is to refuse the documentary tradition's pretended sympathy and to admit the camera's voyeurism — and that this admission is what makes the photograph capable of dignity."
  },
  a542: { // Donald Judd
    message: "Judd was born in Excelsior Springs, Missouri in 1928, trained in art at the Art Students League and in philosophy at Columbia, worked as a critic at Arts Magazine for most of the 1960s, and bought the small West Texas town of Marfa in 1971 where he lived until his death in 1994. His specific objects — Untitled (Stack) (1967, ten identical galvanized iron boxes spaced 9 inches apart up a wall), the 100 Untitled Works in Mill Aluminum (1982–86, installed in two Marfa artillery sheds), the long progressions — are not paintings, not sculptures, but objects: industrial-fabricated, repetitive, scaled to architecture, refusing pictorial illusion. The estate-managed argument, sustained by the Judd Foundation and the Chinati Foundation in Marfa, is that the object must be installed in the artist-determined architectural condition — and that displaying it otherwise (in a curatorial mix, on a museum pedestal) misunderstands what the work fundamentally is."
  },
  a846: { // Doug Aitken
    message: "Aitken was born in Redondo Beach, California in 1968, trained at the Art Center College of Design in Pasadena, and lives in Venice, California. His film and sound installations — Sleepwalkers (2007, an eight-channel night-time projection onto the exterior of MoMA), Mirage (2017, a mirrored ranch-house in the Coachella Valley), Black Mirror (2011, the New Museum five-channel video) — work at architectural scale, often outdoors and at night, with multichannel synchronisation and ambient electronic scores. The argument is that contemporary American video has been too long confined to the gallery's black-box screening room — and that its proper venue is the building exterior, the desert, the night sky, where the moving image can finally meet the landscape that produced it."
  },
  a7: { // Ed Ruscha
    message: "Ruscha was born in Omaha, Nebraska in 1937, raised in Oklahoma City, and arrived in Los Angeles in 1956 to study at the Chouinard Art Institute (now CalArts) under Robert Irwin and Joe Goode. His paintings — Standard Station, Amarillo, Texas (1963), The Los Angeles County Museum on Fire (1965–68), the long word-paintings (OOF, NOISE, OK) — and his artist-books (Twentysix Gasoline Stations (1963, the foundational artist-book of the postwar period), Every Building on the Sunset Strip (1966), Some Los Angeles Apartments (1965)) work in a register that takes the vernacular American highway, the dry humour of Pop Art, and the deadpan distance of European conceptualism as equally legitimate. The argument, sustained over sixty years, is that Los Angeles vernacular architecture and signage is a serious painterly subject — and that the artist's book, mass-produced, cheaply printed, and uneditioned, is a medium of equal weight to the painting."
  },
  a72: { // Eva Hesse
    message: "Hesse was born in Hamburg in 1936 to a German-Jewish family, escaped on the Kindertransport at age two, grew up in New York, trained at Pratt, Cooper Union, and Yale (under Josef Albers), spent a transformative year in a German abandoned factory in 1965, and died of a brain tumour in 1970 at thirty-four. Her work — Hang Up (1966, a wood-and-cloth frame with an absurd 10-foot steel-tube cord), Repetition Nineteen III (1968, nineteen fibreglass cylinders), the latex Contingent (1969), the rope piece Untitled (1970) — used latex, fibreglass, rope, and rubber to dissolve minimalism's industrial rigidity into the bodily, the deteriorating, the slumping, the irregular. The estate-managed argument is that the strict minimalist grid required the post-minimal corrective her materials provide — and that the inherent decay of latex (her works have darkened, cracked, and become unstable since her death) is a condition the conservation profession has had to invent new categories to handle."
  },
  a747: { // Genesis Belanger
    message: "Belanger was born in Greenfield, Massachusetts in 1978, trained at the New York Academy of Art and the School of Visual Arts, and lives in Brooklyn; she worked for years as a sculptor at the Cherry Lane Theater and in commercial scenic studios before her own practice took off. Her pastel-glazed porcelain still-lives — A Strange Relative (2019), Through the Eye of a Needle (2022) — depict cigarettes, severed hands, pills, sliced fruit, and disembodied tongues in a colour palette that recalls 1950s American advertising and the Memphis Group. The argument is that the women's domestic still-life — supposedly resolved by Pop Art — was actually deepened into a quietly menacing register that the era did not name, and that contemporary ceramic sculpture can finally take that register seriously."
  },
  a15: { // Georg Baselitz
    message: "Baselitz was born Hans-Georg Kern in Deutschbaselitz, Saxony in 1938 (taking the town's name as his surname in 1961), escaped from East to West Berlin in 1957, and lives between the Ammersee in Bavaria and Imperia, Italy. His decisive move came in 1969, when he inverted all his canvases — eagles, painters, the lost German Romantic forest, then his own self-portraits — and has painted upside-down ever since, including the recent Negative Paintings (where he reverses figure and ground). His earliest scandal, The Big Night Down the Drain (1962–63, a masturbating boy that was confiscated by West German prosecutors for obscenity), set the tone; later Adler (Eagle) (1972), the Hero series, and the Big Heads (1969–) sustain it. The argument is that painting must declare its independence from representation — and that the inverted canvas is the most efficient way to keep the painter and the viewer permanently aware that what they are looking at is not the world but a painted surface."
  },
  a96: { // George Condo
    message: "Condo was born in Concord, New Hampshire in 1957, dropped out of UMass Lowell, worked briefly at Andy Warhol's Factory as a silkscreen printer in 1980, and has lived between New York and Paris since the early 1980s. His paintings — The Butcher's Wife (2009), the Mental States series (2011, the New Museum survey), the Insane Asylum series (2003), the Kanye West My Beautiful Dark Twisted Fantasy album cover (2010) — depict grotesque hybrid figures, multi-eyed portraits, and psychologically distorted bodies in a register he calls 'Artificial Realism'. The argument is that the human face in painting has always been a composite of multiple psychological states held in single image — and that the contemporary portrait painter's job is to show all of them at once rather than to select."
  },
  a197: { // Gerhard Richter
    message: "Richter was born in Dresden in 1932, lived through the Allied bombing of the city as a child, trained at the Dresden Academy under Socialist Realism, fled to West Germany in 1961, and has lived in Cologne since. His decisive choice has been to work simultaneously in three modes — photorealist painting from family photographs and press images (Ema Nude Descending a Staircase, the October 18, 1977 RAF series), pure abstract painting built with a 4-metre squeegee (Cage paintings, 2006), and the colour-chart works — refusing to commit to any one for over sixty years. The argument is that the post-1945 European painter cannot adopt a single style without lying about the historical condition — and that the sustained, methodical doubt across simultaneously incompatible visual idioms is the only honest position painting can take."
  },
  a375: { // Gilbert & George
    message: "Gilbert Proesch (born 1943, Dolomites, Italy) and George Passmore (born 1942, Devon, England) met at Saint Martin's School of Art in 1967, declared themselves 'Living Sculptures' in 1969 with The Singing Sculpture (in which they stood on a table and mimed to Underneath the Arches in face-paint and matching suits for hours), and have lived together as a single artistic body in Spitalfields, East London for over fifty years. Their large stained-glass-style photo-pictures — Death Hope Life Fear (1984), the long Naked Shit Pictures, the Gingko Pictures — combine self-portraiture, urban material (graffiti, blood, vomit, semen, detritus), and a relentless deadpan provocation. The argument is that the artist's life is not separable from the artwork — that two men in matching tweed, walking the same East End streets for decades, are themselves the long-term sculptural medium."
  },
  a183: { // Giorgio Morandi
    message: "Morandi was born in Bologna in 1890, lived almost his entire life in a single Bologna apartment with his three sisters, taught printmaking at the Accademia di Belle Arti di Bologna from 1930 to 1956, and died in 1964 without having travelled outside Italy in any meaningful way. His paintings — the Natura Morta (still life) series sustained for over forty years — depict a small set of bottles, vases, and small boxes from a single shelf in his studio, repeated, rearranged, repainted in soft ochre, white, and grey palette of remarkable narrowness. The estate-managed argument is that twentieth-century European painting's most quietly radical move was not Cubism or Surrealism but the decision to paint the same shelf, the same objects, for forty years — and that the slow life-long subject is the painter's most under-credited available form."
  },
  a38: { // Helen Frankenthaler
    message: "Frankenthaler was born in Manhattan in 1928 to a New York Supreme Court Justice and a German-Jewish mother, trained at Bennington College and with Hans Hofmann, married Robert Motherwell from 1958 to 1971, and died in 2011. Her decisive innovation came at age 23: her Mountains and Sea (1952), a 7-by-10-foot canvas made by pouring thinned oil paint directly onto unprimed cotton duck on the studio floor, dissolved the gestural-Abstract-Expressionist mark into a stain. Morris Louis and Kenneth Noland visited her studio in 1953 and the Color Field movement followed; the long Bay (1963) and beyond extends the soak-stain logic. The estate-managed argument is that the post-Pollock corrective to gestural abstraction was a woman's invention — and that the soak-stain, materially intimate with the canvas's bare weave, is a feminist innovation the canonical narrative has had trouble naming as such."
  },
  a86: { // Henry Taylor
    message: "Taylor was born in Ventura, California in 1958, worked for ten years as a psychiatric technician at Camarillo State Hospital, trained at CalArts in his thirties, and lives in Los Angeles. His paintings — THE TIMES THEY AINT A CHANGING, FAST ENOUGH! (2017, his Whitney Biennial entry depicting the police killing of Philando Castile), the long series of portraits of friends, family, basketball players, jazz musicians, and historical Black figures — work with immediate, confident flat brushwork on raw canvas that recalls Alice Neel and Bob Thompson rather than the polished MFA painting hand. The argument is that figurative portraiture of Black life is American painting's most central available subject — and that the painter's accessibility, intimacy, and refusal of preciousness are not deficits but the form's correct register."
  },
  a637: { // Hélio Oiticica
    message: "Oiticica was born in Rio de Janeiro in 1937 to an entomologist father, trained in painting and in samba at the Mangueira school in the Mangueira favela (an experience he described as foundational), and worked in Rio and briefly in London and New York until his death of a stroke at 42 in 1980. His decisive break came with the Parangolés (1964 onward) — wearable layered cape sculptures designed to be danced in by participants from the favela; his earlier Bólides and Penetráveis (penetrable architectures) and the later Tropicália (1967, an immersive sand-and-plant Tropicalismo environment that gave the cultural movement its name) extend the same logic. The estate-managed argument is that Brazilian Neo-Concrete art's most radical move was to refuse the gallery-wall altogether — and that the body, the favela, the dance, the wearable colour were collectively a serious sculptural medium that the European modernist canon was not equipped to register."
  },
  a267: { // JR
    message: "JR was born Jean René in Paris in 1983 to a Tunisian-Eastern-European family and worked from age 15 as a graffiti writer in Paris suburbs before moving to large-format photographic wheat-pasting in his twenties. His projects — Portrait of a Generation (2004, banlieue portraits pasted onto Parisian Haussmann buildings), Women Are Heroes (2008, women's eyes pasted onto Kibera and Rio favela rooftops), the Inside Out Project (2011 onward, a participatory portrait booth that has printed 500,000 individual portraits in 150 countries), the US/Mexico border installations — operate at the scale of cities. The argument, sustained for two decades, is that the documentary photograph's contemporary public function is not the magazine or the museum but the wall — and that the giant pasted face, visible from the satellite as well as from the street, returns the documentary subject's gaze to the people who would otherwise look past them."
  },

  // ── Chunk 6 ──

  a339: { // James Turrell
    message: "Turrell was born in Pasadena in 1943 to a Quaker family, trained as a perceptual psychologist at Pomona before his MFA at Claremont, and is, with Robert Irwin and Doug Wheeler, one of the central figures of California Light and Space. His life-project Roden Crater — an extinct volcanic cinder cone in northern Arizona that he has been re-engineering into a naked-eye observatory since 1979 — sits alongside the long-running Skyspaces (rooms whose ceiling apertures frame the sky), the Ganzfelds, and Breathing Light (2013). The argument is that light, treated as a sculptural medium rather than a metaphor, is the most direct vehicle for perceptual experience — and that the Quaker meeting-house tradition of silent attention is its proper devotional model."
  },
  a884: { // Jamian Juliano-Villani
    message: "Juliano-Villani was born in Newark, NJ in 1987, trained at Rutgers, and lives in New York where she also runs the artist-curated gallery O'Flaherty's on the Lower East Side. Her airbrushed and projected canvases — Tickle Me (2015) onward — sample cartoons, midcentury advertising, pop ephemera, and obscure visual quotation in absurdist juxtapositions painted from photographic source. The argument is that contemporary painting's source archive is the same Google Image Search the rest of culture is using — and that the painter who refuses to admit this is operating from a fantasy of pre-internet purity that no longer exists."
  },
  a240: { // Jean Dubuffet
    message: "Dubuffet was born in Le Havre in 1901, was a wine merchant in Paris until age 41, and turned full-time to art in 1942; his decisive theoretical position came when he coined the term Art Brut in 1945 for the work of psychiatric patients, mediums, and self-taught makers he collected at scale. His L'Hourloupe cycle (1962–74) — a single self-contained graphic universe of red, white, blue, and black cellular forms — was painted across canvases, sculptures, walking suits, and a small Périgny chapel-village. The estate-managed argument is that the academic Western canon was a narrow accident, and the broader visual culture of the untrained outsider is the deeper available tradition for the post-war painter."
  },
  a46: { // Jean-Michel Basquiat
    message: "Basquiat was born in Brooklyn in 1960 to a Haitian father and Puerto Rican mother, dropped out of high school, slept in friends' apartments and the back of the Mudd Club in late-1970s downtown New York, and started in 1977 with the SAMO graffiti tag (with Al Diaz) before being painted into the canonical American art history by his early death of a heroin overdose in 1988 at twenty-seven. His paintings — Untitled (Skull) (1981), Hollywood Africans (1983), Irony of Negro Policeman (1981) — combine the crown motif, anatomical diagrams from Gray's Anatomy, jazz-and-boxing references, and dense layered text in compositions that owe equally to Twombly's writing-mark and to the New York subway car. The estate-managed argument is that the canonical narrative of 1980s American painting was deeply racialised, and that Basquiat's reinstatement, sustained by the family across thirty-five years of estate work, is not a market story but a corrective."
  },
  a3: { // Jeff Koons
    message: "Koons was born in York, Pennsylvania in 1955, trained at the Maryland Institute College of Art and the School of the Art Institute of Chicago, worked briefly as a Wall Street commodities broker, and now operates a New York studio of over a hundred employees that fabricates each of his pieces to specifications he himself rarely touches. His works — the inflatable-as-cast-stainless-steel Rabbit (1986, the $91.1m 2019 auction record), Balloon Dog (Orange) (1994–2000), Made in Heaven (1989–91, hyperreal porcelain and large photographs with his then-wife Ilona Staller), the long Celebration series — collapse the categories of kitsch, luxury good, and contemporary sculpture. The argument is that the auction house is the contemporary art world's true cathedral — and that the artist who builds the brand-engineering, the studio system, and the press-managed persona is the one being most honest about it."
  },
  a537: { // Jenny Holzer
    message: "Holzer was born in Gallipolis, Ohio in 1950, trained at Ohio University and at the Whitney Independent Study Program in the late 1970s, and lives in upstate New York. Her Truisms (1977–79) — short imperative aphorisms (\"ABUSE OF POWER COMES AS NO SURPRISE,\" \"MONEY CREATES TASTE\") — were initially printed as cheap posters and pasted up around Lower Manhattan, then moved into LED scrolls, carved stone benches, building projections, and large electronic signage at the Times Square Spectacolor board (the 1982 Public Art Fund commission). The argument is that political language, removed from its institutional context (legislature, news, manifesto) and rendered in the typography of commerce (LED, marble, billboard), becomes legible as the public statement it always was."
  },
  a17: { // Jenny Saville
    message: "Saville was born in Cambridge, England in 1970, trained at the Glasgow School of Art, was bought out of her degree show in 1992 by Charles Saatchi (who funded her studio for two years), and now lives in Oxford. Her monumental nudes — Branded (1992), Reverse (2002), Stare (2004–05), the recent Mother and Child series — render flesh at heroic religious-painting scale: heavy, scarred, surgical, bruised, in flux, painted with a loaded brush that recalls Lucian Freud and Willem de Kooning. The argument is that the female nude in painting has been historically composed of polite lies about body weight and surface, and that the figurative painter who returns to the nude must paint actual flesh — its volume, its bruising, its post-surgical condition — without flinching."
  },
  a434: { // John Baldessari
    message: "Baldessari was born in National City, California in 1931, trained at San Diego State, founded the post-studio programme at the new California Institute of the Arts in 1970, and taught there until 1988; he then taught at UCLA. His decisive 1970 act was to cremate every painting he had made before 1966 (the Cremation Project) and to declare a clean break with the medium. The conceptual works that followed — I Will Not Make Any More Boring Art (1971), the dot-over-face photo series, Wrong (1967), the long Pencil and Eraser collages — combined photography, text, and found image with a deliberately deadpan instructional voice. The argument, transmitted directly to a generation of CalArts students (Mike Kelley, Tony Oursler, Jack Goldstein, James Welling, David Salle, Matt Mullican), is that the artist's most consequential decisions are conceptual and pedagogical — and that humour is not the opposite of seriousness but its honest companion."
  },
  a945: { // John Currin
    message: "Currin was born in Boulder, Colorado in 1962, trained at Carnegie Mellon and Yale (where his classmates were Lisa Yuskavage, Sean Landers, and Joe Andoe), and lives in New York. His paintings — Ms. Omni (1993), Big Hands (1999), The Patch (2002), the long Pornography series — use the chiaroscuro, varnish, and figurative refinement of seventeenth-century Dutch and Italian Mannerist painting to depict mannered women, suburban couples, golfing widows, and increasingly explicit soft-pornographic scenarios. The argument is that the late-1990s American painter had a choice between extending late-modernist abstraction and reclaiming Old-Master technique — and that the second choice, applied to the most degraded available subject matter, is more politically uncomfortable than the first."
  },
  a665: { // Joseph Beuys
    message: "Beuys was born in Krefeld in 1921, served as a Stuka pilot in World War II until being shot down over Crimea in 1944 (the founding myth he constructed around being saved by Tatar nomads who wrapped him in fat and felt provided the totemic materials of his later sculpture), taught at the Düsseldorf Kunstakademie from 1961 until his political dismissal in 1972, and died in 1986. His best-known works — How to Explain Pictures to a Dead Hare (1965, in which he carried a dead hare around a Düsseldorf gallery for three hours, his head covered in honey and gold leaf), 7000 Oaks (1982, his contribution to documenta 7 — a city-wide tree-planting project completed in 1987 after his death) — built a single life-long argument that society itself is a sculpture to be shaped. The estate-managed argument is that his concept of \"social sculpture\" was the most consequential European post-war proposal — and that art's most important venue is the political assembly, the lecture hall, the public space."
  },
  a588: { // Kara Walker
    message: "Walker was born in Stockton, California in 1969, moved to Atlanta with her family at thirteen (an event she has cited as foundational for confronting American racial history directly), trained at the Atlanta College of Art and Rhode Island School of Design, and lives in New York where she taught at Columbia for many years. Her room-scale cut-paper silhouettes — Gone: An Historical Romance of a Civil War as It Occurred B'tween the Dusky Thighs of One Young Negress and Her Heart (1994), the long Slavery! Slavery! sequence — adopt the format of nineteenth-century parlour silhouette portraiture and load it with scenes of slavery, sexual violence, and racial caricature. Her later monumental works — A Subtlety (2014, the 35-foot Domino Sugar Factory sphinx-mammy in sugar), Fons Americanus (2019, Tate Turbine Hall) — argue that the contemporary public monument tradition is still operating on the foundational American racial mythology, and that the artist's job is to make that mythology's psychosexual underpinnings illegibly visible."
  },
  a317: { // Lauren Quin
    message: "Quin was born in Los Angeles in 1992, trained at the Tyler School of Art and Yale MFA, and lives and works in Los Angeles. Her densely layered paintings — Tongue Tied (2022), Hot Knife (2021) — build tubular and energetic mark-making over fluorescent grounds in compositions that read as both biological cross-section and atmospheric weather event. The argument is that the post-2015 American painter has a usable claim on a kind of intricate, gestural, almost neurologically detailed abstraction that the Greenbergian story declared finished — and that the small studio paintings, accumulated mark by mark, are not less serious than the heroic large gestural canvas."
  },
  a278: { // Lee Ufan
    message: "Lee Ufan was born in Haman, Korea in 1936, moved to Tokyo in 1956 where he studied philosophy at Nihon University, and is the central theorist of the Mono-ha (\"School of Things\") movement that emerged at Tama Art University in the late 1960s. His Relatum sculptures (1968 onward) — typically a single rough stone placed beside or atop a sheet of steel — and his From Line (1973) and From Point (1972) painting series, in which he deposits a single colour-loaded brushstroke and then deposits its decreasing trace until the brush is dry, work with materials at their minimal point of contact. The argument is that the artist's task is not to make new objects but to stage encounters between existing things — and that the philosophical condition of art in late modernity is one of presence (the stone) meeting industrial production (the steel) without either erasing the other."
  },
  a281: { // Li Hei Di
    message: "Li Hei Di was born in Shenyang, China in 1997, trained at the Royal College of Art in London, and lives in London. Her dreamlike sensuous abstract canvases — Bruises Bloom Wherever You Touch (2023), Ravishing Disquiet (2024) — work in a register of queer Asian desire painted in deliberately blurred, atmospheric colour that operates between figure and pure field. The argument is that the contemporary queer-Asian-painting subject is most precisely held in atmospheric abstraction — and that the painter who refuses the obligation to render legible identity through legible figure is doing the harder political work."
  },
  a84: { // Lorna Simpson
    message: "Simpson was born in Brooklyn in 1960, trained at the School of Visual Arts and at UC San Diego under Allan Kaprow, and lives in Brooklyn. Her decisive 1980s work — Waterbearer (1986), Stereo Styles (1988), Wigs (1994) — paired photographs of Black women, often photographed from behind so that the face is withheld, with short blocks of text printed on plaques beneath, in a register that worked with the documentary tradition while refusing its identification practices. Her later painted ice-and-photo works (2014 onward) and the dense Source Notes paintings argue that the question of how Black women's bodies are represented is not resolved by simply photographing them differently — and that the artist must work systematically through every available image-and-text relationship the institution has handed her."
  },
  a162: { // Louis Fratino
    message: "Fratino was born in Annapolis in 1993, trained at the Maryland Institute College of Art and at the Yale Norfolk programme, lived briefly in Berlin, and lives in Brooklyn. His paintings — Metropolitan (2019), An argument (2021), the long Self-Portrait and Lover series — depict tender, intimate scenes of contemporary queer life rendered in a deliberately archaic figurative idiom that draws on Pavel Tchelitchew, Marsden Hartley, and the early-twentieth-century European modernism that took male bodies seriously as painting subjects. The argument is that the contemporary queer figurative painter's most useful precedents are not the high-modernist sublimations of the closet — they are the early modernist precedents who painted lovers and friends without metaphor."
  },
  a70: { // Louise Bourgeois
    message: "Bourgeois was born in Paris in 1911 to a family of tapestry restorers, was deeply affected by her father's long affair with the family's English governess (a biographical fact she returned to obsessively for seven decades), moved to New York in 1938 with her husband the American art historian Robert Goldwater, and worked there in near-institutional obscurity until her 1982 MoMA retrospective at age seventy-one. The works that became canonical in her last three decades — Maman (1999, the 30-foot bronze spider with marble eggs in its sac, installed first at Tate Modern's Turbine Hall), the Cells (1990–2010, the cage installations with bones, scissors, mirrors, mothballs), the long stuffed-fabric Couples — work through a single sustained autobiographical psychoanalytic vocabulary. The estate-managed argument is that the long delayed institutional recognition is itself the work's subject — and that the woman artist's life-span is part of the medium she had to work with."
  },
  a212: { // Luc Tuymans
    message: "Tuymans was born in Mortsel, Belgium in 1958 and lives in Antwerp; his family had ambiguous positions during the German occupation and his earliest works addressed Belgium's colonial role in the Congo and the country's wartime collaboration. His paintings — Gas Chamber (1986), Still Life (2002, his unsettlingly bland 9/11 response shown at documenta 11), The Secretary of State (2005, Condoleezza Rice) — render their subjects in a deliberately washed-out, off-key, almost-failed colour register that draws on Polaroid film stock and on faded 1970s news media. The argument is that contemporary historical and political subject matter requires the painting to look as if it could not bear to render the subject directly — and that the deliberately bland, almost wrong-coloured surface is the only honest contemporary response."
  },
  a592: { // Marina Abramović
    message: "Abramović was born in Belgrade in 1946 to two Yugoslav partisan parents who became Tito-era army generals and then a strict authoritarian household. Her decisive early works — Rhythm 0 (1974, in which she stood passively for six hours in a Naples gallery alongside seventy-two objects including a loaded gun and invited the audience to do what they wanted to her), Lips of Thomas (1975, in which she cut a star into her stomach and lay on a cross of ice), the long Marina/Ulay collaboration (1976–88, especially The Great Wall Walk, 1988) — built the foundational language of endurance performance. Her later institutional work — The Artist is Present (2010, sitting silently across from individual MoMA visitors for 700 hours over three months), the Marina Abramović Institute — argue that the body, sustained at the threshold of physical or psychological collapse, is the only artistic medium that the institution has not yet learned how to commodify cleanly."
  },
  a81: { // Mark Bradford
    message: "Bradford was born in Los Angeles in 1961, worked for two decades as a hairdresser in his mother's South LA salon, trained at CalArts in his thirties, and lives in Los Angeles where he co-founded the social-practice space Art + Practice in Leimert Park. His monumental abstract paintings — Scorched Earth (2006), Pickett's Charge (2017, the eight-canvas Hirshhorn cycle), Process of Elimination (2021), the US Pavilion at the 2017 Venice Biennale — are built by laminating, sanding, and tearing away merchant posters, billboard scraps, end-paper from hair-perming, and tar paper salvaged from South Los Angeles. The argument is that the surface of the contemporary abstract painting can be the material archive of a specific neighbourhood's labour — and that the painter's body, working with sander and razor, is doing the work of an excavator more than a brush-handler."
  },
  a137: { // Marlene Dumas
    message: "Dumas was born in Cape Town in 1953, grew up in apartheid-era South Africa on her family's wine farm, moved to the Netherlands in 1976 to study at the Atelier '63 in Haarlem, and lives in Amsterdam. Her paintings — The Painter (1994, her daughter Helena confronting the viewer), Dead Girl (2002), Measuring Your Own Grave (2003), the long Magdalena series, the recent series on the Israel-Palestine conflict — work from photographic source material (pornography, news media, personal Polaroids, art-historical reproduction) in thinned washes of oil that recall Polaroid film stock dissolved in water. The argument is that the painted figure cannot escape its photographic source in the contemporary period — and that the painter's task is to register the photograph's emotional residue without imitating its surface."
  },
  a442: { // Maurizio Cattelan
    message: "Cattelan was born in Padua in 1960 to a working-class family (his mother was a cleaner, his father a truck driver), is largely self-taught, and lives between New York and Milan. His works — La Nona Ora (1999, a wax sculpture of Pope John Paul II struck down by a meteorite), America (2016, the 18-karat-gold working toilet installed at the Guggenheim and later stolen from Blenheim Palace), Comedian (2019, the duct-taped banana at Art Basel Miami that sold for $120,000), Him (2001, a kneeling Hitler) — operate as deliberate media events that the press and the auction record together complete. The argument is that the contemporary artwork's actual material is its circulation — and that the artist who treats the press release, the stunt, and the auction record as part of the work, rather than as ancillary noise around it, is being more honest about what art currently is."
  },
  a298: { // Maysha Mohamedi
    message: "Mohamedi was born in Los Angeles in 1980 to Iranian parents and lives there; she trained at UC San Diego, holds a PhD in neuroscience that she completed before turning fully to painting, and uses experimental pigments she makes from materials drawn from her own life (oil from a sardine can, Pasadena tap water, her son's medication). Her paintings — Mother Tongue (2019), Pacifica (2021) — combine the abstract language vocabulary inherited from her Iranian first-generation childhood with a Californian gestural-painting tradition. The argument is that the abstract painting is not a culturally neutral medium — and that the Persian-American painter's first-language and second-language relation is itself a usable formal vocabulary, provided the materials of her actual life are part of the paint."
  },
  a73: { // Mike Kelley
    message: "Kelley was born in Wayne, Michigan in 1954 to a Catholic auto-industry family, trained at the University of Michigan and at CalArts under John Baldessari and David Askevold (where he formed Destroy All Monsters with Jim Shaw), and worked in Los Angeles until his suicide in 2012. His major works — More Love Hours Than Can Ever Be Repaid (1987, a wall of handmade afghans and stuffed-animal craft objects), Educational Complex (1995, a model of every school he had ever attended with the architectural details he couldn't remember left blank), the Day Is Done film cycle (2005) — mined the discarded craft objects, suburban architecture, repressed-memory therapy, and trauma narratives of post-war American adolescence. The estate-managed argument is that the canonical narrative of late-twentieth-century American art has under-credited the unfashionable suburban-working-class material it actually drew from — and that the dismissed object (the macramé hanger, the church basement) is its proper subject."
  },
  a22: { // Nan Goldin
    message: "Goldin was born in Washington, DC in 1953 to a Jewish middle-class family, was sent to a foster home at fourteen after her older sister Barbara killed herself by lying down on commuter rail tracks (an event Goldin has cited as the founding fact of her artistic life), trained at the School of the Museum of Fine Arts Boston, and has worked across New York, Berlin, Paris, and Provincetown. Her best-known work, The Ballad of Sexual Dependency (begun 1979, refined continuously across more than thirty years), is a slideshow of seven hundred snapshots from her own life — friends, lovers, drag queens, the post-Stonewall Lower East Side downtown scene, the early years of the AIDS epidemic — set to a continuous popular-music soundtrack. The argument, sustained over forty years and most recently into her PAIN activism against the Sackler family, is that documentary photography of one's own community is not a lesser register than fine-art photography — and that the slideshow, with music, is the medium's most honest contemporary form."
  },
  a139: { // Njideka Akunyili Crosby
    message: "Akunyili Crosby was born in Enugu, Nigeria in 1983, moved to the United States at sixteen to study at Swarthmore (pre-medicine), trained at Yale MFA, and lives in Los Angeles; her mother Dora was the director of Nigeria's national food and drug agency before her 2008 assassination, a biographical fact that the family-archive content of her work carries quietly. Her paintings — Predecessors (2013), I Refuse to be Invisible (2018), The Beautyful Ones (2012 onward) — combine photo-transfer printing of Nigerian domestic and popular imagery (advertising, family snapshots, fabric pattern, political portraits) with gestural painting in compositions that operate at both the wall scale and the close-reading scale. The argument is that the diasporic Nigerian-American painter's interior is not a single biographical room — and that the painting must function at two reading distances simultaneously (the body of work seen across the room, the photographic detail seen with the nose against the canvas)."
  },
  a168: { // On Kawara
    message: "Kawara was born in Kariya, Aichi, Japan in 1932, lived in New York from 1965 until his death in 2014, and built his lifelong practice around the simplest possible rule: paint the day's date. The Today series (1966–2013, almost three thousand date paintings completed over nearly five decades, each made in one day or destroyed if not finished) is paired with the I Got Up postcards (1968–79, daily postcards stamped with the time he woke up), the One Million Years readings, and the I Am Still Alive telegrams. The estate-managed argument is that the artist's lifelong daily existence is itself a long-form conceptual work — and that the rule, sustained for forty-eight years without exception, is the most honest available proposition about what a contemporary practice can be."
  },
  a185: { // Oscar Murillo
    message: "Murillo was born in La Paila, Colombia in 1986, moved to London with his family at ten, trained at Westminster and at the Royal College of Art, won the Turner Prize in 2019 (in a self-organised four-way tie with Lawrence Abu Hamdan, Helen Cammock, and Tai Shani), and divides his practice between studios in London, La Paila, and Mexico City. His Frequencies project (2013 onward) installs raw cotton canvases on classroom desks in schools across the global South and South-East Asia and collects them after several years of being drawn-on, scuffed, and torn by children; his Manifestation paintings, dense gestural canvases, and stitched assemblages extend the same logic. The argument is that the painted surface in late capitalism is most honestly a record of accumulated labour and time — and that the artist's task is to outsource the marking to people whose marks the gallery system would not otherwise see."
  },
  a190: { // Palermo
    message: "Peter Schwarze, who took the name Blinky Palermo from the boxing manager who had himself been named after the Italian-American mob town, was born in Leipzig in 1943, was adopted into the West German family Heisterkamp shortly after, was Joseph Beuys's most precocious student at the Düsseldorf Kunstakademie from 1962, and died in mysterious circumstances in the Maldives at thirty-three in 1977. His brief practice — the Cloth Pictures (1966–72, sewn-fabric canvases of monochromatic colour), the Wall Paintings (1968 onward, painted directly onto gallery walls and then over-painted at exhibition's end), To the People of New York City (1976, the fifteen-panel last work) — addressed colour, scale, and architecture with a quietness that the later canonical narrative of post-war German painting (around Polke, Richter, Beuys) tended to over-shadow. The estate-managed argument is that Palermo's work proposes a fourth path within post-war German painting — neither Beuysian social sculpture nor Polke-Richter image-doubt — and that the slow recovery of his work continues to expand what that period's options actually were."
  },
  a246: { // Pam Evelyn
    message: "Evelyn was born in Surrey, England in 1996, trained at the Slade and the Royal College of Art, and lives in London; she emerged in 2021 immediately into top-tier gallery representation and rapid auction recognition. Her gestural atmospheric canvases — A Handful of Dust (2023), The Painter's Field (2024) — draw on English landscape painting (Turner, Constable, Frank Auerbach), the gestural inheritance of Cecily Brown, and pure mood-based abstraction in large scrubbed and layered surfaces. The argument, made by the painter's brief and unusually high-velocity early career, is that the English landscape tradition is still available to a young painter today — provided the painter is willing to enter it through abstraction rather than topographic record."
  },
  a409: { // Park Seo-Bo
    message: "Park Seo-Bo was born in Yecheon, North Gyeongsang Province in 1931, trained at Hongik University in Seoul, and died in 2023; he is one of the founding figures of Dansaekhwa, the Korean monochrome movement that emerged in the 1970s as a deliberate non-aligned position between Western minimalism and Japanese Mono-ha. His Écriture series (1967–2023, sustained for over half a century) was made by painting a canvas with thick paint and then dragging a pencil or a chopstick through the wet surface in long horizontal lines, leaving a topography of repeated furrow. The estate-managed argument is that Dansaekhwa is not the Korean version of any Western movement — and that the meditative repeated mark, sustained as a daily practice across decades, is a specifically Korean post-war Buddhist-Confucian sculptural-painterly innovation."
  },
  a71: { // Philip Guston
    message: "Guston was born Phillip Goldstein in Montreal in 1913 to a family of Russian Jewish émigrés who moved to Los Angeles when he was six (his father killed himself in the family barn when Philip was ten, an event he found and that shaped his later imagery), and he died in Woodstock, NY in 1980. His decisive mid-career break came in 1969, when, after twenty years as one of the most respected American Abstract Expressionists, he returned to figuration — to crude, cartoonish, hooded-Klansman figures in flat reds and pinks, shown at the disastrous 1970 Marlborough Gallery exhibition that Hilton Kramer attacked as \"Mandarin Pretending to Be a Stumblebum.\" Works like Painter (1969) and The Studio (1969) sustained the new mode until his death. The estate-managed argument is that the mid-career break is itself the most honest position a painter can take — and that the institution that demanded stylistic consistency was demanding something painting is not built to deliver."
  },
  a457: { // Pierre Huyghe
    message: "Huyghe was born in Paris in 1962, trained at the École nationale supérieure des Arts Décoratifs, and lives between Santiago and New York. His installations work with living systems as artistic collaborators: Untilled (documenta 13, 2012) installed a beehive on a concrete sculpture's head in Kassel's compost heap, with a white-legged hound named Human roaming the site; A Journey That Wasn't (2005) staged an Arctic expedition and a Central Park ice-rink concert based on its findings; the more recent Liminal (2024, Punta della Dogana) introduces an AI agent that learns from visitor behaviour. The argument is that contemporary sculpture's most consequential move is to admit non-human agents — bees, dogs, AI, algae — as full collaborators, and to accept that the work changes when the artist is not there."
  },
  a80: { // Pipilotti Rist
    message: "Rist was born Elisabeth Charlotte Rist in Grabs, Switzerland in 1962 (she changed her first name to Pipilotti after Pippi Longstocking around 1986), trained at the Vienna Institute of Applied Arts and at the Basel School of Design in audiovisual communication, and lives in Zürich. Her video works — I'm Not the Girl Who Misses Much (1986, a 7-minute slowed-and-distorted lip-sync of John Lennon), Ever Is Over All (1997, the Venice Biennale work in which a young woman in a blue dress walks down a city street and smashes parked cars with a flower-shaped iron rod), Pixel Forest (2016, the New Museum suspended-LED installation) — work in saturated colour, immersive multi-channel projection, and the body's relation to architectural space. The argument is that video's gallery condition has been treated too long as the black-box screening room — and that the medium's actual proper site is the entire room, ceiling-and-floor, drenched in colour."
  },
  a8: { // Richard Prince
    message: "Prince was born in the Panama Canal Zone in 1949 to American parents in US government employ, was raised in Massachusetts, and lives in upstate New York. His decisive early work, Untitled (Cowboys) (1980 onward), is a series of re-photographed Marlboro advertising images stripped of their original ad copy and presented as photographs; his Nurse Paintings (2002 onward), his Joke Paintings, his Instagram Portraits (2014, screen-captures of strangers' Instagrams with brief Prince comments printed onto the canvas), and his Spiritual America (1983, the controversial reproduction of a Garry Gross photograph of a ten-year-old Brooke Shields) extend the same logic. The argument, sustained against multiple copyright lawsuits, is that the contemporary American visual environment is the public domain whether the courts agree or not — and that the artist whose practice is built entirely on re-photographing someone else's work is making a position about authorship that the law has not yet caught up with."
  },
  a6: { // Richard Serra
    message: "Serra was born in San Francisco in 1938 to a Spanish steel-mill rigger father and a Russian-Jewish mother, paid for his BA at UC Santa Barbara by working in his father's mill, trained at Yale MFA, and lived in New York until his death in 2024. His decisive works — Prop (1968, a lead plate held up by a single rolled lead pipe), Tilted Arc (1981, the 120-foot Cor-Ten steel wall installed in Manhattan's Federal Plaza and then removed in 1989 after public petition), the Torqued Ellipses (1996–99) and Torqued Toruses — used industrial steel at architectural scale to make sculptures the viewer's body had to physically walk through. The estate-managed argument is that post-war American sculpture's most consequential proposition was the embodied scale of industrial steel — and that the Tilted Arc removal is not a footnote to Serra's career but a foundational document about what public sculpture is permitted to ask of its viewers."
  },
  a285: { // Robert Longo
    message: "Longo was born in Brooklyn in 1953, grew up on Long Island, trained at SUNY Buffalo (where he was deeply involved with the Hallwalls artist-run space, alongside Cindy Sherman and Charles Clough), and lives in New York. His Men in the Cities (1979–82, large monochrome charcoal drawings of writhing corporate-attire figures contorted as if shot or electrocuted) became iconic 1980s American image; his later large charcoal drawings — Untitled (Ferguson Police, August 13, 2014) (2014), the long Wave, Atomic Bomb, and Mountain series — apply the same photo-realistic charcoal technique to political flashpoints. The argument is that charcoal drawing at heroic painting scale, slowed to the duration of photography rendered by hand, is the medium most honestly suited to American political imagery — and that the contemporary press photograph, drawn in graphite, becomes a different kind of public document."
  },
  a699: { // Robert Mapplethorpe
    message: "Mapplethorpe was born in Floral Park, Queens in 1946 to a Catholic family, lived from 1969 onward in the Chelsea Hotel with Patti Smith (whose memoir Just Kids documents their relationship), and died of AIDS in 1989 at forty-two. His photographs — Self Portrait (1980, the cigar-smoking middle-finger one; the more famous bullwhip-anus 1978 self-portrait), the X Portfolio (1978, the controversial S&M and gay-male sexual imagery), the long Lisa Lyon bodybuilder portraits, the Flowers, the celebrity portraits (Lisa Lyon, Patti Smith, Andy Warhol) — combined classical Greco-Roman compositional rigour, formal black-and-white printing, and explicit sexual subject matter. The 1989 cancellation of his retrospective The Perfect Moment at the Corcoran Gallery in Washington, DC after Senator Jesse Helms's intervention became the central American culture-wars flashpoint. The estate-managed argument is that the classical formal rigour and the sexual content are not in tension — they were always meant to be inseparable parts of the same photograph."
  },
  a47: { // Robert Rauschenberg
    message: "Rauschenberg was born in Port Arthur, Texas in 1925 to working-class Cajun parents, trained at Black Mountain College under Josef Albers (where he met John Cage, Merce Cunningham, and Cy Twombly), and worked from a Lower Manhattan loft and then from Captiva Island, Florida until his death in 2008. His decisive Combines (1954–64) — Bed (1955, a stretched quilt and pillow vertical on the gallery wall, splashed with paint), Monogram (1955–59, an Angora goat with a tyre around its waist standing on a horizontal canvas) — collapsed painting and sculpture into a single category and prefigured nearly every subsequent materially promiscuous practice from Fluxus through installation art. The estate-managed argument, sustained by the Robert Rauschenberg Foundation since his death, is that postwar American art's most generative single innovation was the Combine — and that the conventional category boundaries between painting, sculpture, photography, and performance were the convention against which Rauschenberg's entire career was a polite, sustained refusal."
  },
  a918: { // Rudolf Stingel
    message: "Stingel was born in Merano in the South Tyrol in 1956, was trained at the Fine Arts Academy in Vienna, and lives between New York and Merano. His practice has been organised around a sustained interrogation of what painting is by means of substitution: large carpeted floors installed as paintings (Plan B, 2004), gallery walls upholstered in patterned wallpaper that visitors are invited to scratch into, large Styrofoam panels with their installation damage preserved as the work, and meticulously rendered photo-realist self-portraits — Untitled (After Sam) (2005), Untitled (Self-Portrait After Sam) (2006) — made from snapshots taken by his friend Sam Samore. The argument is that what makes a painting a painting is not the medium or the subject but the institutional placement — and that wallpaper, carpet, polystyrene, and snapshot can all be paintings if the gallery hosts them that way."
  },
  a966: { // Sarah Lucas
    message: "Lucas was born in Holloway, North London in 1962, was one of the founding generation of the Young British Artists (her degree show at Goldsmiths in 1987 included Damien Hirst), and lives in Suffolk. Her assemblages — Au Naturel (1994, a stained mattress with two melons and a bucket on one side and two oranges and a cucumber on the other), Self-Portrait with Fried Eggs (1996, the iconic photographic self-portrait with two fried eggs over her breasts), the Bunny series (1997 onward, stuffed-tights female-body forms slumped on chairs), the Nuds (2009 onward, knotted-tights organic body forms) — work with the cheapest available British working-class objects (cigarettes, tabloid newspapers, mattresses, tights stuffed with cotton wool) at the seam of gender, sex, and class. The argument is that the British working-class object is itself a sufficient feminist sculptural material — and that the assemblage made of cigarettes, eggs, and stockings is doing more honest work about gender than most monumentally feminist statements."
  },
  a166: { // Sasha Gordon
    message: "Gordon was born in New York in 1998 to a Korean-American family, trained at the Rhode Island School of Design (2020), and lives in New York. Her psychologically charged self-portraits — Surrogate Siren (2022), Bedfellow (2022) — depict herself multiple times in a single canvas, in distorted bodily configurations and uncanny domestic interiors painted in high-key colour and Old-Master-influenced rendering. The argument is that the queer Asian-American self-portrait is most honestly a multi-figure composition — that the identity is composed, not single, and the painting must register that compositional condition rather than smoothing it into one face."
  },
  a193: { // Sigmar Polke
    message: "Polke was born in Oels, Lower Silesia (now Oleśnica, Poland) in 1941, fled to Düsseldorf with his family in 1953, trained as a glass painter in a Düsseldorf church workshop before studying at the Kunstakademie under Karl Otto Götz and Gerhard Hoehme alongside Gerhard Richter, with whom he founded \"Capitalist Realism\" in 1963. His work — the Rasterbild dot-painted reproductions of cheap news media (1963 onward), the deliberately mediumistic Higher Beings Commanded: Paint the Upper Right Corner Black! (1969), the chemically unstable Watchtower paintings (1984–88, using uranium and other reactive materials), the late large translucent printed-fabric works — extended across painting, photography, alchemical experimentation, and printmaking. The estate-managed argument is that Polke's contribution was not a single signature style but a sustained methodological refusal — and that the painter who allows the materials to misbehave (to flake, to react, to disappear) is being more honest about what painting is than the painter who insists on craft control."
  },
  a12: { // Sterling Ruby
    message: "Ruby was born in 1972 on the US Bitburg Air Base in West Germany to American Air Force parents, grew up in rural Pennsylvania, trained at the Pennsylvania School of Art and Design and at the School of the Art Institute of Chicago before his MFA at Art Center College of Design, and lives in Los Angeles where he runs a Vernon studio with significant staff. His practice — the SP series (2008 onward, large spray-paint canvases), Vampire (2011), the long ceramic, fabric, fashion, and large welded-steel collaborations including the 2014 Calvin Klein menswear collection — collapses studio practice, fashion line, and ceramics into a single coherent visual brand. The argument is that the contemporary American studio is structurally indistinguishable from a fashion-and-design label — and that the artist who admits this and builds his practice around the cross-medium output is being more honest than the painter who pretends his studio is still the lone-genius workshop."
  },
  a468: { // Steve McQueen
    message: "McQueen was born in London in 1969 to Grenadian-Trinidadian parents, trained at Goldsmiths and briefly at the Tisch School at NYU, won the Turner Prize in 1999 (for Deadpan, 1997, a five-minute single-take homage to Buster Keaton in which the front of a house falls toward him and a single open window leaves him standing unharmed), represented Britain at the 1999 Venice Biennale, and won the Academy Award for Best Picture in 2014 for 12 Years a Slave. His later gallery and film works — Hunger (2008, his first feature, about Bobby Sands), Year 3 (2019, a portrait installation of every Year 3 child in London) — work between gallery installation and feature-length cinema. The argument is that the gallery video artist and the commercial feature filmmaker are not separable careers — and that the institutional distinction (festival cinema versus museum video) is one the work itself can refuse."
  },
  a4: { // Takashi Murakami
    message: "Murakami was born in Tokyo in 1962, holds a PhD in Nihonga (traditional Japanese painting) from Tokyo University of the Arts, founded the Hiropon Factory in 1996 (later Kaikai Kiki Co., Ltd, which now employs roughly 300 people across Tokyo and Long Island City), and operates his entire practice as an explicitly branded studio that issues paintings, sculptures, fashion collaborations (Louis Vuitton multicolor monogram from 2003), pop-music album covers (Kanye's Graduation), and a global anime gallery. His Superflat theory (2000) argued that contemporary Japanese visual culture (manga, anime, otaku consumer goods) and traditional Japanese painting share a common formal flatness — and that the boundary between high art, commercial illustration, and brand merchandise is a Western critical category that does not apply. The argument is that the post-war Japanese artist's actual contemporary medium is the brand — and that the painter who refuses to admit that is operating in a historical condition Murakami's generation has moved past."
  },
  a507: { // Tom Wesselmann
    message: "Wesselmann was born in Cincinnati in 1931, served in the US Army Korean-War period, trained in psychology and then at Cooper Union (where he switched from cartooning into fine art), and lived in New York until his death in 2004; he is one of the central American Pop artists alongside Warhol, Lichtenstein, Rosenquist, and Oldenburg. His Great American Nude series (1961 onward, particularly Great American Nude #57, 1964) and the Still Life series (Still Life #30, 1963) combine collage, painted canvas, shaped board, and three-dimensional commercial objects (an actual refrigerator door, an actual radiator) into compositions that push Pop's flatness into mixed-media installation. The estate-managed argument is that the canonical narrative of American Pop has under-credited Wesselmann's formal rigour — that his sensual, hard-edge erotic figures and meticulous commercial-object compositions are the medium's most quietly innovative formal contributions."
  },
  a25: { // Tracey Emin
    message: "Emin was born in Croydon, England in 1963 to a Turkish-Cypriot father and an English mother, grew up in Margate (a coastal English seaside town that she has returned to repeatedly in her work and where she now lives), trained at Maidstone College of Art and the Royal College of Art, and was a central figure of the Young British Artists. Her best-known works — Everyone I Have Ever Slept With 1963–1995 (1995, the appliquéd tent listing every name), My Bed (1998, her unmade bed and the surrounding personal detritus exhibited at the Turner Prize), I Promise to Love You (2012, neon text works) — work through autobiography, female sexuality, abortion, abuse, and confessional first-person language at confrontational scale. The argument, sustained for thirty years, is that the female artist's life — its abortions, its rapes, its sexual history, its bedridden weeks — is a sufficient and serious artistic subject, and that the polite refusal to render those experiences as material is itself a politics that needs refusing."
  },
  a10: { // Urs Fischer
    message: "Fischer was born in Zürich in 1973, trained as a commercial photographer at the Schule für Gestaltung Zürich, and lives in New York with a large fabrication studio in Brooklyn. His works — You (2007, a 38-foot pit dug out of the gallery floor at Gavin Brown's enterprise), Big Clay #4 (2013–14, monumental cast-aluminium clay forms), the long Untitled (Candle) (2001 onward, life-sized candle sculptures cast from human bodies and intended to melt down during the exhibition) — extend sculpture into the question of how the gallery space, the object's permanence, and the work's accidental properties relate. The argument is that the contemporary sculpture's medium includes the gallery floor itself, the slow burning candle, and the exhibition's duration — and that the sculpture which is not slowly being eaten by time is pretending about its actual relationship to it."
  },
  a194: { // Walter Price
    message: "Price was born in Macon, Georgia in 1989, served four years in the US Navy after high school, trained at the Maryland Institute College of Art, and lives in New York. His paintings — Pearl Lines (2018), Time Imagined the Line (2021) — combine personal narrative, Black American history, and abstract gestural composition in layered surfaces that move between figurative legibility and pure painterly mark in a single canvas. The argument is that the contemporary American Black painter is not required to choose between figurative legibility and abstract gesture — and that the painting's surface is itself the site where personal memory and historical inheritance can be held simultaneously."
  },

  // ── Chunk 7 ──

  a160: { // William Eggleston
    message: "Eggleston was born in Memphis, Tennessee in 1939, raised in Sumner, Mississippi, taught himself dye-transfer colour printing in the late 1960s after early black-and-white experiments, and lives in Memphis still. His 1976 MoMA exhibition — William Eggleston's Guide, curated by John Szarkowski — was the first solo colour-photography show at the museum and remade the medium's relationship to fine art; the dye-transfer plates of The Red Ceiling (1973) and the Los Alamos series (1965 onward) made the saturated colour of the American South into a legitimate documentary register. The argument is that colour, the property of the world the documentary tradition had refused to admit, is itself the medium's most under-credited honest material."
  },
  a461: { // William Kentridge
    message: "Kentridge was born in Johannesburg in 1955 to two anti-apartheid lawyers (his father defended families of victims at the Sharpeville and Steve Biko inquests), trained at the University of the Witwatersrand and briefly at Jacques Lecoq's mime school in Paris, and lives in Johannesburg. His charcoal-drawing animation method — drawing, photographing, erasing, redrawing on the same sheet — follows the fictional characters Soho Eckstein and Felix Teitlebaum through apartheid and post-apartheid South Africa across the Drawings for Projection series (1989 onward, including Johannesburg, 2nd Greatest City After Paris and Felix in Exile). His Rome Triumphs and Laments frieze (2016) on the Tiber wall and his More Sweetly Play the Dance procession videos extend the practice into civic-scale historical work. The argument is that the South African artist's responsibility is to the layered history beneath the present — and that charcoal, which can be erased and redrawn endlessly, is the only medium honest about how memory actually works."
  },
  a741: { // Wim Delvoye
    message: "Delvoye was born in Wervik, Belgium in 1965, trained at the Royal Academy of Fine Arts in Ghent, and lives in Brighton, England. His Cloaca (2000 onward) is a working ten-metre-long digestive machine that processes meals served to it daily and produces excrement that is signed and sold as artwork; his Art Farm in China (2003–10) raised pigs that had been tattooed at birth with elaborate motifs (Disney characters, Louis Vuitton monograms) and sold their skins after natural death. The argument is that the consumer-and-luxury-goods system's underlying materiality is bodily waste and animal flesh — and that the contemporary artist who treats those materials with the seriousness they deserve produces objects the market has not yet decided how to handle."
  },
  a210: { // Wolfgang Tillmans
    message: "Tillmans was born in Remscheid, West Germany in 1968, lived briefly in Hamburg before moving to Bournemouth, England in 1990 to study at the Bournemouth and Poole College of Art, and lives between Berlin and London. His Lutz & Alex sitting in the trees (1992, two friends naked in a tree, photographed for i-D magazine), the Concorde Grid (1997), the long abstract Freischwimmer cameraless prints (2003 onward), and the post-2016 Anti-Brexit campaign poster work extend across documentary, club photography, abstraction, scientific imaging, and direct political design. The argument, sustained for over thirty years and recognised by the 2000 Turner Prize (the first non-British and first photographer winner), is that photography's medium-categories — fashion, documentary, abstract, scientific, political — were institutional conventions, and the photographer who works across all of them is being more honest about how images actually circulate now."
  },
  a734: { // Yan Pei-Ming
    message: "Yan Pei-Ming was born in Shanghai in 1960, moved to France in 1980 to study at the École nationale supérieure des Beaux-Arts de Dijon (where he was rejected on his first application for being a foreign student), and lives in Dijon and Ivry-sur-Seine. His monumental black-and-white portraits — the long Mao series (1991 onward), Bruce Lee, Pope John Paul II, his own father, the International Landscape (2007 onward) — are painted with violent, athletic brushwork on three-metre-wide canvases, often with broom-sized brushes loaded with thinned oil. The argument is that the Chinese-born painter trained in the French academy is uniquely positioned to handle the twentieth-century political portrait — and that the gestural-painterly tradition of Manet through Soulages can hold a Mao image without flattening into either propaganda or ironic appropriation."
  },
  a173: { // Yayoi Kusama
    message: "Kusama was born in Matsumoto, Japan in 1929 to a wealthy and abusive seed-merchant family, began experiencing hallucinations of dots and net patterns covering surfaces in early childhood, moved to New York in 1957 (corresponding first with Georgia O'Keeffe), worked through the 1960s downtown scene alongside Andy Warhol and Donald Judd, returned to Japan in 1973, and has lived voluntarily in a Tokyo psychiatric hospital adjacent to her studio since 1977. Her decades-long output — the early Infinity Net paintings (1959 onward), the soft phallic Accumulations (1962 onward), Infinity Mirror Room — Phalli's Field (1965), the recent Dots Obsession installations and the long Pumpkin series (1981 onward) — sustain the same compulsive proposition. The argument is that the hallucinated visual pattern is not a defect of the artist's perception — it is the most honest available rendering of the world, and the artist's task is to deposit it consistently across whatever surface, body, or city is available."
  },
  a186: { // Yoshitomo Nara
    message: "Nara was born in Hirosaki, in Japan's northern Aomori Prefecture, in 1959, trained at Aichi University of the Arts and then at the Kunstakademie Düsseldorf under A.R. Penck (1988–93), and lives in Nasushiobara, Tochigi. His paintings of large-eyed, slightly menacing children — Knife Behind Back (2000), Miss Forest (2010), the long ongoing Untitled girl-and-dog series — derive equally from manga, the children's-book illustration of his isolated rural childhood, and the German Neo-Expressionist painting of the years he spent in Düsseldorf. The argument is that the post-1990s Japanese painter's most usable visual inheritance is not the high-art canon of Mono-ha or Gutai but the unauthorised, often pirated, often-cheap visual culture of the latchkey Japanese childhood — and that this inheritance, taken seriously, produces a register the Western canon was not prepared to receive."
  },

  // The remaining 43 in this chunk arrive with only one-line placeholder bios.
  // Where I have confident training-data knowledge I write substantively; where I do not I keep the entry conservative.

  a870: { // Aaron Garber-Maikovska — LOW-CONTEXT
    message: "Garber-Maikovska is an American artist born in 1978 and represented by Massimo De Carlo; he is best known for large-scale paintings on cardboard executed with intuitive gestural mark-making that appears to be drawn from movement and physical performance. Originally trained as an actor and dancer before turning to visual art, his pictorial vocabulary remains shaped by choreography and breath. The available record suggests an interest in the body's relation to the picture plane as a register of attention rather than depiction — but a fuller account awaits more documented exhibition writing."
  },
  a775: { // Adel Abdessemed
    message: "Abdessemed was born in Constantine, Algeria in 1971, trained at the École supérieure des Beaux-Arts d'Alger and at the École nationale supérieure des Beaux-Arts de Lyon, fled Algeria after the murder of one of his teachers during the country's 1990s civil war, and lives in Paris. His video, sculpture, and drawing — including the controversial 2008 documentation of slaughtered animals at a Marian Goodman exhibition, the wire-figure Coup de tête (2012, the Zidane head-butt as monumental bronze), and the burning helicopter painting Mappemonde — work in deliberately confrontational registers that test what the European gallery is willing to host. The argument is that the displaced North African artist's medium is the violence that produced the displacement — and that the gallery's willingness to refuse the work tells you more about the institution than about the artist."
  },
  a253: { // Adolph Gottlieb
    message: "Gottlieb was born in New York in 1903 to Czech Jewish immigrants and died there in 1974; he trained at the Art Students League and at Cooper Union and was a foundational member of the New York School alongside Mark Rothko (with whom he co-wrote the 1943 New York Times letter declaring the group's commitment to 'the simple expression of the complex thought'). His Pictographs (1941–51) divided the canvas into grid compartments containing primitivist and Surrealist-derived symbols; his later Bursts (1957 onward) reduced the composition to a single floating sun-disc above an explosive ground. The estate-managed argument is that the canonical narrative of Abstract Expressionism has under-credited the Pictographs as the movement's foundational moment — and that Gottlieb's reduction to the Burst format anticipates Color Field painting as much as Pollock or Rothko."
  },
  a32: { // Adriana Varejão
    message: "Varejão was born in Rio de Janeiro in 1964 and lives there; she is one of the central Brazilian painters of her generation. Her work — including the Saunas and Baths series of intricately tiled architectural interiors and the Charque (jerky) paintings that depict cracked tiled walls bursting open to reveal raw-flesh viscera — engages Portuguese colonial visual culture (especially the azulejo tile tradition that decorated Lusophone churches and palaces) as a wound visible at the level of the painted surface. The argument is that Brazilian visual culture's foundational decoration — the imported European tile — is also the country's foundational colonial document, and that the painting that splits open that surface is doing political work the unrestored architecture is no longer doing."
  },
  a462: { // Agnieszka Kurant
    message: "Kurant was born in Łódź, Poland in 1978, trained at the Académie des Beaux-Arts in Łódź and at Goldsmiths in London, and lives in New York. Her installations and sculptures — including A.A.I. (Artificial Artificial Intelligence) (2014–17, mounds of compressed industrial detritus produced by termites in collaboration with the artist), the Conversions stock-market sculptures, and the long End of Signature crowd-sourced fictional brand-name projects — treat non-human collective intelligence (termite colonies, financial markets, online crowds) as legitimate sculptural collaborators. The argument is that contemporary value is increasingly produced by non-human collective systems — and that the sculpture honest about that condition takes the collective as the maker, with the artist as the framer."
  },
  a382: { // Al Held
    message: "Held was born in Brooklyn in 1928 and died in Italy in 2005; he trained at the Art Students League and at the Académie de la Grande Chaumière in Paris on the GI Bill, and taught at Yale from 1962 to 1980 where he was a significant figure for generations of younger painters. His career divided into two distinct halves: the heavily impastoed, hard-edged geometric paintings of the 1950s and 1960s, and the deep-perspective architectural-grid paintings (Mantegna's Last Supper, the Brughes series, the long Italian-Renaissance-perspective explorations) of his last three decades. The estate-managed argument is that twentieth-century American abstraction did not need to choose between flat surface and Renaissance perspective — and that the painter who reinstates spatial illusion using the geometric vocabulary of minimalism is producing a third option both traditions had to suppress."
  },
  a208: { // Al Taylor
    message: "Taylor was born in Springfield, Missouri in 1948, trained at the Kansas City Art Institute, and lived in New York from 1970 until his death from cancer in 1999 at fifty. He worked as Robert Rauschenberg's studio assistant in the 1970s before establishing his own practice; the assemblages he made from broomsticks, plastic-bottle caps, plumbing fixtures, and his daughter's discarded children's-toy parts — including the Pet Stains (1989–) and the long Hanging Puddles series — turn from drawing into sculpture and back again, often within the same wall-mounted piece. The estate-managed argument is that the late-twentieth-century American sculptural tradition between Eva Hesse, Bruce Nauman, and Richard Tuttle had a fourth quieter strand — visible only after Taylor's untimely death — that took the studio-bench's accidental composition as seriously as any monumental statement."
  },
  a41: { // Alberto Giacometti
    message: "Giacometti was born in Borgonovo, Switzerland in 1901 to the post-Impressionist painter Giovanni Giacometti, trained briefly at the École des Beaux-Arts de Genève before moving to Paris in 1922 to study under Antoine Bourdelle, and worked from a small studio at 46 rue Hippolyte-Maindron in Montparnasse until his death in 1966. His best-known sculptures — the post-1945 elongated standing figures (Walking Man, Standing Woman, Tall Figure), the Palace at 4 a.m. (1932), the early Surrealist works, the Annette and Diego portrait sculptures and drawings — emerge from a continuous studio rebuilding of the human figure at the point where it becomes nearly nothing without disappearing. The estate-managed argument is that twentieth-century European sculpture's most consequential single body of work was made in one cramped Paris studio across forty years, and that the act of looking at a human figure standing still — sustained for that long — is itself sculpture's most under-credited subject."
  },
  a578: { // Alexandre Singh
    message: "Singh was born in Bordeaux in 1980 to a French mother and an Indian father, trained at the Ruskin School at Oxford and at the Cooper Union, and lives in New York. His work — including the long Assembly Instructions lecture-performances, the Causeries (long monologue performances delivered in the voice of an art-historical character), and the Witches puppet-opera (2013) — combine art-historical narrative, Greek philosophical dialogue, and stand-up performance into multi-hour works that resist the contemporary art world's expectation of brief encounters. The argument is that the contemporary artist can still be a long-form narrative producer — and that the gallery's typical brief-attention environment is a curatorial convention rather than the medium's natural limit."
  },
  a266: { // Alfred Jensen
    message: "Jensen was born in Guatemala City in 1903 to a Danish-German father and a Polish mother, trained in San Diego and at the Académie Scandinave in Paris under Charles Despiau, and lived in New York and Glen Ridge, NJ until his death in 1981. His paintings from the late 1950s onward — the Number Paintings, the Square Beginning–Cyclic Ending grids, the Per the Diagram of Numbers — combine the I Ching, Mayan numerology, Pythagorean cosmology, and the colour theory of Goethe into thick impasto grid compositions of primary colour and gold leaf. The estate-managed argument is that the New York School had a hidden second-generation member working from non-Western numerical systems — and that Jensen's posthumous reinstatement as a serious cosmological-systems painter remains incomplete."
  },
  a660: { // Ali Banisadr
    message: "Banisadr was born in Tehran in 1976, was a child during the Iran-Iraq War (an experience he has cited as foundational to his synaesthetic relationship between sound and image), emigrated to the United States as a teenager, trained at the School of Visual Arts and at the New York Academy of Art, and lives in Brooklyn. His paintings — including the long Foreign Lands and Trust in the Future series — combine large dense compositions of small distributed figures, mythological and historical references (Bosch, Brueghel, the Persian miniature tradition), and an explicit synaesthetic relation between the painter's auditory experience and the colour-and-mark composition. The argument is that the contemporary painter who came of age in war is making, knowingly or not, history painting in the sense Delacroix would have recognised — and that Persian miniature, Bosch, and Brueghel were already in dialogue with that subject."
  },
  a357: { // Alia Ahmad — LOW-CONTEXT
    message: "Ahmad is a Saudi painter born in Riyadh in 1996; she is among the youngest artists on the White Cube roster and represents a recent generation of Saudi contemporary painters working at the intersection of Arabic calligraphic tradition and large-scale abstract painting. Her canvases work in saturated palettes that draw on the Nejd landscape and on the textile and tilework of Saudi domestic interiors. A fuller account of her practice's stated argument and lineage awaits more critical-press documentation."
  },
  a187: { // Alice Neel
    message: "Neel was born in Merion Square, Pennsylvania in 1900 and lived in Spanish Harlem and Greenwich Village in New York until her death in 1984; she trained at the Philadelphia School of Design for Women and worked through the 1930s WPA in conditions of severe poverty and personal crisis (her first daughter died, her second was taken from her by her husband's family). Her portraits — including Andy Warhol (1970, scarred from Valerie Solanas's shooting), the long series of pregnant nude friends, her Harlem neighbours, art-world figures, and her own elderly self (Self-Portrait, 1980, painted at eighty in glasses and seated, naked) — were ignored by the canonical narrative of postwar American painting for almost forty years. The estate-managed argument is that the figurative female portraitist was systematically excluded from the New York School's canon — and that her quiet sixty-year career, sustained without market or institutional support, is now the canon's most necessary corrective."
  },
  a275: { // Alicja Kwade
    message: "Kwade was born in Katowice, Poland in 1979, moved to Germany at age eight after the fall of communism, trained at the Universität der Künste Berlin, and lives in Berlin. Her sculptures — including the long ParaPivot series (2019, the Metropolitan Museum's rooftop commission of pivoting steel frames holding planet-like stones), Linienland, and the WeltenLinie mirror-and-object installations — work with mirrors, doppelganger objects, and the apparent transformation of materials (a stone becoming wood, an object becoming its reflection) in compositions that test the viewer's perception of substance and place. The argument is that the contemporary subject's relation to material reality has been quietly destabilised by physics and by digital experience — and that sculpture's job is to make that destabilisation literal at the scale of the body."
  },
  a520: { // Alighiero Boetti
    message: "Boetti was born in Turin in 1940 and died in Rome in 1994; he was a central figure of Arte Povera who, in 1972, doubled his name to Alighiero e Boetti (Alighiero and Boetti) to mark the artist as plural rather than singular. His best-known works — the long Mappa series (1971 onward, large embroidered maps of the world in which each country's flag fills its outline, executed in Afghanistan by Afghan women embroiderers across two decades and through war), the Cento by Mille Postcards (1973–74), the Aerei (Airplanes) drawings — distribute the work across collaborators (his wife Anne-Marie Sauzeau, Afghan embroiderers, his Italian assistants, his Pakistani interlocutors after the Soviet invasion). The estate-managed argument is that the artist is a coordinator rather than a maker — and that the Afghan-embroidered map, sustained across the Soviet invasion, the Mujahideen war, and the post-1979 Pakistan refugee period, is one of late-twentieth-century art's most consequential cross-cultural collaborations."
  },
  a106: { // Alina Szapocznikow
    message: "Szapocznikow was born in Kalisz, Poland in 1926 to a Polish-Jewish family, survived the Łódź, Auschwitz, Bergen-Belsen, and Theresienstadt camps as a teenager, trained at the Académie des Beaux-Arts in Prague and at the École nationale supérieure des Beaux-Arts in Paris, and died of breast cancer in 1973 at forty-six. Her sculptures from the 1960s onward — the Lampes-Bouches (Mouth Lamps, 1966 onward, lit lamps in the form of her own lips set on slender bronze stems), the resin Tumeurs (Tumours, 1969–71, cast from her own diseased flesh), the Photosculptures of chewed gum and bread — work with the body's surfaces as casts from her own life. The estate-managed argument, sustained over the last twenty years by the Hauser & Wirth estate-management of her work, is that European post-war sculpture's missing major figure was a Jewish woman who had survived four camps and was making sculpture from her own dying body — and that the canonical narrative did not have the categories to register what she was doing."
  },
  a121: { // Allan Kaprow
    message: "Kaprow was born in Atlantic City, NJ in 1927 to a department-store family, trained under Hans Hofmann and John Cage (the latter at the New School for Social Research), and taught at Rutgers, Stony Brook, and finally UC San Diego until his death in 2006. His decisive 1959 18 Happenings in 6 Parts at the Reuben Gallery in New York gave the term 'Happening' to a new artistic form — participatory, scripted, ephemeral events in which the audience and the gathered participants were equally the work; his later 'activities' (Comfort Zones, Routine, Trading Dirt) reduced the scale further into deliberately ordinary daily structured tasks. The estate-managed argument is that twentieth-century art's most consequential expansion was not minimalism or pop but the dissolution of the art-versus-life boundary — and that Kaprow's deliberately mundane later activities are not minor compared to the early Happenings but their logical continuation."
  },
  a491: { // Allan Sekula
    message: "Sekula was born in Erie, Pennsylvania in 1951 and died in 2013; he trained at UC San Diego (where he studied with Herbert Marcuse) and at CalArts, taught at CalArts for over thirty years, and worked as both a photographer and a critical theorist whose 1978 essay 'Dismantling Modernism, Reinventing Documentary' is foundational for late-twentieth-century photography theory. His major photographic projects — Fish Story (1989–95, large-format documentary of global shipping containerisation), Black Tide (2002–03, oil-spill documentation), Polonia and Other Tales (1993–2009) — work in long-form research-and-documentary mode with extended written component. The estate-managed argument is that the documentary photograph, sustained at book length with serious accompanying text, is the medium's properly political form — and that the gallery's preference for single-image hangings has consistently misread what photographers like Sekula were trying to make."
  },
  a112: { // Allison Katz
    message: "Katz was born in Montreal in 1980, trained at McGill, the School of the Art Institute of Chicago, and Columbia University (MFA), and lives in London. Her paintings — including the Roosters, Reads, Lassies, and Apples series — work in a flat, deliberately stylised figurative idiom that quotes from late-modernist illustration, advertising, and the artist's own initials (her A appears as a recurring motif). The argument is that the contemporary painter can take the modernist signature itself — the artist's mark, the gallery logo, the painted A — as a substantive painted subject, and that this self-quotation, sustained across an entire body of work, becomes a coherent painterly proposition rather than a stylistic affectation."
  },
  a662: { // Alvaro Barrington — LOW-CONTEXT
    message: "Barrington was born in Caracas, Venezuela in 1983 to a Caribbean family, was raised between Grenada and Brooklyn, and is now based in London; he trained at Hunter College and at the Slade. His paintings, often on burlap, postcards, or fabric stretched around concrete or yarn, combine references to Caribbean diasporic memory, hip-hop, and post-war abstract painting. He was awarded the Notting Hill Carnival 2024 commission, which gave his practice its largest civic platform. The argument, still emerging into critical reception, is that the contemporary painting can be a site for assembling diasporic material objects — and that the painting's structural support (burlap, concrete, yarn) carries as much meaning as the painted surface."
  },
  a459: { // Amar Kanwar
    message: "Kanwar was born in New Delhi in 1964, trained in history at St Stephen's College, Delhi and in film at the Mass Communication Research Centre at Jamia Millia Islamia, and lives in New Delhi. His long-form film projects — A Season Outside (1997, his decisive Wagah-Attari India-Pakistan border film), the Sovereign Forest (2011 onward, ongoing collaboration with tribal and farmer communities in Odisha resisting industrial mining), The Lightning Testimonies (2007) — work at the seam of documentary film, contemporary art installation, and direct political collaboration with the communities he films. The argument is that the long-form documentary film, installed in the gallery space with seating and time for full viewing, is the medium most adequate to the political weight of the post-colonial Indian rural condition — and that the gallery's hurried viewing assumption is what most film art has had to refuse."
  },
  a681: { // Amos Gitaï
    message: "Gitaï was born in Haifa in 1950 to the architect Munio Weinraub (a Bauhaus graduate and student of Mies van der Rohe), trained as an architect at the Technion in Haifa and at UC Berkeley, and turned to film after being injured in the 1973 Yom Kippur War. His feature films and gallery installations — including the long Wadi trilogy (1981, 1991, 2001, returning to the same Wadi Rushmiya neighbourhood in Haifa across two decades), the architecturally rigorous House trilogy (1980, 1998, 2005, on a single Jerusalem house through Israeli-Palestinian historical periods), and the more recent Letter to a Friend in Gaza — work at the seam of architectural documentation and political testimony. The argument is that architectural training, sustained over four decades into film and gallery installation, gives the artist a slow, structural relationship to political subject matter that the photo-journalistic register cannot deliver."
  },
  a87: { // Amy Sherald
    message: "Sherald was born in Columbus, Georgia in 1973, trained at Clark Atlanta University and the Maryland Institute College of Art, and lives in Baltimore. Her portraits — painted exclusively of Black American sitters with grey-rendered skin against flat saturated coloured backgrounds — work in a deliberately quiet figurative register that draws on the Bo Bartlett and Grant Wood American figurative tradition while substantively rejecting its racial assumptions. Her 2018 official portrait of First Lady Michelle Obama (commissioned by the National Portrait Gallery) made her one of the central American painters of her generation. The argument is that the grey-rendered skin tone is not a chromatic curiosity — it is a deliberate refusal of skin as the painting's first racial signal, and that the portrait can render Blackness through stillness, posture, and clothing without the painting having to perform the racial identification."
  },
  a464: { // An-My Lê
    message: "Lê was born in Saigon in 1960, fled with her family as a teenager during the 1975 evacuation, was resettled in the United States, trained at Stanford and at the Yale MFA, and lives in New York and Vermont where she teaches at Bard. Her large-format photographic projects — Small Wars (1999–2002, US Civil War battle-reenactors), 29 Palms (2003–04, US Marine training exercises in Mojave desert), Events Ashore (2005–14, US Navy operations across the Pacific) — document the rehearsal and execution of American military action with the formal compositional rigour of nineteenth-century landscape photography. The argument is that the Vietnamese-born American photographer is uniquely positioned to document the American military's contemporary global operations — and that the formal large-format tradition is the right register for that subject because it cannot pretend to embedded immediacy."
  },
  a470: { // Ana Mendieta
    message: "Mendieta was born in Havana in 1948, was sent to the United States in 1961 at age twelve as part of Operation Peter Pan (a Catholic-organised emigration of Cuban children after the Revolution), grew up in Iowa foster care, trained at the University of Iowa under Hans Breder, and died at thirty-six in 1985 after falling from a 34th-floor window in the New York apartment she shared with the minimalist sculptor Carl Andre (whose 1988 trial for her death ended in acquittal in a verdict the art world has never fully accepted). Her Siluetas (1973–80) — body-shaped impressions in mud, grass, sand, gunpowder, and flowers, photographed and filmed across landscapes in Iowa, Mexico, and Cuba — are her major work. The estate-managed argument is that Cuban-diasporic feminist body art is the foundational tradition the canonical narrative under-credited — and that Mendieta's death and its prosecutorial handling are inseparable from the question of how the canon does and does not register the work."
  },
  a571: { // Analia Saban
    message: "Saban was born in Buenos Aires in 1980, trained at Loyola University New Orleans and at UCLA (MFA), and lives in Los Angeles. Her work — including the Marble Slab paintings (paint poured directly onto raw slabs of marble), the woven canvas thread compositions (canvas threads physically rearranged into geometric patterns), the Pleated Ink series — investigates painting as a physical-material object at the level of its constituent parts (canvas weave, stretcher bar, paint as substance). The argument is that the contemporary painter's task can be the disassembly of painting into its component parts — and that revealing how a painting is structurally made is itself a serious painted subject."
  },
  a452: { // Andrea Fraser
    message: "Fraser was born in Billings, Montana in 1965, trained at the School of Visual Arts and at the Whitney Independent Study Program, and teaches at UCLA. Her performance-and-video practice — Museum Highlights: A Gallery Talk (1989, in which she gave a 30-minute tour of the Philadelphia Museum of Art as Jane Castleton, a museum docent character delivering deadpan ideological critique), Untitled (2003, a one-hour video recording of her having sex with a collector who had paid $20,000 for the encounter), Official Welcome (2001) — extend institutional critique into the artist's own body and sexual labour. The argument is that the artist's work is structurally inseparable from the conditions of the art market — and that the artist who refuses to make those conditions visible is participating in their erasure."
  },
  a575: { // Andreas Schulze
    message: "Schulze was born in Hannover in 1955, trained at the Düsseldorf Kunstakademie under Gerhard Hoehme and Joseph Beuys, and lives in Cologne. His large paintings — including the long Brutmaschinen (Brood Machines), Stillleben (Still Life), and Möbel (Furniture) series — depict pillowy, cartoon-rounded everyday objects (sofas, beds, lamps, garden hoses, telephones) in deliberately limited colour palettes and on architecturally large scale. The argument is that the postwar German painter trained under Beuys had a choice between Beuysian social-sculpture seriousness and a deliberately funny, decorative, almost-children's-illustration register — and that the second choice, sustained over four decades, produces paintings the institutional reception is only now learning how to read."
  },
  a723: { // Andreas Slominski
    message: "Slominski was born in Meppen, Germany in 1959, trained at the Hochschule für bildende Künste Hamburg, and lives in Hamburg. His best-known works are large, mechanically intricate animal traps (his Tierfallen series, 1980s onward) — beautifully crafted, fully functional traps for fox, marten, rabbit, mole — installed in galleries as sculptural objects, with no animal ever caught. His later works extend the same logic: a bicycle ridden in elaborate impossible routes around an exhibition opening, a chimney sweep dressed in soot-blackened uniform delivered to museum administrators. The argument is that contemporary sculpture's medium is the apparatus rather than the object — and that the unsprung trap, perfect and unused, is a more honest sculpture than any taxidermied prey."
  },
  a590: { // Andro Wekua
    message: "Wekua was born in Sukhumi, Abkhazia, Soviet Georgia in 1977; his father, an Abkhazian official, disappeared during the 1992–93 war that ended Wekua's childhood and ethnically cleansed the city; the artist and his mother fled and he trained at the Tbilisi State Academy of Arts and at the Visual Arts School in Basel. He lives in Berlin. His paintings, sculptures, and architectural-model installations — including the dance figures, the long Pink Wave Hunter wax-and-aluminium sculptures, and the photo-collage paintings — return obsessively to the lost Sukhumi childhood and the architecture of the disappeared city. The argument is that the displaced post-Soviet artist's medium is the reconstructed memory of a city that no longer exists — and that the painting and sculpture together can host the kind of slow rebuilding that no documentary photograph can deliver."
  },
  a113: { // Angel Otero
    message: "Otero was born in Santurce, Puerto Rico in 1981 (his grandmother raised him), trained at the School of the Art Institute of Chicago, and lives in Brooklyn. His decisive technique — painting an image onto a sheet of glass or other release surface in oil paint, allowing the paint to fully dry into an oil-skin sheet, peeling that sheet, and then arranging the dried sheets across a canvas — turns the painting into a textile-like collage of pre-painted oil flesh. The argument is that the contemporary painter inherits the medium as already-historical material (the oil-paint sheet, already painted before the artist gets to it) — and that the painter's job is to compose with that pre-existing painterly material rather than to invent paintings from blank surfaces."
  },
  a350: { // Anicka Yi
    message: "Yi was born in Seoul in 1971 and was raised in Alabama; she had no formal art training, worked for years in advertising and fashion (including at the agency BBDO), and turned to art-making in her thirties. She lives in New York. Her installations — including 7,070,430K of Digital Spit (2015, agar petri dishes growing bacteria collected from one hundred women's swabs), Biologizing the Machine (2019 Venice Biennale, suspended kelp-and-LED light works), In Love with the World (2021 Tate Turbine Hall, autonomous floating aerocene 'machines') — work with bacteria, scent, biotechnology, and AI as sculptural collaborators. The argument is that the contemporary sensorium has been radically narrowed by the visual-dominant gallery system — and that the artist's task is to reintroduce smell, microbial life, and algorithmic agency as legitimate sculptural material."
  },
  a92: { // Anj Smith
    message: "Smith was born in Kent, England in 1978, trained at Slade School of Fine Art (BA) and at the Royal Academy Schools (postgraduate), and lives in London. Her small-scale dense paintings — depicting hybrid figures part-human and part-plant, draped landscapes with carefully painted insect detail, and elaborate jewelled costumes — work in a register that recalls Northern Renaissance painting, Lucas Cranach, and the seventeenth-century Dutch tradition of still life as veiled allegory. The argument is that the small intimate canvas, painted at slow figurative scale with botanical precision, is a serious contemporary form — and that the unfashionable choice of slowness is itself the contemporary statement."
  },
  a109: { // Anna Maria Maiolino
    message: "Maiolino was born in Scalea, Calabria, Italy in 1942, emigrated to Venezuela with her family at age twelve, then moved to Brazil in 1960 where she has lived since; she trained at the Escola Nacional de Belas Artes in Rio. Her practice — from the early 1960s woodcuts and the Buraco Negro (Black Hole) clay sculpture installations through her current ongoing clay-cylinder accumulations — works across woodcut printing, drawing, super-8 film, performance, and unfired clay. Her early Polo Negativo Em (1964) and her food-bound 1981 In/Out (Antropofagia) confront Brazilian cultural-political conditions through the body. The argument is that the immigrant Latin American woman artist who has lived through Italian Fascism, Venezuelan dictatorship, and Brazilian military rule is uniquely positioned to make work about displacement, hunger, and silence — and that unfired clay, which can return to dust, is its proper medium."
  },
  a539: { // Anne Imhof
    message: "Imhof was born in Gießen, Germany in 1978, worked as a doorperson at a Frankfurt nightclub for years (an experience she has cited as foundational), trained at the Städelschule in Frankfurt under Judith Hopf, and lives between Frankfurt and Paris. Her decisive work, Faust (2017, the German Pavilion at the Venice Biennale, awarded the Golden Lion), staged a five-hour multi-day performance with twenty performers in a glass-floored interior, with Dobermans patrolling the courtyard beneath. Subsequent works — Sex (2019), Natures Mortes (2021) at the Palais de Tokyo, Wish You Were Gay (2024) — extend the same combination of nightclub aesthetic, fashion-styled performers, and long-duration ambient performance. The argument is that contemporary performance's most adequate medium is the post-2010 European nightclub — and that the long-duration ensemble work, with the audience never sure where to stand, is the form most honest about the present-day subject's condition."
  },
  a140: { // Anni Albers
    message: "Albers was born Annelise Fleischmann in Berlin in 1899 to an upper-middle-class Jewish family, trained at the Bauhaus from 1922 (where she met Josef Albers, whom she married in 1925), was confined to the weaving workshop (the only Bauhaus department open to women), and emigrated to the United States in 1933 after the Nazis closed the Bauhaus. She taught at Black Mountain College from 1933 to 1949 (where Robert Rauschenberg, Cy Twombly, Ruth Asawa, and many others were her students), and lived in Connecticut until her death in 1994. Her weavings, prints, and theoretical writings (On Weaving, 1965; On Designing, 1959) re-positioned textile from craft-tradition to a serious modernist medium. The estate-managed argument is that twentieth-century modernist abstraction's most consequential under-credited figure was a Bauhaus woman who was confined to the weaving workshop and turned that confinement into the foundation of postwar American art-school textile education."
  },
  a114: { // Annie Leibovitz
    message: "Leibovitz was born in Waterbury, Connecticut in 1949 to a Jewish-American military family that moved repeatedly during her childhood, trained at the San Francisco Art Institute, and worked as chief photographer for Rolling Stone (1973–83) before moving to Vanity Fair and now Vogue. Her best-known photographs — the December 1980 Rolling Stone cover of a naked John Lennon curled around a clothed Yoko Ono (shot five hours before Lennon's murder), the 1991 Vanity Fair cover of the pregnant nude Demi Moore, the long portraits of American presidents, actors, dancers, and writers — built the dominant visual language of late-twentieth-century American celebrity photography. The argument, sustained over fifty years and now into the gallery context with the Hauser & Wirth representation, is that the magazine portrait is not a lesser medium than the fine-art photograph — and that the editorial deadline and the celebrity sitter's brief availability are working conditions that produce a kind of photograph nothing else can."
  },
  a487: { // Anri Sala
    message: "Sala was born in Tirana, Albania in 1974 to a family of communist intellectuals (his mother was a Marxist scholar), trained at the National Academy of Arts in Tirana and at the École nationale supérieure des Arts Décoratifs in Paris, and lives in Berlin. His decisive early work, Intervista — Finding the Words (1998), found a Super-8 film of his mother giving a 1977 Albanian Communist Party youth speech with the sound missing; he hired lip-readers and used reconstruction to retrieve the words. His later films — Time After Time (2003), Long Sorrow (2005, the long single take of Brooklyn jazz saxophonist Jemeel Moondoc playing from a Berlin high-rise window), Ravel Ravel Unravel (2013, the French Pavilion at the Venice Biennale) — work at the seam of film, music, and architectural space. The argument is that the post-communist artist's relation to inherited language is one of careful retrieval — and that film is the medium that can perform that retrieval visibly, in time."
  },
  a554: { // Anthony McCall
    message: "McCall was born in St Paul's Cray, England in 1946, trained at the Ravensbourne College of Art and Design and at the Royal College of Art, lived in New York from 1973 onward (with a fifteen-year period away from film during which he worked as a graphic designer), and is now based in New York. His decisive 1973 Line Describing a Cone (a 16mm film of a single line of light traced into a slow circle, projected into a smoke-filled room, so that the cone of light itself became a sculptural object) opened the medium of 'solid-light film' that he has developed continuously since (Five Minutes of Pure Sculpture, 2012; the long Doubling Back series). The argument is that film's actual sculptural medium is the cone of light between projector and screen, not the image on the screen — and that the artist who insists on this proposition for fifty years is producing the longest continuous investigation of a single sculptural idea in the contemporary field."
  },
  a335: { // Antoni Tàpies
    message: "Tàpies was born in Barcelona in 1923 to an upper-middle-class Catalan family, lived through the Spanish Civil War as a teenager and contracted tuberculosis (his two-year recovery, in his telling, gave him his serious painting commitment), trained briefly at the Escuela de Bellas Artes de Sant Jordi, co-founded the Dau-al-Set group in 1948, and lived in Barcelona until his death in 2012. His work — the marble-dust, sand, latex, and earth-mixed paintings of the 1950s onward, the Catalan-cross and four-bar (the four bars of the Catalan flag) recurring motifs, the wall-cast object sculptures, the long ongoing Catalan Anti-Franco political work — built a single coherent post-war Catalan visual register. The estate-managed argument is that the Catalan painter's medium is the wall itself — sand, lime, marble dust, soil — and that the painting is a built object whose surface registers political, spiritual, and personal mark with equal weight."
  },
  a604: { // Antonio Calderara
    message: "Calderara was born in Abbiategrasso, near Milan, in 1903 and lived on Lake Orta in northern Italy until his death in 1978; he trained briefly as an engineer before turning to painting in his twenties and remained largely self-directed. His paintings — small, often square, of carefully calibrated horizontal-and-vertical colour fields in narrow grey-blue-pink palettes derived from the specific light of Lake Orta — work in a register of slow contemplative reduction that anticipates and runs parallel to Italian Spazialismo and the European hard-edge tradition. The estate-managed argument is that the regional Italian painter, working far from Milan or Rome, sustained over fifty years a contribution to mid-century European abstraction that the centralised canonical narrative is now correcting — and that the lake's particular light is itself the painting's subject."
  },
  a430: { // Arakawa
    message: "Shusaku Arakawa was born in Nagoya, Japan in 1936, moved to New York in 1961 with $14 in his pocket (a story he later mythologised), trained briefly under Marcel Duchamp's influence, and lived in New York with the writer Madeline Gins as his lifelong collaborator until his death in 2010. His paintings, drawings, and diagrams — the long Mechanism of Meaning project (1963–73, a co-authored book and exhibition with Gins consisting of eighty-three painted diagrams testing language, perception, and the body), the later Reversible Destiny philosophical project — sit at the seam of conceptual art, philosophy of mind, and built architecture (the Reversible Destiny Lofts in Mitaka, Tokyo, and the Reversible Destiny Park in Yoro). The estate-managed argument, sustained by the Reversible Destiny Foundation, is that the artist's work is properly understood not as paintings or buildings but as a multi-decade philosophical investigation of whether human mortality is itself a built convention that can be re-designed."
  },
  a828: { // Arcangelo Sassolino
    message: "Sassolino was born in Vicenza, Italy in 1967, trained as an engineer before turning to art, and lives in Vicenza. His mechanical sculptures — including AAA (2005, a steel ball repeatedly slammed against a steel plate by hydraulic pressure), Untitled (Solo) (2018, a single steel block hovering on a magnetic field), Memoria (2022, the Maltese Pavilion at the Venice Biennale — a piece in which molten iron drips from a steel beam into pools of water below, with the sound of solidifying metal as part of the work) — work at industrial-engineering scale and the threshold of structural failure. The argument is that contemporary sculpture has an under-credited mechanical-engineering tradition — and that the work that operates at the boundary of its own destruction (the hydraulic press, the dripping molten metal) is registering the literal physical condition the gallery has trained itself to deny."
  },

  // ── Chunk 8 ──

  a326: { // Arlene Shechet
    message: "Shechet was born in New York in 1951, trained at NYU and Rhode Island School of Design, and lives between Manhattan and the Hudson Valley. Her ceramics, plaster, and welded-steel works — including the long Buddha series, the multi-coloured Bumps and Forms wood-and-glaze pieces, and the recent steel-and-painted-wood Skirts — combine traditional Eastern Buddhist iconography with a deliberately rough, off-balance contemporary handling. The argument is that sculpture in clay and ceramic has been treated as a lesser medium by the post-minimal canon — and that the artist who refuses smoothness and stability is doing more honest work about what bodies and forms actually are."
  },
  a431: { // Arman
    message: "Arman was born Armand Pierre Fernandez in Nice, France in 1928 and died in New York in 2005; he trained at the École nationale supérieure des Beaux-Arts in Nice and co-founded Nouveau Réalisme in 1960 alongside Yves Klein, Pierre Restany, and others. His Accumulations (1959 onward) — vitrine displays of dozens or hundreds of identical objects (toothbrushes, gas masks, paint tubes, telephones) — and his Colères (Rages) of smashed and reassembled instruments built the dominant 1960s French response to American Pop. The estate-managed argument is that the post-war French artist's relationship to the consumer object was philosophically distinct from the American one — sociological rather than celebratory — and that the accumulated identical object is a more rigorous proposition about mass production than the painted soup can."
  },
  a712: { // Arnulf Rainer
    message: "Rainer was born in Baden, Austria in 1929, lives in Enzenkirchen, and is one of the central figures of Austrian post-war painting and performance. His decisive Übermalungen (Overpaintings, 1953 onward) — found photographs, his own paintings, art-historical reproductions, then his own and others' faces overpainted with thick gestural black and red — and his later Face Farces (self-photographs grimacing under heavy paint) sustained a single proposition across seven decades. The argument is that the painted image's relationship to its original is one of suppression rather than reproduction, and that the most honest gesture available to a painter is to obliterate what is already there."
  },
  a115: { // Arshile Gorky
    message: "Gorky was born Vostanik Manoug Adoian in Khorgom, Ottoman Empire (now eastern Turkey) c. 1904, survived the Armenian Genocide as a child (his mother died of starvation in his arms in Yerevan in 1919), emigrated to the United States in 1920, took the name Arshile Gorky and a constructed Russian identity, and killed himself in Connecticut in 1948 at forty-four. His paintings — The Liver is the Cock's Comb (1944), Agony (1947), the long Garden in Sochi series (1940–43, a remembered Armenian-childhood landscape) — bridge Surrealism and Abstract Expressionism by means of a biomorphic line drawn equally from Miró, Picasso, and the Armenian khatchkar cross-stone tradition. The estate-managed argument is that the foundational figure of New York Abstract Expressionism was an Armenian genocide survivor whose work cannot be read without that biography — and that the constructed-identity assumed-name strategy is itself one of the medium's central subjects."
  },
  a541: { // Arthur Jafa
    message: "Jafa was born in Tupelo, Mississippi in 1960, trained as an architect at Howard University, worked for two decades as a cinematographer (including on Julie Dash's Daughters of the Dust in 1991, for which he developed the 'Black visual intonation' technique), and turned fully to gallery video art in the 2010s. His Love is the Message, the Message is Death (2016, a seven-minute montage of Black American experience set to Kanye West's Ultralight Beam) was bought and donated simultaneously to thirteen American museums by a coalition of collectors. The argument is that Black American visual culture has its own time-signature, its own colour temperature, its own rhythmic logic — and that the artist's task is to find the editing grammar that lets the medium register it."
  },
  a546: { // Astrid Klein
    message: "Klein was born in Köln in 1951, trained at the Hochschule für Bildende Künste in Kassel, and lives between Cologne and Lake Como. Her photo-and-text works from the late 1970s onward — typically very large dark photo-collages with single sentences of philosophical or political German text overlaid — emerged alongside the Pictures Generation in New York but in a distinctly German Cold War register that engaged Frankfurt School theory directly. The argument is that the contemporary photo-text work is not the design-derived register of American Pictures-Generation work — it is a philosophical-political medium that the post-war German artist has access to in a way the American does not."
  },
  a122: { // August Sander
    message: "Sander was born in Herdorf, in Germany's Westerwald region, in 1876, trained as a commercial portrait photographer, and worked until his death in 1964; his life-project People of the 20th Century (Menschen des 20. Jahrhunderts) photographed citizens of Weimar Germany organised typologically by occupation and class (farmer, civil servant, artist, judge, unemployed). The Nazis destroyed the printing plates of his 1929 Antlitz der Zeit (Face of Our Time) book; his son Erich was imprisoned and died as a political prisoner; the project itself was nearly destroyed in a 1946 cellar fire. The estate-managed argument is that the typological documentary portrait — frontal, formal, captioned by occupation rather than name — is one of twentieth-century photography's foundational innovations, and that the Bechers, Andreas Gursky, Thomas Struth, Candida Höfer, and the entire Düsseldorf School traces directly to it."
  },
  a89: { // Avery Singer
    message: "Singer was born in New York in 1987 to two artists (her father Jay Singer was an abstract painter), trained at Cooper Union, and lives in New York. Her paintings — made by airbrushing acrylic onto canvas through a 3D-modelled SketchUp computer rendering — depict figures, gallery interiors, and party scenes in a flat grey-scale register that looks both digital and like grisaille modelling exercises. The argument is that contemporary painting cannot pretend the digital rendering tools didn't happen — and that the painter who uses SketchUp and the airbrush as her actual materials is being honest about what painting now is."
  },
  a543: { // Axel Kasseböhmer — LOW-CONTEXT
    message: "Kasseböhmer was born in Herne, Germany in 1952 and died in 2018; he trained at the Kunstakademie Düsseldorf and lived in Munich for most of his career. His paintings worked in a quiet, reduced register sometimes associated with the parallel German tradition to Polke and Richter — small still lifes, landscapes, and abstract compositions. The estate argument is that the quieter regional painters of the post-1968 German generation deserve continued reassessment alongside the better-known figures."
  },
  a744: { // Aya Takano
    message: "Takano was born in Saitama, Japan in 1976, trained at Tama Art University, and is one of the central Kaikai Kiki Co. artists alongside Takashi Murakami who recruited her into the Superflat circle in the early 2000s. Her paintings, illustrations, and animation works depict slender wide-eyed female figures in dreamlike intergalactic or post-apocalyptic spaces, rendered in flat Japanese-illustration line and saturated colour. The argument, developed alongside Murakami's Superflat theory, is that the contemporary Japanese girl in manga-derived visual vocabulary is a legitimate painted subject — and that the supposed boundary between illustration and fine art is a Western critical convention not applicable to the post-1980s Japanese tradition."
  },
  a727: { // Banks Violette
    message: "Violette was born in Ithaca, NY in 1973, trained at the School of Visual Arts and at Columbia (MFA), and lives in upstate New York; he was a central figure of the early-2000s New York scene around the Whitney Biennial 2004. His salt-and-aluminium sculptures — including a full-scale burnt-out black church (Untitled, 2005) cast in salt — and his black-and-grey paintings drew on Norwegian black metal, the cultural iconography of suburban darkness, and minimalist sculpture's industrial materiality. The argument, sustained across a notably reclusive subsequent two decades, is that minimalism's industrial vocabulary was always available to subcultural use — and that the artist who refuses art-world participation while continuing to make work is doing the harder career work."
  },
  a768: { // Barry McGee
    message: "McGee was born in San Francisco in 1966 to a Chinese-American mother and an Irish-American father, trained at the San Francisco Art Institute, and lives in San Francisco; he started tagging the city under the name Twist in the 1980s and is a central figure of the Mission School graffiti generation. His installations — including the long Untitled wall-of-frames cluster compositions (dozens or hundreds of small framed drawings, photographs, and panels hung together), the painted geometric pattern walls, and the Animation figures — combine graffiti, comic illustration, found photographs, and the visual culture of San Francisco's Mission District. The argument is that the gallery installation organised as a graffitied wall-of-things is the medium most adequate to the artist whose actual practice is the wall — and that the gallery's grid-spacing convention is a value-judgment about which marks count."
  },
  a736: { // Bernar Venet
    message: "Venet was born in Château-Arnoux-Saint-Auban, France in 1941, lived in New York from 1966 to 1971 alongside Sol LeWitt and Carl Andre (with whom he showed in the foundational 1969 When Attitudes Become Form), and lives between New York and Le Muy in Provence (where he runs the Venet Foundation). His decisive 1960s conceptual works — large Tar paintings, blackboard mathematical diagrams — gave way to his best-known sustained series since 1979: large welded Cor-Ten steel arcs, lines, indeterminate lines, and angles that read as line-drawings in three-dimensional space. The argument is that the line, sculpture's most basic vocabulary unit, has been treated too long as a planar drawing problem — and that the welded steel arc at architectural scale is the line's proper contemporary form."
  },
  a453: { // Bernard Frize
    message: "Frize was born in Saint-Mandé, France in 1954, trained briefly at the École supérieure d'art de Marseille-Luminy, and lives in Paris and Berlin. His paintings since the late 1970s have worked through a single rule-based proposition: each painting is made by a specific protocol the painter follows mechanically (multiple brushes loaded with different colours, dragged across the canvas in a single sweep; resin-poured drips; assistants executing pre-specified colour mixes). The argument is that the painter's individual sensibility is the part of painting that can most usefully be subtracted — and that the rule-bound protocol, executed without taste-decisions, produces paintings that are more rather than less authored."
  },
  a643: { // Bernard Piffaretti
    message: "Piffaretti was born in Saint-Étienne, France in 1955 and lives in Paris. Since 1986 he has made every painting according to a single rule: a vertical line bisects the canvas; he paints a composition on the left side; then he paints the same composition again on the right side, attempting to reproduce his own gestures from memory but inevitably producing a slightly different version. The argument is that the painter's gesture is never repeatable — and that the visible failure of self-imitation, sustained as a single protocol for forty years, is itself the painting's subject."
  },
  a517: { // Bernd & Hilla Becher
    message: "Bernd Becher (Siegen, 1931 – Rostock, 2007) and Hilla Becher (née Wobeser, Potsdam, 1934 – Düsseldorf, 2015) married in 1961 and worked as a single artistic body until Bernd's death; they taught photography at the Düsseldorf Kunstakademie from 1976 onward. Their decades-long typological photography project — water towers, blast furnaces, gas tanks, framework houses, winding towers — was always organised as grids of nine or twelve identical compositions of the same industrial structure photographed in identical light and angle. The estate-managed argument is that the modernist industrial built environment was being demolished without record across post-war Western Europe — and that the systematic typological photograph was the only adequate response. Their teaching produced Gursky, Struth, Höfer, Ruff, the entire Düsseldorf School."
  },
  a100: { // Bharti Kher
    message: "Kher was born in London in 1969 to Indian parents, trained at Newcastle Polytechnic, moved to India in 1992, and lives in Gurgaon, near Delhi. Her sculptures and paintings — including the long Bindi paintings (large canvases covered in hundreds of thousands of bindi forehead-decorations applied in field-like patterns), the resin-cast Intermediaries, and the polyester-and-fibreglass life-size cast hybrid creatures (The Skin Speaks a Language Not Its Own, 2006, a full-scale fibreglass elephant collapsed on the floor) — engage the visual cultures of Indian femininity and the iconography of hybridity. The argument is that the bindi, that most over-determined Indian feminine sign, is also an abstract painted mark — and that the painting made of bindis is doing chromatic and political work the institution had not yet been trained to register together."
  },
  a701: { // Bjarne Melgaard
    message: "Melgaard was born in Sydney in 1967 to a Norwegian engineer father, was raised in Oslo, trained briefly at the Statens Kunstakademi and Jan van Eyck Academie, and lives in Tribeca, New York. His paintings, sculptures, installations, and fashion-collaborations — including the deliberately offensive Beware of the Holy Whore series, the Allen Jones-derivative furniture, and the long social-media-and-tabloid press performance — work in a register designed to provoke serious institutional discomfort. The argument is that the contemporary artist's most under-credited tool is the deliberate trans-art-world provocation — and that the artist whose tabloid-press profile is part of the work is doing harder career work than the artist whose institutional profile is curated."
  },
  a472: { // Boris Mikhailov
    message: "Mikhailov was born in Kharkiv, Ukraine in 1938 to a Jewish family, trained as an engineer in Soviet engineering institutes, lost his engineering job in the 1960s when the KGB found nude photographs of his wife on his work computer, and has lived between Kharkiv and Berlin since 2001. His major project Case History (1997–98) is a 400-photograph documentation of the homeless, post-Soviet Kharkiv population in the immediate years after the USSR's collapse, including paid-for nude portraits of severely impoverished subjects — work that has remained controversial since its publication. The argument is that the post-Soviet documentary photograph cannot be made under the assumptions of Western humanist tradition — and that the photographer who pays his subjects, and admits paying them, is more honest about the conditions of contemporary documentary than the photographer who pretends to a neutral observer position."
  },
  a365: { // Bram Bogart
    message: "Bogart was born Abraham van den Boogaart in Delft, Netherlands in 1921 and died in Saint-Truiden, Belgium in 2012; he trained briefly as a house painter before turning to art and lived in Paris, Rome, and Brussels across his career. His paintings, especially the late large impasto works, deposit pigment-and-binder mixtures up to several inches thick onto board, in compositions that read as both sculpture and painting and that have a literal physical weight unusual in mid-twentieth-century European abstraction. The estate-managed argument is that European post-war abstraction had a more material strand than the canonical narrative around Tachism and CoBrA registered — and that the painting heavy enough to bend the gallery wall is making a claim about painting's physical condition that the canonical history of style was not equipped to receive."
  },
  a343: { // Brent Wadden
    message: "Wadden was born in Glace Bay, Nova Scotia in 1979, trained at the Nova Scotia College of Art and Design and at the Universität der Künste Berlin under Daniel Richter, and lives between Berlin, Vancouver, and Cape Breton. His large compositions are hand-woven on traditional Maritime-Canadian floor looms in wool, hand-spun cotton, and acrylic yarn, then stretched onto canvas-style supports and treated as paintings; the weaving's inevitable irregularities (the loom's tension, the artist's body's variation) become the composition. The argument is that hand-weaving on a floor loom is a legitimate contemporary painting medium — and that the irregularity that the loom produces is the body's evidence that a person made the painting."
  },
  a59: { // Brice Marden
    message: "Marden was born in Bronxville, NY in 1938, trained at Boston University and Yale, worked as Robert Rauschenberg's studio assistant in the late 1960s, and lived in New York and Tivoli, NY (along with extended periods on Hydra, Greece) until his death in 2023. His decisive 1970s wax-on-canvas monochrome panels — single colour fields whose surface holds the brush's slow horizontal application — gave way after a 1984 trip to Asia to the long Cold Mountain series (1989–91, calligraphic line paintings after the Chinese Tang poet Han Shan) and the later layered glyph paintings. The argument, sustained over fifty years, is that the monochrome panel and the calligraphic line are the same painting at different speeds — and that the slow surface, taken seriously, is what minimalism's institutional reception missed about the medium it had named."
  },
  a198: { // Bridget Riley
    message: "Riley was born in Norwood, London in 1931, was raised partly in wartime Cornwall, trained at Goldsmiths and at the Royal College of Art, and lives in London. Her early black-and-white Op paintings — Movement in Squares (1961), Current (1964) — used flat planes of repeated black-and-white forms to produce intense optical movement that the canvas itself did not contain; her colour Op work from 1967 onward extended the proposition into vertical-stripe and curve compositions in narrow palettes. The argument, sustained over six decades, is that the painting can produce a perceptual event that genuinely exceeds the painted surface — and that the dismissal of Op art as merely decorative in the 1960s was a category mistake the institution has been slowly correcting since."
  },
  a403: { // Bruce Nauman
    message: "Nauman was born in Fort Wayne, Indiana in 1941, trained at the University of Wisconsin and at UC Davis (under William T. Wiley, alongside Stephen Kaltenbach), and lives in Galisteo, New Mexico. His decisive 1966–70 studio films and videos (Bouncing in the Corner No. 1, Walking in an Exaggerated Manner Around the Perimeter of a Square, Stamping in the Studio) treated the studio as a laboratory for the artist's body alone; his neon works (Run from Fear, Fun from Rear; The True Artist Helps the World by Revealing Mystic Truths) and the long sound-and-text-and-video installations across the 1970s through the present (Days, 2009; ContraPposto Studies, 2015–16) extend the same proposition. The argument is that contemporary sculpture's actual medium is the artist's body in the studio, the recorded mark of that body, and the words that body says — and that everything else is curatorial mediation."
  },
  a101: { // Camille Henrot
    message: "Henrot was born in Paris in 1978, trained at the École nationale supérieure des Arts Décoratifs and at Le Fresnoy, lives between Paris and New York. Her decisive Grosse Fatigue (2013, Silver Lion at the Venice Biennale, a 13-minute video that scrolls through digital photographs of the Smithsonian's collections set to spoken-word creation myths) and the related Days Are Dogs / Carte Blanche (2017 Palais de Tokyo) work across video, sculpture, drawing, and writing on themes of taxonomy, mythology, and the calendar. The argument is that the contemporary subject's relation to information is mediated through the act of scrolling — and that the gallery installation can render that scrolling experience at the scale of an inhabitable architecture."
  },
  a521: { // Cao Fei
    message: "Cao Fei was born in Guangzhou in 1978 to two artist parents (her father is a sculptor of state-commissioned monuments), trained at the Guangzhou Academy of Fine Arts, and lives in Beijing. Her video work — including the RMB City Second Life project (2007–11, a virtual Chinese city built in the Second Life metaverse), Whose Utopia (2006, the Osram factory documentary), and the long Asia One feature on a Beijing logistics warehouse — moves between observational documentary, science-fiction worldbuilding, and pure participatory game-engine. The argument is that the post-1990s Chinese city is itself a fictional construction at metropolitan scale — and that the artist whose medium is both the factory floor and the Second Life virtual world is being honest about the conditions of contemporary Chinese labour and consumption."
  },
  a847: { // Carl Andre
    message: "Andre was born in Quincy, Massachusetts in 1935 to a Swedish-American shipyard family, briefly attended Phillips Academy Andover (alongside Hollis Frampton and Frank Stella), worked as a freight brakeman on the Pennsylvania Railroad in the early 1960s, and lived in New York until his death in 2024; he was the husband of the Cuban artist Ana Mendieta from January 1985 until her death from a 34th-floor fall later that year (he was tried for and acquitted of her murder in a 1988 bench trial whose verdict the art world has never fully accepted). His major sculptures — the long line, square, and grid compositions of identical industrial-fabricated units (firebrick, cedar timber, zinc plates, magnesium tiles) laid directly on the gallery floor (Equivalent VIII, 1966; Lever, 1966; 144 Magnesium Square, 1969) — were foundational for American minimalism. The estate-managed argument is that the canonical narrative of American minimalism has had to handle the prosecutorial and reception history of his marriage to Mendieta for forty years — and that no contemporary engagement with the work is possible without that question still in the room."
  },
  a796: { // Carlos Garaicoa
    message: "Garaicoa was born in Havana in 1967 and lives between Havana and Madrid; he trained at the Instituto Superior de Arte. His installations, photographs, and architectural sculptures — including the long Continuity of Somebody's Architecture series (architectural-drawing-meets-photography of decayed Havana modernist buildings), the Now Let's Play to Disappear (mirror-floor walking pieces), and the ongoing Las Joyas de la Corona (architectural-models of secret police facilities of various regimes) — engage the Cuban built environment as the document of revolutionary decay. The argument is that the Cuban city is itself a half-erased political document — and that the artist whose medium is the architectural drawing rendered over the ruined building is reading that document in real time."
  },
  a108: { // Carolee Schneemann
    message: "Schneemann was born in Fox Chase, Pennsylvania in 1939 to a country doctor's family and died in 2019; she trained at Bard College and the University of Illinois, and lived in upstate New York and Manhattan. Her decisive early works — Meat Joy (1964, a half-naked group performance with raw chicken, fish, and sausages), Fuses (1964–68, an unedited 18-minute Bolex film of herself and her partner James Tenney having sex), Interior Scroll (1975, in which she stood naked on a table and slowly extracted a long paper scroll from her vagina while reading the feminist text inscribed on it) — established the foundational vocabulary of feminist body art. The estate-managed argument is that the canonical narrative of 1960s American performance and the New York avant-garde has under-credited the feminist body-art lineage — and that the corrective is not symbolic inclusion but the close re-reading of work that the period itself refused to take seriously."
  },
  a612: { // Ceal Floyer
    message: "Floyer was born in Karachi in 1968, trained at Goldsmiths in the early 1990s (where she was a contemporary of Damien Hirst and Sarah Lucas), and lives in Berlin. Her works are typically small conceptual gestures pointed at the gallery itself: Light Switch (1992, a projector throwing a life-size image of a light switch onto a gallery wall); Garbage Bag (1996, a single black plastic bin liner inflated with air); Door (1995, a closed door whose underside leaks a strip of light); 'Til I Get It Right (2010, an audio piece looping Tammy Wynette's words 'till I get it right' indefinitely). The argument is that the gallery's smallest available conventions — the light switch, the rubbish bag, the closed door, the song lyric — are sufficient sculptural material if the artist is willing to be quiet enough to attend to them."
  },
  a619: { // Channa Horwitz
    message: "Horwitz was born in Los Angeles in 1932 and died there in 2013; she trained at Otis Art Institute and CalArts in her thirties, raised a family, and worked in near-institutional obscurity for fifty years before her late-career international re-discovery. Her practice was a single sustained system — Sonakinatography (sound-and-movement notation, 1968 onward) — in which she developed an eight-colour eight-tone visual notation system rendered on graph paper, and used it to score sound, dance, and movement compositions. The estate-managed argument is that systematic visual notation by a woman working alone in Los Angeles across fifty years is a serious contribution to American conceptual practice — and that the canonical narrative around Sol LeWitt's instructional drawing was systematically incomplete."
  },
  a427: { // Chantal Akerman
    message: "Akerman was born in Brussels in 1950 to a Polish-Jewish mother who survived Auschwitz, made her first film at 17, and killed herself in Paris in 2015 a year after her mother's death. Her decisive Jeanne Dielman, 23, quai du Commerce, 1080 Bruxelles (1975, the 201-minute observational film of three days in the domestic life of a single-mother widow that was voted the greatest film of all time in the Sight and Sound 2022 poll) and her gallery video installations — including Bordering on Fiction: Chantal Akerman's D'Est (1995, an installation of her Eastern European post-Wall footage), No Home Movie (2015, her last work, on her mother) — move between feature film and gallery video. The estate-managed argument is that the long observational shot, sustained at duration most audiences cannot easily bear, is the medium most adequate to the post-war Jewish daughter's relation to her mother — and that the gallery installation context lets the work find the slower viewer it required."
  },
  a85: { // Charles Gaines
    message: "Gaines was born in Charleston, South Carolina in 1944, was raised in Newark, New Jersey, trained at Jersey City State College and at the Rochester Institute of Technology, and lives in Los Angeles where he has taught at CalArts since 1989 (his students have included Mark Bradford, Henry Taylor, Edgar Arceneaux, and Andrea Bowers, among many others). His decisive work, sustained since 1974, is the long Numbers and Trees series — in which he photographs a tree, then plots each leaf as a numbered grid square in colour-coded ink, building a single composition that is simultaneously photograph, grid-painting, and conceptual scheme. The argument, sustained over fifty years, is that the rule-based system in art-making is not the cool minimalist-conceptual proposition that the canonical 1970s narrative made it — and that the Black conceptual artist working through systems has a long, under-credited tradition that runs parallel to the better-known one."
  },
  a789: { // Chen Zhen
    message: "Chen Zhen was born in Shanghai in 1955 to a family of doctors (he was diagnosed with hemolytic anaemia at 25 and lived with the illness until his death in Paris in 2000), trained at the Shanghai Drama Institute, moved to Paris in 1986, and was a central figure of the Chinese-French artist diaspora until his early death at forty-five. His installations — including Field of Synergies (1990s), Daily Incantations (1996, a wall of Chinese chamber-pots and synthetic Buddhist mantra), Bibliothèque musicale (1999, a forest of swing-hung musical instruments in glass) — work at the seam of Chinese medicine, Buddhist cosmology, and globalised consumer culture. The estate-managed argument is that the Chinese-French diasporic artist of the 1980s and 1990s had a distinct contribution that the canonical narrative of post-1990s globalised contemporary art has incompletely registered — and that the medical body, sustained through illness, was inseparable from the work's vocabulary."
  },
  a651: { // Cheyney Thompson
    message: "Thompson was born in Baton Rouge, Louisiana in 1975, trained at Bard MFA, and lives in New York. His paintings work through algorithmic colour-mapping systems — the long Chromachrome series and the related Stochastic Process paintings — in which each painting's colour-and-mark composition is generated by a defined mathematical operation applied to canvas-colour conversion tables and noise functions. The argument is that the painter who automates the colour-decisions is being more honest about the painting's actual production conditions in the algorithmic era — and that the visible failure of pure system (the residue of the painter's hand at moments where the algorithm did not specify) is the painting's properly contemporary subject."
  },
  a745: { // Chiho Aoshima
    message: "Aoshima was born in Tokyo in 1974 and trained in economics rather than art at Hosei University before joining Takashi Murakami's Hiropon Factory (later Kaikai Kiki Co.) in the late 1990s; she lives in Tokyo. Her digitally produced large mural prints, animation, and ceramics depict floating-eyed wide-faced young women, ghosts, and surreal nature scenes in a flat saturated palette that draws on manga, ukiyo-e ghost imagery, and contemporary Japanese girls' visual culture. The argument, made within Murakami's Superflat framework, is that the female-Japanese-illustration register has its own substantive lineage — and that the artist working in Photoshop and Illustrator on the scale of a subway-station tile wall is making something the canonical narrative of contemporary painting has not yet developed the vocabulary to receive."
  },
  a23: { // Chris Burden
    message: "Burden was born in Boston in 1946, trained at Pomona College and at UC Irvine (his 1971 MFA thesis was Five Day Locker Piece, in which he locked himself in a 2-by-2-by-3-foot university locker for five days with no food), and lived in Topanga Canyon, California until his death in 2015. His decisive early body works — Shoot (1971, in which a friend shot him in the upper arm with a .22 rifle inside a Santa Ana gallery), Trans-fixed (1974, nailed to the back of a Volkswagen Beetle), Doomed (1975, lying motionless under a leaning glass sheet for forty-five hours until a curator broke the implied piece) — established the foundational vocabulary of the artist-body-as-medium under genuine physical risk. His later monumental sculpture (Urban Light, 2008, the LACMA entrance street-lamp forest; Metropolis II, 2010) extends the same proposition into civic scale. The argument is that the artist's actual primary material is the willingness to be physically at risk inside the institutional frame — and that the institution learns where it actually stands by where it draws the line."
  },
  a189: { // Chris Ofili
    message: "Ofili was born in Manchester, England in 1968 to Nigerian parents, trained at Chelsea College of Art and at the Royal College of Art, won the Turner Prize in 1998 (the first Black artist to do so), and has lived in Trinidad since 2005. His decisive 1990s paintings — The Holy Virgin Mary (1996, the work New York Mayor Rudy Giuliani tried to censor from the 1999 Brooklyn Museum Sensation exhibition), No Woman No Cry (1998, a tribute to the parents of murdered Black teenager Stephen Lawrence) — combined oil paint, glitter, magazine collage of pornographic and Black-culture sources, and elephant dung that the painter collected from London Zoo and varnished into balls used as the canvas's literal supports. The argument is that British painting's most central available subject in the late 1990s was the Black British condition — and that the materially radical surface (elephant dung as load-bearing base) was the work's serious proposition, not its tabloid hook."
  },
  a624: { // Christian Jankowski
    message: "Jankowski was born in Göttingen, Germany in 1968, trained at the Hochschule für bildende Künste Hamburg, and lives in Berlin. His video and performance works — including Telemistica (1999, in which he phoned Italian TV-fortune-tellers from inside a Venice Biennale exhibition asking whether his work would succeed), Hochzeit der Geliebten (Wedding of the Bride, 2008), and The Hunt (1992, in which the young artist 'hunted' supermarket food with a bow-and-arrow) — typically stage a deliberately absurd encounter between art-world conventions and other professional or commercial systems (television, sports, retail, fortune-telling). The argument is that contemporary art's value is co-produced with adjacent commercial and entertainment systems — and that the artist who explicitly co-produces with them, on the record, is being more honest about the work's actual conditions of meaning than the artist who pretends gallery autonomy."
  },
  a90: { // Christina Quarles
    message: "Quarles was born in Chicago in 1985 to a Black father and a white mother (she identifies as Black, queer, and presenting-as-white, an inheritance her work treats as a primary subject), trained at Hampshire College and at Yale MFA, and lives in Los Angeles. Her large figurative paintings — depicting multi-limbed entangled bodies whose limbs cross into and through one another, set against flat patterned ground — work through a deliberately ambiguous figural register in which the bodies' race, gender, and number cannot be cleanly resolved. The argument is that the contemporary mixed-race queer body cannot be cleanly counted, classified, or even bounded — and that the painting must register that condition formally rather than illustrate it narratively."
  },
  a362: { // Christine Ay Tjoe
    message: "Ay Tjoe was born in Bandung, Indonesia in 1973 to a Chinese-Indonesian family, trained at the Bandung Institute of Technology in printmaking, and lives in Bandung. Her paintings — including the long Layers of the In-Between, Surrounded by Curtains, and Small Flies and Other Wings series — work in a thin, scribbled, semi-erased gestural register that recalls Cy Twombly but is rooted in the personal-spiritual register of Indonesian contemporary painting. The argument is that contemporary Southeast Asian painting need not perform regional identity for the Western institution — and that the painter working at the seam of drawing and painting, in deliberate quietness, can find a register that the louder international contemporary scene cannot easily reach."
  },
  a629: { // Christopher Le Brun
    message: "Le Brun was born in Portsmouth, England in 1951, trained at the Slade School of Fine Art and at the Chelsea College of Art, served as President of the Royal Academy from 2011 to 2019, and lives in London. His paintings — large abstract compositions in saturated colour that emerged from earlier figurative-derived images of horses, towers, and forests — work in a Romantic-painterly register that draws on Constable, the European Symbolist tradition, and post-war American abstraction. The argument, partly institutional, is that British painting's tradition can sustain the Romantic mode into the contemporary moment without irony — and that the painter who admits the inheritance, including the inherited subject matter of forests and figures, is making more difficult painting than the painter who refuses it."
  },
  a232: { // Chuck Close
    message: "Close was born in Monroe, Washington in 1940 with severe dyslexia and prosopagnosia (face-blindness — he could not recognise even close family members visually, the condition that drove his portrait practice), trained at the University of Washington and at Yale, and lived in New York until his death in 2021. His decisive 1967–70 large-format black-and-white photo-realist portraits (Big Self-Portrait, 1967–68; the long series of friends including Philip Glass and Joe Zucker) were made by enlarging a photograph onto canvas via a precisely ruled grid, then airbrushing or hand-painting each grid square at extreme scale. After the 1988 spinal-artery collapse that paralysed him below the chest, he developed a wheel-chair-mounted painting method using assistants and continued the grid-portrait practice until his death. The argument is that the painter's task can be the systematic, gridded reconstruction of a face that the painter literally cannot perceive — and that the disability is not a limitation around which the practice was built but the proposition the practice substantively explored."
  },
  a750: { // Chung Chang-Sup
    message: "Chung Chang-Sup was born in Cheongju, North Chungcheong Province, Korea in 1927 and died in 2011; he trained at Seoul National University and is one of the founding figures of Dansaekhwa, the Korean monochrome painting movement of the 1970s. His long Tak series (1970s onward) was made by laying handmade Korean dak (mulberry) paper, while still wet, directly onto raw cotton canvas, where it would adhere as it dried; the resulting paintings preserve the literal absorption of paper into canvas as the composition. The estate-managed argument is that the Korean monochrome's most under-credited material proposition was the use of dak paper as both ground and surface — a substantive break from the European-American monochrome tradition that did not exist in the Korean material register."
  },
  a816: { // Cildo Meireles
    message: "Meireles was born in Rio de Janeiro in 1948 (his father worked for the Indian Protection Service in the Amazon, an experience that shaped Cildo's later relationship to Brazilian indigenous-state politics), trained at the Escolinha de Arte in Rio, and lives in Rio. His decisive Insertions into Ideological Circuits (1970 onward) — Coca-Cola bottles overstamped with political messages (Yankees Go Home) and returned to circulation; banknotes overprinted with the question 'Who killed Herzog?' (referring to a journalist murdered by the Brazilian military regime) — used the existing consumer-and-monetary distribution networks as the artwork's medium. His monumental installations — Mission/Missions (How to Build Cathedrals) (1987, 600,000 coins on the floor under 2,000 cattle bones suspended from the ceiling, connected by a column of communion wafers), Babel (2001, a tower of 800 radios each tuned to a different station) — argue at civic scale. The argument, sustained for over fifty years, is that the consumer-monetary-information circuits of contemporary capitalism are themselves the artist's available sculptural medium — and that the Brazilian artist working under dictatorship had to invent that proposition because the gallery alone was insufficient to host it."
  },
  a415: { // Cinga Samson
    message: "Samson was born in Ethwecu, in the Eastern Cape of South Africa, in 1986, was largely self-taught after coming to Cape Town as a young man, and lives in Cape Town. His paintings — figurative portraits of young Xhosa men, painted with the sitter's eyes rendered as featureless white rectangles (the eyes deliberately blinded to the viewer's recognition) — work in a deeply saturated palette and a slow figurative-painting register. The argument is that the South African Black portraitist's first responsibility is to refuse the viewer's expected access to the sitter's interiority — and that the blinded eye, sustained as a single formal device, returns the portrait to a register the Western tradition of psychological portraiture had to abandon."
  },
  a598: { // Cory Arcangel
    message: "Arcangel was born in Buffalo, NY in 1978, trained as a classical guitarist at the Oberlin Conservatory, and lives in Stavanger, Norway. His decisive 2002 Super Mario Clouds — a hacked Nintendo cartridge that displays only the scrolling cloud background of Super Mario Bros., with all other game elements removed — and his subsequent works (Various Self Playing Bowling Games, 2011; the long Photoshop Gradient series; Super Slow Tetris) work in a register that takes obsolete consumer technology, popular software defaults, and entertainment systems as the artist's available sculptural medium. The argument is that contemporary art's most under-credited inheritance is the consumer-software-and-gaming visual culture of the 1990s and 2000s — and that the artist who works with hacked Nintendos and Photoshop gradients is recovering an aesthetic tradition the gallery had not yet learned how to recognise."
  },
  a544: { // Craig Kauffman
    message: "Kauffman was born in Los Angeles in 1932 and died in Mexico in 2010; he trained at USC and at UCLA, and was one of the founding figures of the Los Angeles Finish Fetish / Light and Space generation in the 1960s. His vacuum-formed plastic wall reliefs — large biomorphic translucent shapes in candy-coloured acrylic, often called Bubbles or Loops — were made by heating sheets of plastic over plywood moulds and spraying them with automobile lacquer, in a working method directly borrowed from the Los Angeles automotive customisation industry. The estate-managed argument is that Los Angeles post-war art's foundational material innovation was the borrowing of Detroit automotive technology — and that the candy-finish plastic wall sculpture is, technologically and aesthetically, a specifically Californian contribution that the East-Coast-dominated post-war canon under-credited for decades."
  },
  a640: { // Dalton Paula
    message: "Paula was born in Brasília in 1982 and lives in Goiânia, Brazil. His paintings work as a sustained portraiture project — reconstructions of historical Black Brazilian figures (quilombola leaders, religious figures, abolitionists, market women) whose images either were never made or did not survive — painted from oral history, archival fragments, and ethnographic sources, in a slow, intimate figurative register. The argument is that the Brazilian art-historical record has erased the Black figures whose existence the country's history required — and that the contemporary Brazilian painter's most consequential task is to produce the portraits the colonial-and-post-colonial archive refused to keep."
  },
  a49: { // Dan Colen
    message: "Colen was born in Leonia, NJ in 1979, trained at the Rhode Island School of Design, and lives in upstate New York where he runs the Sky High Farm non-profit (an agricultural community-food project on his property). His paintings — the long Chewing Gum paintings (made by friends chewing gum into colour-coordinated wads pressed onto canvas), the Trash paintings, the Birdshit paintings, the Boulder works — collaborate with deliberately abject and accidental materials in compositions that read as both painting and assemblage. The argument is that the post-2000 American painter cannot pretend authorial control over the studio — and that the painting that admits the friends, the chewing gum, the bird shit, the random accident as collaborators is more honest about how the studio actually works than the painting that pretends to lone-genius execution."
  },

};

// Expose for the quiz UI
if (typeof window !== 'undefined') window.QUIZ_MESSAGES = QUIZ_MESSAGES;
